window.__programmeIndexChunk("passages",{"d790582f6f1e0bd5afcf3a1a2d90fc05c8eb31cb4d444a1db77a8de1f596ff6c":{"markdown":"## 3. Comparison of the two groups\n\n<a id=\"oa-flow.l157.group-comparison\"><\/a>\n\nAssume condition 2 and put \\(D=D(H)=D(K)\\). Fix a real \\(t\\) and \\(v\\) in \\(D\\). Consider, as a function of real \\(s\\) between 0 and \\(t\\),\n\n\\[\nF_t(s)v=e^{i(t-s)K}e^{isH}v.\n\\]\n\nThe right factor preserves \\(D(H)=D(K)\\). Splitting the actual increment quotient into its two factors and using the spectral derivative on that fixed domain gives\n\n<a id=\"oa-flow.eq.lp6\"><\/a>\n\n\\[\n\\frac{d}{ds}F_t(s)v\n=-i e^{i(t-s)K}(K-H)e^{isH}v\n=-i e^{i(t-s)K}a e^{isH}v.\n\\tag{LP6}\n\\]\n\nFor the split quotient, a factor multiplying a convergent vector is uniformly bounded by one; the estimate \\(\\|U_hx_h-Ux\\|\\leq\\|x_h-x\\|+\\|(U_h-U)x\\|\\) justifies its limit. In particular no assertion that \\(a\\) preserves \\(D\\) is needed. The final derivative is norm-continuous because \\(a\\) is bounded.\n\nThe vector fundamental theorem in SF-3 yields\n\n<a id=\"oa-flow.eq.lp7\"><\/a>\n\n\\[\n(e^{itK}-e^{itH})v\n=i\\int_0^t e^{i(t-s)K}a e^{isH}v\\,ds.\n\\tag{LP7}\n\\]\n\nThe integrand has norm at most \\(\\|a\\|\\) \\(\\|v\\|\\). Density of \\(D\\) and that uniform bound extend (LP7) to every vector. Apply it to \\(e^{-itH}v\\) and substitute \\(r=t-s\\) in the oriented integral. The result identifies the prescribed product itself:\n\n<a id=\"oa-flow.eq.lp8\"><\/a>\n\n\\[\nu_t-1=i\\int_0^t B_r\\,dr,\n\\qquad B_r=e^{irK}a e^{-irH},\\quad\\|B_r\\|=\\|a\\|.\n\\tag{LP8}\n\\]\n\nThis is a strong vector integral. The bounded operators defined by its vector integrals lie in \\(M\\): their uniformly bounded Riemann sums lie in \\(M\\) and converge strongly. It is not an assertion of operator-norm continuity or operator-norm integrability of \\(r\\mapsto B_r\\).\n\nBoth \\(B_r\\) and \\(B_r^*\\) are strongly continuous. Pairing their vector integrals against two vectors shows that the adjoint of the integral of \\(B_r\\) is the integral of \\(B_r^*\\). Hence\n\n<a id=\"oa-flow.eq.lp9\"><\/a>\n\n\\[\nu_t^*-1=-i\\int_0^t B_r^*\\,dr,\n\\qquad B_r^*=e^{irH}a e^{-irK}.\n\\tag{LP9}\n\\]\n\n<a id=\"oa-flow.l157.adjoint-integral\"><\/a>\n\nThese identities establish the adjoint formula for the actual product, with all real signs fixed by the oriented integrals.\n\n<a id=\"oa-flow.l157.intrinsic-derivative\"><\/a>\n\n<a id=\"OA-FLOW.XII5.LOG.NORMAL\"><\/a>\n<a id=\"oa-flow.xii5.log.normal\"><\/a>\n<a id=\"OA-FLOW.XII5.LOG.ALLTIME\"><\/a>\n<a id=\"oa-flow.xii5.log.alltime\"><\/a>","html":"<h2>3. Comparison of the two groups<\/h2>\n<p>Assume condition 2 and put <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(D=D(H)=D(K)\\)\">\\(D=D(H)=D(K)\\)<\/span>. Fix a real <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(t\\)\">\\(t\\)<\/span> and <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(v\\)\">\\(v\\)<\/span> in <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(D\\)\">\\(D\\)<\/span>. Consider, as a function of real <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(s\\)\">\\(s\\)<\/span> between 0 and <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(t\\)\">\\(t\\)<\/span>,<\/p>\n<p><span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\nF_t(s)v=e^{i(t-s)K}e^{isH}v.\n\\]\">\\[\nF_t(s)v=e^{i(t-s)K}e^{isH}v.\n\\]<\/span><\/p>\n<p>The right factor preserves <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(D(H)=D(K)\\)\">\\(D(H)=D(K)\\)<\/span>. Splitting the actual increment quotient into its two factors and using the spectral derivative on that fixed domain gives<\/p>\n<p><span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n\\frac{d}{ds}F_t(s)v\n=-i e^{i(t-s)K}(K-H)e^{isH}v\n=-i e^{i(t-s)K}a e^{isH}v.\n\\tag{LP6}\n\\]\">\\[\n\\frac{d}{ds}F_t(s)v\n=-i e^{i(t-s)K}(K-H)e^{isH}v\n=-i e^{i(t-s)K}a e^{isH}v.\n\\tag{LP6}\n\\]<\/span><\/p>\n<p>For the split quotient, a factor multiplying a convergent vector is uniformly bounded by one; the estimate <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\|U_hx_h-Ux\\|\\leq\\|x_h-x\\|+\\|(U_h-U)x\\|\\)\">\\(\\|U_hx_h-Ux\\|\\leq\\|x_h-x\\|+\\|(U_h-U)x\\|\\)<\/span> justifies its limit. In particular no assertion that <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(a\\)\">\\(a\\)<\/span> preserves <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(D\\)\">\\(D\\)<\/span> is needed. The final derivative is norm-continuous because <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(a\\)\">\\(a\\)<\/span> is bounded.<\/p>\n<p>The vector fundamental theorem in SF-3 yields<\/p>\n<p><span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n(e^{itK}-e^{itH})v\n=i\\int_0^t e^{i(t-s)K}a e^{isH}v\\,ds.\n\\tag{LP7}\n\\]\">\\[\n(e^{itK}-e^{itH})v\n=i\\int_0^t e^{i(t-s)K}a e^{isH}v\\,ds.\n\\tag{LP7}\n\\]<\/span><\/p>\n<p>The integrand has norm at most <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\|a\\|\\)\">\\(\\|a\\|\\)<\/span> <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\|v\\|\\)\">\\(\\|v\\|\\)<\/span>. Density of <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(D\\)\">\\(D\\)<\/span> and that uniform bound extend (LP7) to every vector. Apply it to <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(e^{-itH}v\\)\">\\(e^{-itH}v\\)<\/span> and substitute <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(r=t-s\\)\">\\(r=t-s\\)<\/span> in the oriented integral. The result identifies the prescribed product itself:<\/p>\n<p><span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\nu_t-1=i\\int_0^t B_r\\,dr,\n\\qquad B_r=e^{irK}a e^{-irH},\\quad\\|B_r\\|=\\|a\\|.\n\\tag{LP8}\n\\]\">\\[\nu_t-1=i\\int_0^t B_r\\,dr,\n\\qquad B_r=e^{irK}a e^{-irH},\\quad\\|B_r\\|=\\|a\\|.\n\\tag{LP8}\n\\]<\/span><\/p>\n<p>This is a strong vector integral. The bounded operators defined by its vector integrals lie in <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(M\\)\">\\(M\\)<\/span>: their uniformly bounded Riemann sums lie in <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(M\\)\">\\(M\\)<\/span> and converge strongly. It is not an assertion of operator-norm continuity or operator-norm integrability of <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(r\\mapsto B_r\\)\">\\(r\\mapsto B_r\\)<\/span>.<\/p>\n<p>Both <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(B_r\\)\">\\(B_r\\)<\/span> and <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(B_r^*\\)\">\\(B_r^*\\)<\/span> are strongly continuous. Pairing their vector integrals against two vectors shows that the adjoint of the integral of <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(B_r\\)\">\\(B_r\\)<\/span> is the integral of <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(B_r^*\\)\">\\(B_r^*\\)<\/span>. Hence<\/p>\n<p><span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\nu_t^*-1=-i\\int_0^t B_r^*\\,dr,\n\\qquad B_r^*=e^{irH}a e^{-irK}.\n\\tag{LP9}\n\\]\">\\[\nu_t^*-1=-i\\int_0^t B_r^*\\,dr,\n\\qquad B_r^*=e^{irH}a e^{-irK}.\n\\tag{LP9}\n\\]<\/span><\/p>\n<p>These identities establish the adjoint formula for the actual product, with all real signs fixed by the oriented integrals.<\/p>\n","source":"docs/courses/OA-FLOW/src/OA-FLOW-L157.md","start_offset":5205,"end_offset":7519,"role":"registered_statement_and_proof_passage"},"d719cb87c2987f47947baf132f619f9598d9eb560825e094ee993511a649b7fb":{"markdown":"## PW-5. Weight preservation including infinity, and the restricted trace\n\nFor \\(a\\geq0\\), each positive Riemann average has \\(\\rho(E_n(a))=\\rho(a)\\), by whole-cone modular invariance and additivity. If the value is finite, [EW3](OA-FLOW-EW.md#oa-flow.ew.3)'s ultraweak lower semicontinuity gives \\(\\rho(E(a))\\leq\\rho(a)\\); if it is infinite this inequality is automatic.\n\nFor the reverse inequality use precisely [WS3](OA-FLOW-WS.md#oa-flow.weight-sum.ws3), which proves that\n\n<a id=\"equation-pw25\"><\/a>\n\n\\[\n \\mathcal D_\\rho=\\{f\\in M_*^+:f\\leq r\\rho\n                    \\text{ for some }0<r<1\\}\n \\text{ is directed},\\qquad\n \\sup_{f\\in\\mathcal D_\\rho}f(z)=\\rho(z)\\quad(z\\geq0).\n \\tag{PW25}\n\\]\nFix a real number \\(L<\\rho(a)\\). When \\(\\rho(a)=0\\) the desired inequality is already immediate; otherwise take \\(0\\leq L<\\rho(a)\\), with \\(L\\) arbitrarily large when the value is infinite. The continuous functions\n\\(t\\mapsto f(\\alpha_t(a))\\), \\(f\\in\\mathcal D_\\rho\\), increase as a directed family and have the constant supremum \\(\\rho(a)\\) at every \\(t\\). The open sets where they exceed \\(L\\) cover \\(K_P\\). A finite subcover and directedness give one \\(f\\) for which \\(f(\\alpha_t(a))>L\\) everywhere. Since \\(f\\leq\\rho\\), ([PW22](OA-FLOW-PW.md#equation-pw22)) implies\n\\[\n \\rho(E(a))\\geq f(E(a))\n   =\\int_{K_P}f(\\alpha_t(a))\\,dm_{K_P}(t)\\geq L .\n\\]\nLetting \\(L\\uparrow\\rho(a)\\), or letting \\(L\\) tend to infinity, proves\n\n<a id=\"equation-pw26\"><\/a>\n\n\\[\n \\rho(E(a))=\\rho(a)\\qquad(a\\in M_+).                          \\tag{PW26}\n\\]\nThis is a full-cone proof. It never interchanges an unbounded weight with an integral, and uses neither a countable decomposition of \\(\\rho\\) nor a countability assumption on \\(M\\).\n\nPut \\(\\tau=\\rho|_{D_+}\\). This restriction is faithful and normal, since increasing suprema in the fixed von Neumann algebra agree with those in \\(M\\). By [GW4](OA-FLOW-GW.md#oa-flow.gw.4) there are finite positive contractions \\(u_i\\uparrow1\\) in \\(M\\). Equations ([PW24](OA-FLOW-PW.md#equation-pw24)) and ([PW26](OA-FLOW-PW.md#equation-pw26)) give finite positive contractions \\(E(u_i)\\uparrow1\\) in \\(D\\). [GW4](OA-FLOW-GW.md#oa-flow.gw.4), now inside \\(D\\), proves semifiniteness of \\(\\tau\\).\n\nThe finite domains have the exact descriptions\n\n<a id=\"equation-pw27\"><\/a>\n\n\\[\n F_\\tau=F_\\rho\\cap D,\\quad N_\\tau=N_\\rho\\cap D,\\quad\n A_\\tau=A_\\rho\\cap D,\\quad m_\\tau=m_\\rho\\cap D.                \\tag{PW27}\n\\]\nThe first three follow from restriction. To prove the last, \\(m_\\tau\\subseteq m_\\rho\\cap D\\) is immediate. Conversely [GW1](OA-FLOW-GW.md#oa-flow.gw.1) expresses any \\(z\\in m_\\rho\\) as a finite complex linear combination of positive finite-weight elements. If \\(z\\in D\\), applying \\(E\\) leaves \\(z\\) fixed and, by ([PW26](OA-FLOW-PW.md#equation-pw26)), expresses it as a linear combination of elements of \\(F_\\tau\\). Hence \\(z\\in m_\\tau\\).\n\nFor completeness, positivity of the integral of\n\\((\\alpha_t(x)-E(x))^*(\\alpha_t(x)-E(x))\\) gives\n\n<a id=\"equation-pw28\"><\/a>\n\n\\[\n E(x)^*E(x)\\leq E(x^*x).                                    \\tag{PW28}\n\\]\nTo verify the expansion, ([PW21](OA-FLOW-PW.md#equation-pw21)) averages \\(\\alpha_t(x)\\) and its adjoint to \\(E(x)\\) and \\(E(x)^*\\); the remaining constant product is unchanged. All integrands are bounded operators. Equations ([PW26](OA-FLOW-PW.md#equation-pw26))–([PW28](OA-FLOW-PW.md#equation-pw28)) imply \\(E(N_\\rho)=N_\\tau\\); applying them to \\(x^*\\) gives \\(E(A_\\rho)=A_\\tau\\). The finite positive argument above gives \\(E(m_\\rho)=m_\\tau\\), and linearity of the finite extensions gives\n\n<a id=\"equation-pw29\"><\/a>\n\n\\[\n \\tau_0(E(z))=\\rho_0(z)\\qquad(z\\in m_\\rho).                   \\tag{PW29}\n\\]\n\nThe identity automorphism group of \\(D\\) preserves \\(\\tau\\). If \\(a,b\\in A_\\tau\\), they lie in the full \\(A_\\rho\\), and are fixed by \\(\\alpha_t\\). The bounded closed-strip function supplied by [KT2](OA-FLOW-KT.md#oa-flow.kt.2) for \\(\\rho\\) therefore has boundaries \\(\\tau_0(ab)\\) and \\(\\tau_0(ba)\\). Products lie in \\(m_\\tau\\), by ([PW27](OA-FLOW-PW.md#equation-pw27)). This is exactly the full finite-star KMS hypothesis of [KU1](OA-FLOW-KU.md#oa-flow.ku.1)–3 for the identity group on \\(D\\). Its weight is now proved faithful normal semifinite, so uniqueness gives \\(\\sigma_t^\\tau=\\mathrm{id}\\). [KT5](OA-FLOW-KT.md#oa-flow.kt.5) then proves\n\n<a id=\"equation-pw30\"><\/a>\n\n\\[\n \\tau(x^*x)=\\tau(xx^*)\\quad(x\\in D),                          \\tag{PW30}\n\\]\nincluding every infinite value. Thus \\(\\tau\\) is a faithful normal semifinite trace. Apply this entire construction to \\(\\rho=\\phi\\) from [PW3](OA-FLOW-PW.md#oa-flow.pw.3) to obtain ([PW2](OA-FLOW-PW.md#equation-pw2)).\n\n<a id=\"pw-6\"><\/a>\n\n<a id=\"oa-flow.pw.6\"><\/a>","html":"<h2>PW-5. Weight preservation including infinity, and the restricted trace<\/h2>\n<p>For <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(a\\geq0\\)\">\\(a\\geq0\\)<\/span>, each positive Riemann average has <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\rho(E_n(a))=\\rho(a)\\)\">\\(\\rho(E_n(a))=\\rho(a)\\)<\/span>, by whole-cone modular invariance and additivity. If the value is finite, <a href=\"../courses/OA-FLOW/OA-FLOW-EW.html#oa-flow.ew.3\">EW3<\/a>'s ultraweak lower semicontinuity gives <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\rho(E(a))\\leq\\rho(a)\\)\">\\(\\rho(E(a))\\leq\\rho(a)\\)<\/span>; if it is infinite this inequality is automatic.<\/p>\n<p>For the reverse inequality use precisely <a href=\"../courses/OA-FLOW/OA-FLOW-WS.html#oa-flow.weight-sum.ws3\">WS3<\/a>, which proves that<\/p>\n<p><span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n \\mathcal D_\\rho=\\{f\\in M_*^+:f\\leq r\\rho\n                    \\text{ for some }0&lt;r&lt;1\\}\n \\text{ is directed},\\qquad\n \\sup_{f\\in\\mathcal D_\\rho}f(z)=\\rho(z)\\quad(z\\geq0).\n \\tag{PW25}\n\\]\">\\[\n \\mathcal D_\\rho=\\{f\\in M_*^+:f\\leq r\\rho\n                    \\text{ for some }0&lt;r&lt;1\\}\n \\text{ is directed},\\qquad\n \\sup_{f\\in\\mathcal D_\\rho}f(z)=\\rho(z)\\quad(z\\geq0).\n \\tag{PW25}\n\\]<\/span>\nFix a real number <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(L&lt;\\rho(a)\\)\">\\(L&lt;\\rho(a)\\)<\/span>. When <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\rho(a)=0\\)\">\\(\\rho(a)=0\\)<\/span> the desired inequality is already immediate; otherwise take <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(0\\leq L&lt;\\rho(a)\\)\">\\(0\\leq L&lt;\\rho(a)\\)<\/span>, with <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(L\\)\">\\(L\\)<\/span> arbitrarily large when the value is infinite. The continuous functions\n<span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(t\\mapsto f(\\alpha_t(a))\\)\">\\(t\\mapsto f(\\alpha_t(a))\\)<\/span>, <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(f\\in\\mathcal D_\\rho\\)\">\\(f\\in\\mathcal D_\\rho\\)<\/span>, increase as a directed family and have the constant supremum <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\rho(a)\\)\">\\(\\rho(a)\\)<\/span> at every <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(t\\)\">\\(t\\)<\/span>. The open sets where they exceed <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(L\\)\">\\(L\\)<\/span> cover <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(K_P\\)\">\\(K_P\\)<\/span>. A finite subcover and directedness give one <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(f\\)\">\\(f\\)<\/span> for which <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(f(\\alpha_t(a))&gt;L\\)\">\\(f(\\alpha_t(a))&gt;L\\)<\/span> everywhere. Since <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(f\\leq\\rho\\)\">\\(f\\leq\\rho\\)<\/span>, (<a href=\"../courses/OA-FLOW/OA-FLOW-PW.html#equation-pw22\">PW22<\/a>) implies\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n \\rho(E(a))\\geq f(E(a))\n   =\\int_{K_P}f(\\alpha_t(a))\\,dm_{K_P}(t)\\geq L .\n\\]\">\\[\n \\rho(E(a))\\geq f(E(a))\n   =\\int_{K_P}f(\\alpha_t(a))\\,dm_{K_P}(t)\\geq L .\n\\]<\/span>\nLetting <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(L\\uparrow\\rho(a)\\)\">\\(L\\uparrow\\rho(a)\\)<\/span>, or letting <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(L\\)\">\\(L\\)<\/span> tend to infinity, proves<\/p>\n<p><span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n \\rho(E(a))=\\rho(a)\\qquad(a\\in M_+).                          \\tag{PW26}\n\\]\">\\[\n \\rho(E(a))=\\rho(a)\\qquad(a\\in M_+).                          \\tag{PW26}\n\\]<\/span>\nThis is a full-cone proof. It never interchanges an unbounded weight with an integral, and uses neither a countable decomposition of <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\rho\\)\">\\(\\rho\\)<\/span> nor a countability assumption on <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(M\\)\">\\(M\\)<\/span>.<\/p>\n<p>Put <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\tau=\\rho|_{D_+}\\)\">\\(\\tau=\\rho|_{D_+}\\)<\/span>. This restriction is faithful and normal, since increasing suprema in the fixed von Neumann algebra agree with those in <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(M\\)\">\\(M\\)<\/span>. By <a href=\"../courses/OA-FLOW/OA-FLOW-GW.html#oa-flow.gw.4\">GW4<\/a> there are finite positive contractions <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(u_i\\uparrow1\\)\">\\(u_i\\uparrow1\\)<\/span> in <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(M\\)\">\\(M\\)<\/span>. Equations (<a href=\"../courses/OA-FLOW/OA-FLOW-PW.html#equation-pw24\">PW24<\/a>) and (<a href=\"../courses/OA-FLOW/OA-FLOW-PW.html#equation-pw26\">PW26<\/a>) give finite positive contractions <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(E(u_i)\\uparrow1\\)\">\\(E(u_i)\\uparrow1\\)<\/span> in <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(D\\)\">\\(D\\)<\/span>. <a href=\"../courses/OA-FLOW/OA-FLOW-GW.html#oa-flow.gw.4\">GW4<\/a>, now inside <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(D\\)\">\\(D\\)<\/span>, proves semifiniteness of <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\tau\\)\">\\(\\tau\\)<\/span>.<\/p>\n<p>The finite domains have the exact descriptions<\/p>\n<p><span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n F_\\tau=F_\\rho\\cap D,\\quad N_\\tau=N_\\rho\\cap D,\\quad\n A_\\tau=A_\\rho\\cap D,\\quad m_\\tau=m_\\rho\\cap D.                \\tag{PW27}\n\\]\">\\[\n F_\\tau=F_\\rho\\cap D,\\quad N_\\tau=N_\\rho\\cap D,\\quad\n A_\\tau=A_\\rho\\cap D,\\quad m_\\tau=m_\\rho\\cap D.                \\tag{PW27}\n\\]<\/span>\nThe first three follow from restriction. To prove the last, <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(m_\\tau\\subseteq m_\\rho\\cap D\\)\">\\(m_\\tau\\subseteq m_\\rho\\cap D\\)<\/span> is immediate. Conversely <a href=\"../courses/OA-FLOW/OA-FLOW-GW.html#oa-flow.gw.1\">GW1<\/a> expresses any <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(z\\in m_\\rho\\)\">\\(z\\in m_\\rho\\)<\/span> as a finite complex linear combination of positive finite-weight elements. If <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(z\\in D\\)\">\\(z\\in D\\)<\/span>, applying <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(E\\)\">\\(E\\)<\/span> leaves <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(z\\)\">\\(z\\)<\/span> fixed and, by (<a href=\"../courses/OA-FLOW/OA-FLOW-PW.html#equation-pw26\">PW26<\/a>), expresses it as a linear combination of elements of <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(F_\\tau\\)\">\\(F_\\tau\\)<\/span>. Hence <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(z\\in m_\\tau\\)\">\\(z\\in m_\\tau\\)<\/span>.<\/p>\n<p>For completeness, positivity of the integral of\n<span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\((\\alpha_t(x)-E(x))^*(\\alpha_t(x)-E(x))\\)\">\\((\\alpha_t(x)-E(x))^*(\\alpha_t(x)-E(x))\\)<\/span> gives<\/p>\n<p><span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n E(x)^*E(x)\\leq E(x^*x).                                    \\tag{PW28}\n\\]\">\\[\n E(x)^*E(x)\\leq E(x^*x).                                    \\tag{PW28}\n\\]<\/span>\nTo verify the expansion, (<a href=\"../courses/OA-FLOW/OA-FLOW-PW.html#equation-pw21\">PW21<\/a>) averages <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\alpha_t(x)\\)\">\\(\\alpha_t(x)\\)<\/span> and its adjoint to <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(E(x)\\)\">\\(E(x)\\)<\/span> and <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(E(x)^*\\)\">\\(E(x)^*\\)<\/span>; the remaining constant product is unchanged. All integrands are bounded operators. Equations (<a href=\"../courses/OA-FLOW/OA-FLOW-PW.html#equation-pw26\">PW26<\/a>)–(<a href=\"../courses/OA-FLOW/OA-FLOW-PW.html#equation-pw28\">PW28<\/a>) imply <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(E(N_\\rho)=N_\\tau\\)\">\\(E(N_\\rho)=N_\\tau\\)<\/span>; applying them to <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(x^*\\)\">\\(x^*\\)<\/span> gives <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(E(A_\\rho)=A_\\tau\\)\">\\(E(A_\\rho)=A_\\tau\\)<\/span>. The finite positive argument above gives <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(E(m_\\rho)=m_\\tau\\)\">\\(E(m_\\rho)=m_\\tau\\)<\/span>, and linearity of the finite extensions gives<\/p>\n<p><span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n \\tau_0(E(z))=\\rho_0(z)\\qquad(z\\in m_\\rho).                   \\tag{PW29}\n\\]\">\\[\n \\tau_0(E(z))=\\rho_0(z)\\qquad(z\\in m_\\rho).                   \\tag{PW29}\n\\]<\/span><\/p>\n<p>The identity automorphism group of <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(D\\)\">\\(D\\)<\/span> preserves <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\tau\\)\">\\(\\tau\\)<\/span>. If <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(a,b\\in A_\\tau\\)\">\\(a,b\\in A_\\tau\\)<\/span>, they lie in the full <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(A_\\rho\\)\">\\(A_\\rho\\)<\/span>, and are fixed by <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\alpha_t\\)\">\\(\\alpha_t\\)<\/span>. The bounded closed-strip function supplied by <a href=\"../courses/OA-FLOW/OA-FLOW-KT.html#oa-flow.kt.2\">KT2<\/a> for <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\rho\\)\">\\(\\rho\\)<\/span> therefore has boundaries <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\tau_0(ab)\\)\">\\(\\tau_0(ab)\\)<\/span> and <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\tau_0(ba)\\)\">\\(\\tau_0(ba)\\)<\/span>. Products lie in <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(m_\\tau\\)\">\\(m_\\tau\\)<\/span>, by (<a href=\"../courses/OA-FLOW/OA-FLOW-PW.html#equation-pw27\">PW27<\/a>). This is exactly the full finite-star KMS hypothesis of <a href=\"../courses/OA-FLOW/OA-FLOW-KU.html#oa-flow.ku.1\">KU1<\/a>–3 for the identity group on <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(D\\)\">\\(D\\)<\/span>. Its weight is now proved faithful normal semifinite, so uniqueness gives <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\sigma_t^\\tau=\\mathrm{id}\\)\">\\(\\sigma_t^\\tau=\\mathrm{id}\\)<\/span>. <a href=\"../courses/OA-FLOW/OA-FLOW-KT.html#oa-flow.kt.5\">KT5<\/a> then proves<\/p>\n<p><span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n \\tau(x^*x)=\\tau(xx^*)\\quad(x\\in D),                          \\tag{PW30}\n\\]\">\\[\n \\tau(x^*x)=\\tau(xx^*)\\quad(x\\in D),                          \\tag{PW30}\n\\]<\/span>\nincluding every infinite value. Thus <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\tau\\)\">\\(\\tau\\)<\/span> is a faithful normal semifinite trace. Apply this entire construction to <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\rho=\\phi\\)\">\\(\\rho=\\phi\\)<\/span> from <a href=\"../courses/OA-FLOW/OA-FLOW-PW.html#oa-flow.pw.3\">PW3<\/a> to obtain (<a href=\"../courses/OA-FLOW/OA-FLOW-PW.html#equation-pw2\">PW2<\/a>).<\/p>\n","source":"docs/courses/OA-FLOW/src/OA-FLOW-PW.md","start_offset":17138,"end_offset":21811,"role":"registered_statement_and_proof_passage"},"d76905f4a51d8d4608d80236d2c7fb46931a2b30ceeee32671d8c53f15e5919d":{"markdown":"# Discrete decomposition exists for every separable-predual type III lambda factor\n\n*CC0-1.0 to the extent of rights held.*\n\nThe starting data are a nonzero von Neumann factor \\(M\\) with separable predual, of type III, and a number \\(0<\\lambda<1\\) such that\n\n<a id=\"equation-dd1\"><\/a>\n\n\\[\n S(M)=\\bigcap_{\\psi\\ {\\rm faithful\\ normal\\ semifinite}}\n              \\operatorname{Sp}(\\Delta_\\psi)\n      =\\{0\\}\\cup\\lambda^{\\mathbb Z}.\n \\tag{DD1}\n\\]\nThus “type \\(\\mathrm{III}_\\lambda\\)” has the explicit meaning in [DD1](OA-FLOW-DD.md#oa-flow.dd.1). Put \\(a=-\\log\\lambda>0\\) and \\(P=2\\pi/a\\). No weight, period or implementing unitary is additional starting data.\n\nThe earlier complete proofs are [CP4 and CP6](OA-FLOW-CP.md#oa-flow.cp.4) for the concrete norm-closed predual; [CF1](OA-FLOW-CF.md#oa-flow.cf.1) for choice, convergent Banach series and elementary limits; [the inner-period construction, IP0–6](OA-FLOW-IP.md#oa-flow.ip.0); [PW1–6](OA-FLOW-PW.md#oa-flow.pw.1) for phase cancellation and full-cone compact averaging; [CA0–7](OA-FLOW-CA.md#oa-flow.ca.0) for the full amplified weight and graph transport; [PF1–7](OA-FLOW-PF.md#oa-flow.pf.1) for the centralizer, infinite-trace comparison and least period; and [GT1–6](OA-FLOW-GT.md#oa-flow.gt.1) for the actual regular decomposition and compact double dual. The spectral identification below uses [MW4](OA-FLOW-MW.md#oa-flow.mw.4), [RF5](OA-FLOW-RF.md#oa-flow.rf.5) and [SF's full Borel domains](OA-FLOW-SF.md#oa-flow.sf.sb4). [GW1–4](OA-FLOW-GW.md#oa-flow.gw.1) supply the finite ideals, and [PC5's filling family](OA-FLOW-PC.md#oa-flow.projection.pc5) applies to the type III unit. These inputs have written proofs; their precise source ranges and order are recorded in the accompanying ledger.\n\nThe free human source actually read is [Connes (1973), Theorem4.3.2(a) and Corollary4.3.3, printed220–222](https://numdam.org/article/ASENS_1973_4_6_2_133_0.pdf#page=89). Its existence mechanism motivates the state, period and amplification chain. Every step here is proved in the earlier programme or below. The comparison assertions in Theorem4.3.2(b,c), and the converse at the beginning of4.4, are not conclusions of this chapter.\n\n<a id=\"oa-flow.dd.0\"><\/a>\n## DD-0. The existence theorem, with exact maps\n\nThere exist a faithful normal semifinite weight \\(\\phi\\) on \\(M\\), a type \\(\\mathrm{II}_\\infty\\) factor \\(N\\subseteq M\\), a faithful normal semifinite trace \\(\\tau\\) on \\(N\\), and a unitary \\(U\\in M\\), with\n\n<a id=\"equation-dd2\"><\/a>\n\n\\[\n\\begin{gathered}\n \\phi(1)=\\infty,\\qquad\n N=M_\\phi=\\{d:\\sigma_t^\\phi(d)=d\\text{ for all }t\\},\\\\\n \\{t:\\sigma_t^\\phi=\\mathrm{id}\\}=P\\mathbb Z,\\qquad\n \\sigma_t^\\phi(U)=\\lambda^{it}U,\\\\\n \\theta=\\operatorname{Ad}U|_N,\\qquad\n \\tau(\\theta^m(d))=\\lambda^m\\tau(d)\n       \\quad(d\\in N_+,\\ m\\in\\mathbb Z).\n \\tag{DD2}\n\\end{gathered}\n\\]\nAll positive-cone formulas include infinite values. The normalized period average \\(E:M\\to N\\) is a faithful normal conditional expectation and\n\n<a id=\"equation-dd3\"><\/a>\n\n\\[\n E(x)=\\frac1P\\int_0^P\\sigma_t^\\phi(x)\\,dt,\\qquad\n \\phi(x)=\\tau(E(x))\\ (x\\in M_+).\n \\tag{DD3}\n\\]\nThe integral means the bounded strong vector integral in the faithful normal weight GNS representation, transported normally to \\(M\\).\n\nOn the complete Hilbert space \\(\\mathcal K=\\ell^2(\\mathbb Z,H_\\tau)\\), form\n\n<a id=\"equation-dd4\"><\/a>\n\n\\[\n (\\pi(d)\\xi)_n=\\pi_\\tau(\\theta^{-n}(d))\\xi_n,\\qquad\n (s\\xi)_n=\\xi_{n-1},\\qquad\n C=(\\pi(N)\\cup\\{s\\})''.\n \\tag{DD4}\n\\]\nThere is an onto unitary and a normal isomorphism with normal inverse\n\n<a id=\"equation-dd5\"><\/a>\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n W:\\mathcal K&\\longrightarrow H_\\phi,&\n W(\\delta_n\\otimes\\Lambda_\\tau(x))&=\\Lambda_\\phi(U^n x)\n                       &&(x\\in\\mathfrak n_\\tau),\\\\\n \\Xi=\\pi_\\phi^{-1}\\operatorname{Ad}W:C&\\longrightarrow M,&\n \\Xi(\\pi(d))&=d,\\quad \\Xi(s)=U .\n \\tag{DD5}\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\nThe ordinary notation \\(N\\rtimes_\\theta\\mathbb Z\\) denotes this specified regular algebra \\(C\\).\n\nIn these coordinates the entire modular operator is\n\n<a id=\"equation-dd6\"><\/a>\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n (W^*\\Delta_\\phi W\\,\\xi)_n&=\\lambda^n\\xi_n,\\\\\n D(W^*\\Delta_\\phi W)&=\n   \\left\\{\\xi\\in\\mathcal K:\n                  \\sum_{n\\in\\mathbb Z}\\lambda^{2n}\\|\\xi_n\\|^2<\\infty\\right\\},\\\\\n \\operatorname{Sp}(\\Delta_\\phi)&=\\{0\\}\\cup\\lambda^{\\mathbb Z},\n       \\qquad \\ker\\Delta_\\phi=\\{0\\}.\n \\tag{DD6}\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n[DD6](OA-FLOW-DD.md#oa-flow.dd.6) concerns the full closed operator; every Borel power and logarithmic domain is given in [DD-6](OA-FLOW-DD.md#oa-flow.dd.6). The compact action \\(\\kappa_{e^{iat}}=\\sigma_t^\\phi\\) has the double-dual identification and trace specified in [DD-8](OA-FLOW-DD.md#oa-flow.dd.8).\n\n<a id=\"oa-flow.dd.1\"><\/a>\n## DD-1. An explicit faithful normal state\n\nTake any faithful concrete representation \\(M\\subseteq B(K)\\). Its unit is nonzero. Every unit vector \\(\\xi\\in K\\) gives a normal state \\(\\omega_\\xi(x)=\\langle x\\xi,\\xi\\rangle\\), by [CP4](OA-FLOW-CP.md#oa-flow.cp.4)/6. In particular the normal state space is nonempty. To see explicitly that it is norm separable, take a countable dense family in the separable Banach space \\(M_*\\). For each ball with center in this family and positive rational radius that meets the normal state space, choose one normal state in the ball. There are countably many such balls. The chosen states are dense: for a state and any positive tolerance, first choose a nearby center and then a rational radius making that ball both contain the state and have diameter less than the tolerance. List the chosen family as a sequence \\((\\omega_j)_{j\\ge1}\\), repeating terms if the family is finite.\n\nDefine in the Banach predual\n\n<a id=\"equation-dd7\"><\/a>\n\n\\[\n \\psi=\\sum_{j\\ge1}2^{-j}\\omega_j .\n \\tag{DD7}\n\\]\nEach state has norm one. The series is absolutely norm convergent; [CP6](OA-FLOW-CP.md#oa-flow.cp.6) makes its limit a normal functional on all of \\(M\\). Norm convergence gives \\(\\psi(1)=1\\), and positivity follows by evaluating the positive partial sums on every positive element.\n\nIf \\(x\\in M_+\\setminus\\{0\\}\\), its positive square root is nonzero. Choose a unit vector \\(\\xi\\) with \\(x^{1/2}\\xi\\ne0\\); then \\(\\omega_\\xi(x)>0\\). Choose \\(j\\) with\n\\(\\|\\omega_j-\\omega_\\xi\\|<\\omega_\\xi(x)/(2\\|x\\|)\\).\nIt follows that \\(\\omega_j(x)>\\omega_\\xi(x)/2>0\\), and [DD7](OA-FLOW-DD.md#oa-flow.dd.7) gives \\(\\psi(x)>0\\). Thus \\(\\psi\\) is faithful. Its whole positive cone is finite, since \\(\\psi(x)\\le\\|x\\|\\). Therefore it is semifinite, with\n\\(\\mathfrak n_\\psi=A_\\psi=\\mathfrak m_\\psi=M\\).\nThe state in [DD7](OA-FLOW-DD.md#oa-flow.dd.7) is now legitimate input to the full n.s.f. modular construction, without supposing that a faithful state was supplied.\n\n<a id=\"oa-flow.dd.2\"><\/a>","html":"<h1>Discrete decomposition exists for every separable-predual type III lambda factor<\/h1>\n<p><em>CC0-1.0 to the extent of rights held.<\/em><\/p>\n<p>The starting data are a nonzero von Neumann factor <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(M\\)\">\\(M\\)<\/span> with separable predual, of type III, and a number <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(0&lt;\\lambda&lt;1\\)\">\\(0&lt;\\lambda&lt;1\\)<\/span> such that<\/p>\n<p><span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n S(M)=\\bigcap_{\\psi\\ {\\rm faithful\\ normal\\ semifinite}}\n              \\operatorname{Sp}(\\Delta_\\psi)\n      =\\{0\\}\\cup\\lambda^{\\mathbb Z}.\n \\tag{DD1}\n\\]\">\\[\n S(M)=\\bigcap_{\\psi\\ {\\rm faithful\\ normal\\ semifinite}}\n              \\operatorname{Sp}(\\Delta_\\psi)\n      =\\{0\\}\\cup\\lambda^{\\mathbb Z}.\n \\tag{DD1}\n\\]<\/span>\nThus “type <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\mathrm{III}_\\lambda\\)\">\\(\\mathrm{III}_\\lambda\\)<\/span>” has the explicit meaning in <a href=\"../courses/OA-FLOW/OA-FLOW-DD.html#oa-flow.dd.1\">DD1<\/a>. Put <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(a=-\\log\\lambda&gt;0\\)\">\\(a=-\\log\\lambda&gt;0\\)<\/span> and <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(P=2\\pi/a\\)\">\\(P=2\\pi/a\\)<\/span>. No weight, period or implementing unitary is additional starting data.<\/p>\n<p>The earlier complete proofs are <a href=\"../courses/OA-FLOW/OA-FLOW-CP.html#oa-flow.cp.4\">CP4 and CP6<\/a> for the concrete norm-closed predual; <a href=\"../courses/OA-FLOW/OA-FLOW-CF.html#oa-flow.cf.1\">CF1<\/a> for choice, convergent Banach series and elementary limits; <a href=\"../courses/OA-FLOW/OA-FLOW-IP.html#oa-flow.ip.0\">the inner-period construction, IP0–6<\/a>; <a href=\"../courses/OA-FLOW/OA-FLOW-PW.html#oa-flow.pw.1\">PW1–6<\/a> for phase cancellation and full-cone compact averaging; <a href=\"../courses/OA-FLOW/OA-FLOW-CA.html#oa-flow.ca.0\">CA0–7<\/a> for the full amplified weight and graph transport; <a href=\"../courses/OA-FLOW/OA-FLOW-PF.html#oa-flow.pf.1\">PF1–7<\/a> for the centralizer, infinite-trace comparison and least period; and <a href=\"../courses/OA-FLOW/OA-FLOW-GT.html#oa-flow.gt.1\">GT1–6<\/a> for the actual regular decomposition and compact double dual. The spectral identification below uses <a href=\"../courses/OA-FLOW/OA-FLOW-MW.html#oa-flow.mw.4\">MW4<\/a>, <a href=\"../courses/OA-FLOW/OA-FLOW-RF.html#oa-flow.rf.5\">RF5<\/a> and <a href=\"../courses/OA-FLOW/OA-FLOW-SF.html#oa-flow.sf.sb4\">SF's full Borel domains<\/a>. <a href=\"../courses/OA-FLOW/OA-FLOW-GW.html#oa-flow.gw.1\">GW1–4<\/a> supply the finite ideals, and <a href=\"../courses/OA-FLOW/OA-FLOW-PC.html#oa-flow.projection.pc5\">PC5's filling family<\/a> applies to the type III unit. These inputs have written proofs; their precise source ranges and order are recorded in the accompanying ledger.<\/p>\n<p>The free human source actually read is <a href=\"https://numdam.org/article/ASENS_1973_4_6_2_133_0.pdf#page=89\">Connes (1973), Theorem4.3.2(a) and Corollary4.3.3, printed220–222<\/a>. Its existence mechanism motivates the state, period and amplification chain. Every step here is proved in the earlier programme or below. The comparison assertions in Theorem4.3.2(b,c), and the converse at the beginning of4.4, are not conclusions of this chapter.<\/p>\n<h2>DD-0. The existence theorem, with exact maps<\/h2>\n<p>There exist a faithful normal semifinite weight <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\phi\\)\">\\(\\phi\\)<\/span> on <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(M\\)\">\\(M\\)<\/span>, a type <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\mathrm{II}_\\infty\\)\">\\(\\mathrm{II}_\\infty\\)<\/span> factor <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(N\\subseteq M\\)\">\\(N\\subseteq M\\)<\/span>, a faithful normal semifinite trace <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\tau\\)\">\\(\\tau\\)<\/span> on <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(N\\)\">\\(N\\)<\/span>, and a unitary <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(U\\in M\\)\">\\(U\\in M\\)<\/span>, with<\/p>\n<p><span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n\\begin{gathered}\n \\phi(1)=\\infty,\\qquad\n N=M_\\phi=\\{d:\\sigma_t^\\phi(d)=d\\text{ for all }t\\},\\\\\n \\{t:\\sigma_t^\\phi=\\mathrm{id}\\}=P\\mathbb Z,\\qquad\n \\sigma_t^\\phi(U)=\\lambda^{it}U,\\\\\n \\theta=\\operatorname{Ad}U|_N,\\qquad\n \\tau(\\theta^m(d))=\\lambda^m\\tau(d)\n       \\quad(d\\in N_+,\\ m\\in\\mathbb Z).\n \\tag{DD2}\n\\end{gathered}\n\\]\">\\[\n\\begin{gathered}\n \\phi(1)=\\infty,\\qquad\n N=M_\\phi=\\{d:\\sigma_t^\\phi(d)=d\\text{ for all }t\\},\\\\\n \\{t:\\sigma_t^\\phi=\\mathrm{id}\\}=P\\mathbb Z,\\qquad\n \\sigma_t^\\phi(U)=\\lambda^{it}U,\\\\\n \\theta=\\operatorname{Ad}U|_N,\\qquad\n \\tau(\\theta^m(d))=\\lambda^m\\tau(d)\n       \\quad(d\\in N_+,\\ m\\in\\mathbb Z).\n \\tag{DD2}\n\\end{gathered}\n\\]<\/span>\nAll positive-cone formulas include infinite values. The normalized period average <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(E:M\\to N\\)\">\\(E:M\\to N\\)<\/span> is a faithful normal conditional expectation and<\/p>\n<p><span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n E(x)=\\frac1P\\int_0^P\\sigma_t^\\phi(x)\\,dt,\\qquad\n \\phi(x)=\\tau(E(x))\\ (x\\in M_+).\n \\tag{DD3}\n\\]\">\\[\n E(x)=\\frac1P\\int_0^P\\sigma_t^\\phi(x)\\,dt,\\qquad\n \\phi(x)=\\tau(E(x))\\ (x\\in M_+).\n \\tag{DD3}\n\\]<\/span>\nThe integral means the bounded strong vector integral in the faithful normal weight GNS representation, transported normally to <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(M\\)\">\\(M\\)<\/span>.<\/p>\n<p>On the complete Hilbert space <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\mathcal K=\\ell^2(\\mathbb Z,H_\\tau)\\)\">\\(\\mathcal K=\\ell^2(\\mathbb Z,H_\\tau)\\)<\/span>, form<\/p>\n<p><span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n (\\pi(d)\\xi)_n=\\pi_\\tau(\\theta^{-n}(d))\\xi_n,\\qquad\n (s\\xi)_n=\\xi_{n-1},\\qquad\n C=(\\pi(N)\\cup\\{s\\})&#x27;&#x27;.\n \\tag{DD4}\n\\]\">\\[\n (\\pi(d)\\xi)_n=\\pi_\\tau(\\theta^{-n}(d))\\xi_n,\\qquad\n (s\\xi)_n=\\xi_{n-1},\\qquad\n C=(\\pi(N)\\cup\\{s\\})&#x27;&#x27;.\n \\tag{DD4}\n\\]<\/span>\nThere is an onto unitary and a normal isomorphism with normal inverse<\/p>\n<p><span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n W:\\mathcal K&amp;\\longrightarrow H_\\phi,&amp;\n W(\\delta_n\\otimes\\Lambda_\\tau(x))&amp;=\\Lambda_\\phi(U^n x)\n                       &amp;&amp;(x\\in\\mathfrak n_\\tau),\\\\\n \\Xi=\\pi_\\phi^{-1}\\operatorname{Ad}W:C&amp;\\longrightarrow M,&amp;\n \\Xi(\\pi(d))&amp;=d,\\quad \\Xi(s)=U .\n \\tag{DD5}\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\">\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n W:\\mathcal K&amp;\\longrightarrow H_\\phi,&amp;\n W(\\delta_n\\otimes\\Lambda_\\tau(x))&amp;=\\Lambda_\\phi(U^n x)\n                       &amp;&amp;(x\\in\\mathfrak n_\\tau),\\\\\n \\Xi=\\pi_\\phi^{-1}\\operatorname{Ad}W:C&amp;\\longrightarrow M,&amp;\n \\Xi(\\pi(d))&amp;=d,\\quad \\Xi(s)=U .\n \\tag{DD5}\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]<\/span>\nThe ordinary notation <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(N\\rtimes_\\theta\\mathbb Z\\)\">\\(N\\rtimes_\\theta\\mathbb Z\\)<\/span> denotes this specified regular algebra <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(C\\)\">\\(C\\)<\/span>.<\/p>\n<p>In these coordinates the entire modular operator is<\/p>\n<p><span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n (W^*\\Delta_\\phi W\\,\\xi)_n&amp;=\\lambda^n\\xi_n,\\\\\n D(W^*\\Delta_\\phi W)&amp;=\n   \\left\\{\\xi\\in\\mathcal K:\n                  \\sum_{n\\in\\mathbb Z}\\lambda^{2n}\\|\\xi_n\\|^2&lt;\\infty\\right\\},\\\\\n \\operatorname{Sp}(\\Delta_\\phi)&amp;=\\{0\\}\\cup\\lambda^{\\mathbb Z},\n       \\qquad \\ker\\Delta_\\phi=\\{0\\}.\n \\tag{DD6}\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\">\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n (W^*\\Delta_\\phi W\\,\\xi)_n&amp;=\\lambda^n\\xi_n,\\\\\n D(W^*\\Delta_\\phi W)&amp;=\n   \\left\\{\\xi\\in\\mathcal K:\n                  \\sum_{n\\in\\mathbb Z}\\lambda^{2n}\\|\\xi_n\\|^2&lt;\\infty\\right\\},\\\\\n \\operatorname{Sp}(\\Delta_\\phi)&amp;=\\{0\\}\\cup\\lambda^{\\mathbb Z},\n       \\qquad \\ker\\Delta_\\phi=\\{0\\}.\n \\tag{DD6}\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]<\/span>\n<a href=\"../courses/OA-FLOW/OA-FLOW-DD.html#oa-flow.dd.6\">DD6<\/a> concerns the full closed operator; every Borel power and logarithmic domain is given in <a href=\"../courses/OA-FLOW/OA-FLOW-DD.html#oa-flow.dd.6\">DD-6<\/a>. The compact action <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\kappa_{e^{iat}}=\\sigma_t^\\phi\\)\">\\(\\kappa_{e^{iat}}=\\sigma_t^\\phi\\)<\/span> has the double-dual identification and trace specified in <a href=\"../courses/OA-FLOW/OA-FLOW-DD.html#oa-flow.dd.8\">DD-8<\/a>.<\/p>\n<h2>DD-1. An explicit faithful normal state<\/h2>\n<p>Take any faithful concrete representation <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(M\\subseteq B(K)\\)\">\\(M\\subseteq B(K)\\)<\/span>. Its unit is nonzero. Every unit vector <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\xi\\in K\\)\">\\(\\xi\\in K\\)<\/span> gives a normal state <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\omega_\\xi(x)=\\langle x\\xi,\\xi\\rangle\\)\">\\(\\omega_\\xi(x)=\\langle x\\xi,\\xi\\rangle\\)<\/span>, by <a href=\"../courses/OA-FLOW/OA-FLOW-CP.html#oa-flow.cp.4\">CP4<\/a>/6. In particular the normal state space is nonempty. To see explicitly that it is norm separable, take a countable dense family in the separable Banach space <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(M_*\\)\">\\(M_*\\)<\/span>. For each ball with center in this family and positive rational radius that meets the normal state space, choose one normal state in the ball. There are countably many such balls. The chosen states are dense: for a state and any positive tolerance, first choose a nearby center and then a rational radius making that ball both contain the state and have diameter less than the tolerance. List the chosen family as a sequence <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\((\\omega_j)_{j\\ge1}\\)\">\\((\\omega_j)_{j\\ge1}\\)<\/span>, repeating terms if the family is finite.<\/p>\n<p>Define in the Banach predual<\/p>\n<p><span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n \\psi=\\sum_{j\\ge1}2^{-j}\\omega_j .\n \\tag{DD7}\n\\]\">\\[\n \\psi=\\sum_{j\\ge1}2^{-j}\\omega_j .\n \\tag{DD7}\n\\]<\/span>\nEach state has norm one. The series is absolutely norm convergent; <a href=\"../courses/OA-FLOW/OA-FLOW-CP.html#oa-flow.cp.6\">CP6<\/a> makes its limit a normal functional on all of <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(M\\)\">\\(M\\)<\/span>. Norm convergence gives <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\psi(1)=1\\)\">\\(\\psi(1)=1\\)<\/span>, and positivity follows by evaluating the positive partial sums on every positive element.<\/p>\n<p>If <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(x\\in M_+\\setminus\\{0\\}\\)\">\\(x\\in M_+\\setminus\\{0\\}\\)<\/span>, its positive square root is nonzero. Choose a unit vector <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\xi\\)\">\\(\\xi\\)<\/span> with <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(x^{1/2}\\xi\\ne0\\)\">\\(x^{1/2}\\xi\\ne0\\)<\/span>; then <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\omega_\\xi(x)&gt;0\\)\">\\(\\omega_\\xi(x)&gt;0\\)<\/span>. Choose <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(j\\)\">\\(j\\)<\/span> with\n<span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\|\\omega_j-\\omega_\\xi\\|&lt;\\omega_\\xi(x)/(2\\|x\\|)\\)\">\\(\\|\\omega_j-\\omega_\\xi\\|&lt;\\omega_\\xi(x)/(2\\|x\\|)\\)<\/span>.\nIt follows that <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\omega_j(x)&gt;\\omega_\\xi(x)/2&gt;0\\)\">\\(\\omega_j(x)&gt;\\omega_\\xi(x)/2&gt;0\\)<\/span>, and <a href=\"../courses/OA-FLOW/OA-FLOW-DD.html#oa-flow.dd.7\">DD7<\/a> gives <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\psi(x)&gt;0\\)\">\\(\\psi(x)&gt;0\\)<\/span>. Thus <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\psi\\)\">\\(\\psi\\)<\/span> is faithful. Its whole positive cone is finite, since <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\psi(x)\\le\\|x\\|\\)\">\\(\\psi(x)\\le\\|x\\|\\)<\/span>. Therefore it is semifinite, with\n<span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\mathfrak n_\\psi=A_\\psi=\\mathfrak m_\\psi=M\\)\">\\(\\mathfrak n_\\psi=A_\\psi=\\mathfrak m_\\psi=M\\)<\/span>.\nThe state in <a href=\"../courses/OA-FLOW/OA-FLOW-DD.html#oa-flow.dd.7\">DD7<\/a> is now legitimate input to the full n.s.f. modular construction, without supposing that a faithful state was supplied.<\/p>\n","source":"docs/courses/OA-FLOW/src/OA-FLOW-DD.md","start_offset":0,"end_offset":6673,"role":"registered_statement_and_proof_passage"},"d7cfaec122f1b6410959969a8ec768dcbc1ebb17de9b3c289940af2ec320d8cb":{"markdown":"## The reconstruction map is unitary\n\nFor $\\xi\\in\\mathscr D$, define a $K$-valued field\n\n<a id=\"equation-m11\"><\/a>\n\n$$ (W\\xi)(s)=[U_s^*\\xi]. \\tag{M11} $$\n\nJoint continuity of the smoothed densities makes it a continuous field. Its image on each compact is compact in the metric space $K$, hence separable. It satisfies the local measurability convention of [lesson 44](OA-FLOW-L44.md) even when $K$ is not separable. Equations (M9)–(M10) give\n\n<a id=\"equation-m12\"><\/a>\n\n$$ (W\\xi)(sh)=\\chi(h)^{-1/2}V_h^*(W\\xi)(s), \\tag{M12} $$\n\nso it is a field of the induced representation from $V$. If $k_0$ is the cutoff from lesson 43, then (M7), (M3), and $Qk_0=1$ yield\n\n<a id=\"equation-m13\"><\/a>\n\n$$ \\|W\\xi\\|_{\\mathrm{ind}}^2\n   =\\int_G k_0(s)B(U_s^*\\xi,U_s^*\\xi)\\,ds\n   =\\int_G k_0(s)k_{\\xi,\\xi}(s)\\,ds\n   =\\|\\xi\\|_{\\mathcal L}^2. \\tag{M13} $$\n\nThe final equality follows first with $k_0(s)F(sH)$, $F\\in C_c(Y)_+$ and $F\\leq1$, by (M3). Take the supremum over $F$: the scalar positive measure of $\\pi$ has total mass $\\|\\xi\\|^2$ by QF5, and the cutoff identity is (I4) of lesson 44. This does not use a global countable exhaustion or arbitrary-net monotone convergence. Thus $W$ extends to an isometry $\\mathcal L\\to\\operatorname{Ind}_H^G K$. Directly from (M11), $WU_t=U_t^{\\mathrm{ind}}W$. The scalar representing measures from QF5 and (M3) give, for $F\\in C_c(Y)$ and $\\xi,\\eta\\in\\mathscr D$,\n\n<a id=\"equation-m14\"><\/a>\n\n$$ \\langle\\pi_{\\mathrm{ind}}(F)W\\xi,W\\eta\\rangle\n  =\\int_G k_0(s)F(sH)k_{\\xi,\\eta}(s)\\,ds\n  =\\langle\\pi(F)\\xi,\\eta\\rangle. \\tag{M14} $$\n\nThe compressed identity (M14) implies actual intertwining. Put $A=\\pi_{\\mathrm{ind}}(F)$. Apply (M14) also to $F^*F$; multiplicativity of both representations gives\n\n<a id=\"equation-mi1\"><\/a>\n\n\\[\n \\|AW\\xi-W\\pi(F)\\xi\\|^2\n =\\langle\\pi(F^*F)\\xi,\\xi\\rangle-\\|\\pi(F)\\xi\\|^2=0 .\n \\tag{MI1}\n\\]\nHere the mixed terms are evaluated with $W^*AW=\\pi(F)$, and $W$ is an isometry. Thus $W\\pi(F)=\\pi_{\\mathrm{ind}}(F)W$; continuity extends this to $C_0(Y)$.\n\nIt remains to show that $W$ is onto. Choose countably many tests only on one compact at a time. Let $\\theta$ be an induced field and fix a compact $C$. Choose a separable closed subspace $E_C$ containing $\\theta(s)$ for almost every $s\\in C$, and a countable dense family $(v_j)$ in $E_C$. For every $s\\in C$, the set $\\{[U_s^*\\xi]:\\xi\\in\\mathscr D\\}$ is dense in $K$, since $U_s^*\\mathscr D=\\mathscr D$. Given $j,n,s$, choose $\\xi_{s,j,n}$ with $\\|[U_s^*\\xi_{s,j,n}]-v_j\\|<1/(2n)$. Continuity gives a neighborhood of $s$ where the distance is less than $1/n$. Finitely many such neighborhoods cover $C$.\n\nCollect their selected vectors for every pair $j,n$ into a countable subset $\\mathscr D_C\\subseteq\\mathscr D$. It satisfies\n\n<a id=\"equation-m15\"><\/a>\n\n$$ \\inf_{\\xi\\in\\mathscr D_C}\n       \\|[U_s^*\\xi]-v_j\\|=0\n       \\qquad(s\\in C,\\ j\\geq1). \\tag{M15} $$\n\nNeither $G$, $\\mathcal L$ nor $K$ has been assumed separable. Compactness of $C$, continuity of the smoothed fields and the local separable range of this one $\\theta$ give precisely the countable tests needed.\n\nIf $\\theta$ is orthogonal to the range of $W$, it is also orthogonal to every $\\pi_{\\mathrm{ind}}(Qf)W\\xi$. The induced pairing of lesson 44 and (M11) then imply\n\n<a id=\"equation-m16\"><\/a>\n\n$$ \\int_G f(s)\\langle[U_s^*\\xi],\\theta(s)\\rangle\\,ds=0\n      \\qquad(f\\in C_c(G),\\ \\xi\\in\\mathscr D). \\tag{M16} $$\n\nFor each $\\xi\\in\\mathscr D_C$, its locally integrable coefficient in (M16) vanishes locally almost everywhere. Remove their countable union of exceptional sets on $C$, along with the exception for $\\theta(s)\\in E_C$. At a remaining $s$, (M15) gives $\\langle v_j,\\theta(s)\\rangle=0$ for every $j$. Since $\\theta(s)\\in E_C$, it follows that $\\theta(s)=0$. This holds almost everywhere on each compact, so $\\theta$ is locally zero and has zero induced norm by (I3) of lesson 44. The range of the isometry $W$ is closed and has zero orthogonal complement. Therefore $W$ is surjective and is a unitary equivalence of covariant systems.","html":"<h2>The reconstruction map is unitary<\/h2>\n<p>For <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\xi\\in\\mathscr D\\)\">\\(\\xi\\in\\mathscr D\\)<\/span>, define a <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(K\\)\">\\(K\\)<\/span>-valued field<\/p>\n<p><span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[ (W\\xi)(s)=[U_s^*\\xi]. \\tag{M11} \\]\">\\[ (W\\xi)(s)=[U_s^*\\xi]. \\tag{M11} \\]<\/span><\/p>\n<p>Joint continuity of the smoothed densities makes it a continuous field. Its image on each compact is compact in the metric space <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(K\\)\">\\(K\\)<\/span>, hence separable. It satisfies the local measurability convention of <a href=\"../courses/OA-FLOW/OA-FLOW-L44.html\">lesson 44<\/a> even when <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(K\\)\">\\(K\\)<\/span> is not separable. Equations (M9)–(M10) give<\/p>\n<p><span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[ (W\\xi)(sh)=\\chi(h)^{-1/2}V_h^*(W\\xi)(s), \\tag{M12} \\]\">\\[ (W\\xi)(sh)=\\chi(h)^{-1/2}V_h^*(W\\xi)(s), \\tag{M12} \\]<\/span><\/p>\n<p>so it is a field of the induced representation from <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(V\\)\">\\(V\\)<\/span>. If <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(k_0\\)\">\\(k_0\\)<\/span> is the cutoff from lesson 43, then (M7), (M3), and <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(Qk_0=1\\)\">\\(Qk_0=1\\)<\/span> yield<\/p>\n<p><span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[ \\|W\\xi\\|_{\\mathrm{ind}}^2\n   =\\int_G k_0(s)B(U_s^*\\xi,U_s^*\\xi)\\,ds\n   =\\int_G k_0(s)k_{\\xi,\\xi}(s)\\,ds\n   =\\|\\xi\\|_{\\mathcal L}^2. \\tag{M13} \\]\">\\[ \\|W\\xi\\|_{\\mathrm{ind}}^2\n   =\\int_G k_0(s)B(U_s^*\\xi,U_s^*\\xi)\\,ds\n   =\\int_G k_0(s)k_{\\xi,\\xi}(s)\\,ds\n   =\\|\\xi\\|_{\\mathcal L}^2. \\tag{M13} \\]<\/span><\/p>\n<p>The final equality follows first with <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(k_0(s)F(sH)\\)\">\\(k_0(s)F(sH)\\)<\/span>, <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(F\\in C_c(Y)_+\\)\">\\(F\\in C_c(Y)_+\\)<\/span> and <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(F\\leq1\\)\">\\(F\\leq1\\)<\/span>, by (M3). Take the supremum over <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(F\\)\">\\(F\\)<\/span>: the scalar positive measure of <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\pi\\)\">\\(\\pi\\)<\/span> has total mass <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\|\\xi\\|^2\\)\">\\(\\|\\xi\\|^2\\)<\/span> by QF5, and the cutoff identity is (I4) of lesson 44. This does not use a global countable exhaustion or arbitrary-net monotone convergence. Thus <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(W\\)\">\\(W\\)<\/span> extends to an isometry <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\mathcal L\\to\\operatorname{Ind}_H^G K\\)\">\\(\\mathcal L\\to\\operatorname{Ind}_H^G K\\)<\/span>. Directly from (M11), <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(WU_t=U_t^{\\mathrm{ind}}W\\)\">\\(WU_t=U_t^{\\mathrm{ind}}W\\)<\/span>. The scalar representing measures from QF5 and (M3) give, for <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(F\\in C_c(Y)\\)\">\\(F\\in C_c(Y)\\)<\/span> and <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\xi,\\eta\\in\\mathscr D\\)\">\\(\\xi,\\eta\\in\\mathscr D\\)<\/span>,<\/p>\n<p><span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[ \\langle\\pi_{\\mathrm{ind}}(F)W\\xi,W\\eta\\rangle\n  =\\int_G k_0(s)F(sH)k_{\\xi,\\eta}(s)\\,ds\n  =\\langle\\pi(F)\\xi,\\eta\\rangle. \\tag{M14} \\]\">\\[ \\langle\\pi_{\\mathrm{ind}}(F)W\\xi,W\\eta\\rangle\n  =\\int_G k_0(s)F(sH)k_{\\xi,\\eta}(s)\\,ds\n  =\\langle\\pi(F)\\xi,\\eta\\rangle. \\tag{M14} \\]<\/span><\/p>\n<p>The compressed identity (M14) implies actual intertwining. Put <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(A=\\pi_{\\mathrm{ind}}(F)\\)\">\\(A=\\pi_{\\mathrm{ind}}(F)\\)<\/span>. Apply (M14) also to <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(F^*F\\)\">\\(F^*F\\)<\/span>; multiplicativity of both representations gives<\/p>\n<p><span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n \\|AW\\xi-W\\pi(F)\\xi\\|^2\n =\\langle\\pi(F^*F)\\xi,\\xi\\rangle-\\|\\pi(F)\\xi\\|^2=0 .\n \\tag{MI1}\n\\]\">\\[\n \\|AW\\xi-W\\pi(F)\\xi\\|^2\n =\\langle\\pi(F^*F)\\xi,\\xi\\rangle-\\|\\pi(F)\\xi\\|^2=0 .\n \\tag{MI1}\n\\]<\/span>\nHere the mixed terms are evaluated with <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(W^*AW=\\pi(F)\\)\">\\(W^*AW=\\pi(F)\\)<\/span>, and <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(W\\)\">\\(W\\)<\/span> is an isometry. Thus <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(W\\pi(F)=\\pi_{\\mathrm{ind}}(F)W\\)\">\\(W\\pi(F)=\\pi_{\\mathrm{ind}}(F)W\\)<\/span>; continuity extends this to <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(C_0(Y)\\)\">\\(C_0(Y)\\)<\/span>.<\/p>\n<p>It remains to show that <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(W\\)\">\\(W\\)<\/span> is onto. Choose countably many tests only on one compact at a time. Let <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\theta\\)\">\\(\\theta\\)<\/span> be an induced field and fix a compact <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(C\\)\">\\(C\\)<\/span>. Choose a separable closed subspace <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(E_C\\)\">\\(E_C\\)<\/span> containing <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\theta(s)\\)\">\\(\\theta(s)\\)<\/span> for almost every <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(s\\in C\\)\">\\(s\\in C\\)<\/span>, and a countable dense family <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\((v_j)\\)\">\\((v_j)\\)<\/span> in <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(E_C\\)\">\\(E_C\\)<\/span>. For every <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(s\\in C\\)\">\\(s\\in C\\)<\/span>, the set <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\{[U_s^*\\xi]:\\xi\\in\\mathscr D\\}\\)\">\\(\\{[U_s^*\\xi]:\\xi\\in\\mathscr D\\}\\)<\/span> is dense in <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(K\\)\">\\(K\\)<\/span>, since <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(U_s^*\\mathscr D=\\mathscr D\\)\">\\(U_s^*\\mathscr D=\\mathscr D\\)<\/span>. Given <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(j,n,s\\)\">\\(j,n,s\\)<\/span>, choose <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\xi_{s,j,n}\\)\">\\(\\xi_{s,j,n}\\)<\/span> with <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\|[U_s^*\\xi_{s,j,n}]-v_j\\|&lt;1/(2n)\\)\">\\(\\|[U_s^*\\xi_{s,j,n}]-v_j\\|&lt;1/(2n)\\)<\/span>. Continuity gives a neighborhood of <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(s\\)\">\\(s\\)<\/span> where the distance is less than <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(1/n\\)\">\\(1/n\\)<\/span>. Finitely many such neighborhoods cover <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(C\\)\">\\(C\\)<\/span>.<\/p>\n<p>Collect their selected vectors for every pair <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(j,n\\)\">\\(j,n\\)<\/span> into a countable subset <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\mathscr D_C\\subseteq\\mathscr D\\)\">\\(\\mathscr D_C\\subseteq\\mathscr D\\)<\/span>. It satisfies<\/p>\n<p><span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[ \\inf_{\\xi\\in\\mathscr D_C}\n       \\|[U_s^*\\xi]-v_j\\|=0\n       \\qquad(s\\in C,\\ j\\geq1). \\tag{M15} \\]\">\\[ \\inf_{\\xi\\in\\mathscr D_C}\n       \\|[U_s^*\\xi]-v_j\\|=0\n       \\qquad(s\\in C,\\ j\\geq1). \\tag{M15} \\]<\/span><\/p>\n<p>Neither <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(G\\)\">\\(G\\)<\/span>, <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\mathcal L\\)\">\\(\\mathcal L\\)<\/span> nor <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(K\\)\">\\(K\\)<\/span> has been assumed separable. Compactness of <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(C\\)\">\\(C\\)<\/span>, continuity of the smoothed fields and the local separable range of this one <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\theta\\)\">\\(\\theta\\)<\/span> give precisely the countable tests needed.<\/p>\n<p>If <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\theta\\)\">\\(\\theta\\)<\/span> is orthogonal to the range of <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(W\\)\">\\(W\\)<\/span>, it is also orthogonal to every <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\pi_{\\mathrm{ind}}(Qf)W\\xi\\)\">\\(\\pi_{\\mathrm{ind}}(Qf)W\\xi\\)<\/span>. The induced pairing of lesson 44 and (M11) then imply<\/p>\n<p><span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[ \\int_G f(s)\\langle[U_s^*\\xi],\\theta(s)\\rangle\\,ds=0\n      \\qquad(f\\in C_c(G),\\ \\xi\\in\\mathscr D). \\tag{M16} \\]\">\\[ \\int_G f(s)\\langle[U_s^*\\xi],\\theta(s)\\rangle\\,ds=0\n      \\qquad(f\\in C_c(G),\\ \\xi\\in\\mathscr D). \\tag{M16} \\]<\/span><\/p>\n<p>For each <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\xi\\in\\mathscr D_C\\)\">\\(\\xi\\in\\mathscr D_C\\)<\/span>, its locally integrable coefficient in (M16) vanishes locally almost everywhere. Remove their countable union of exceptional sets on <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(C\\)\">\\(C\\)<\/span>, along with the exception for <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\theta(s)\\in E_C\\)\">\\(\\theta(s)\\in E_C\\)<\/span>. At a remaining <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(s\\)\">\\(s\\)<\/span>, (M15) gives <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\langle v_j,\\theta(s)\\rangle=0\\)\">\\(\\langle v_j,\\theta(s)\\rangle=0\\)<\/span> for every <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(j\\)\">\\(j\\)<\/span>. Since <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\theta(s)\\in E_C\\)\">\\(\\theta(s)\\in E_C\\)<\/span>, it follows that <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\theta(s)=0\\)\">\\(\\theta(s)=0\\)<\/span>. This holds almost everywhere on each compact, so <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\theta\\)\">\\(\\theta\\)<\/span> is locally zero and has zero induced norm by (I3) of lesson 44. The range of the isometry <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(W\\)\">\\(W\\)<\/span> is closed and has zero orthogonal complement. Therefore <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(W\\)\">\\(W\\)<\/span> is surjective and is a unitary equivalence of covariant systems.<\/p>\n","source":"docs/courses/OA-FLOW/src/OA-FLOW-L45.md","start_offset":7092,"end_offset":11097,"role":"registered_statement_and_proof_passage"},"d7e59ad5d990b5e7e4222964e828ffb4069eaf69836f426d640f8f134be8505d":{"markdown":"<a id=\"oa-flow.indrec.evaluation-normality\"><\/a>\n### 6. Normality cannot be assumed from evaluation: prove it\n\nFix \\(f\\in C_c(G)\\) and define\n\\[\nS_f:N\\longrightarrow N_2,\\qquad S_f(a)=(\\Theta\\mathcal F_f(a))(e).\n\\tag{RU19}\n\\]\nFor a relatively compact identity neighborhood \\(V\\), choose \\(g_V\\in C_c(G)_+\\), supported in \\(V\\), with integral one. The slice\n\\[\nL_V(y)=(\\mathrm{id}\\otimes g_V)(y)\n\\]\nis normal by [the slice construction IW14–IW15](OA-FLOW-IW.md#iw-2). Thus \\(L_V\\Theta\\mathcal F_f:N\\to N_2\\) is normal. For \\(y=\\Theta\\mathcal F_f(a)\\), (RU6) and (RU11) give\n\\[\n\\|y(s)-y(e)\\|\n\\le\\|\\alpha'_{s^{-1}}y-y\\|\n\\le\\|a\\|\\delta_f(s^{-1}).\n\\]\nConsequently\n\\[\n\\|L_V\\Theta\\mathcal F_f-S_f\\|\n\\le\\sup_{s\\in V}\\delta_f(s^{-1})\\longrightarrow0.\n\\tag{RU20}\n\\]\nFor each \\(\\omega'\\in (N_2)_*\\), its pullbacks along these normal maps converge in functional norm to \\(\\omega'\\circ S_f\\). CP6 proves that this limit is normal. Hence \\(S_f\\) is normal. This is uniform approximation of evaluation on the indicated bounded family; it does not assert normality of unrestricted point evaluation.\n\nIf \\(a\\in E\\) and \\(k=f|_H\\), equations (RU13) and (RU17) yield\n\\[\nS_f(a)=\\varphi(\\beta_k(a))=\\beta'_k(\\varphi(a)).\n\\tag{RU21}\n\\]\nThe last identity is legitimate in operator norm: the compactly supported orbit integral of \\(a\\in E\\) is a Banach-space integral, and the bounded intertwiner \\(\\varphi\\) passes through it.","html":"<h3>6. Normality cannot be assumed from evaluation: prove it<\/h3>\n<p>Fix <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(f\\in C_c(G)\\)\">\\(f\\in C_c(G)\\)<\/span> and define\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\nS_f:N\\longrightarrow N_2,\\qquad S_f(a)=(\\Theta\\mathcal F_f(a))(e).\n\\tag{RU19}\n\\]\">\\[\nS_f:N\\longrightarrow N_2,\\qquad S_f(a)=(\\Theta\\mathcal F_f(a))(e).\n\\tag{RU19}\n\\]<\/span>\nFor a relatively compact identity neighborhood <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(V\\)\">\\(V\\)<\/span>, choose <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(g_V\\in C_c(G)_+\\)\">\\(g_V\\in C_c(G)_+\\)<\/span>, supported in <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(V\\)\">\\(V\\)<\/span>, with integral one. The slice\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\nL_V(y)=(\\mathrm{id}\\otimes g_V)(y)\n\\]\">\\[\nL_V(y)=(\\mathrm{id}\\otimes g_V)(y)\n\\]<\/span>\nis normal by <a href=\"../courses/OA-FLOW/OA-FLOW-IW.html#iw-2\">the slice construction IW14–IW15<\/a>. Thus <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(L_V\\Theta\\mathcal F_f:N\\to N_2\\)\">\\(L_V\\Theta\\mathcal F_f:N\\to N_2\\)<\/span> is normal. For <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(y=\\Theta\\mathcal F_f(a)\\)\">\\(y=\\Theta\\mathcal F_f(a)\\)<\/span>, (RU6) and (RU11) give\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n\\|y(s)-y(e)\\|\n\\le\\|\\alpha&#x27;_{s^{-1}}y-y\\|\n\\le\\|a\\|\\delta_f(s^{-1}).\n\\]\">\\[\n\\|y(s)-y(e)\\|\n\\le\\|\\alpha&#x27;_{s^{-1}}y-y\\|\n\\le\\|a\\|\\delta_f(s^{-1}).\n\\]<\/span>\nConsequently\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n\\|L_V\\Theta\\mathcal F_f-S_f\\|\n\\le\\sup_{s\\in V}\\delta_f(s^{-1})\\longrightarrow0.\n\\tag{RU20}\n\\]\">\\[\n\\|L_V\\Theta\\mathcal F_f-S_f\\|\n\\le\\sup_{s\\in V}\\delta_f(s^{-1})\\longrightarrow0.\n\\tag{RU20}\n\\]<\/span>\nFor each <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\omega&#x27;\\in (N_2)_*\\)\">\\(\\omega&#x27;\\in (N_2)_*\\)<\/span>, its pullbacks along these normal maps converge in functional norm to <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\omega&#x27;\\circ S_f\\)\">\\(\\omega&#x27;\\circ S_f\\)<\/span>. CP6 proves that this limit is normal. Hence <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(S_f\\)\">\\(S_f\\)<\/span> is normal. This is uniform approximation of evaluation on the indicated bounded family; it does not assert normality of unrestricted point evaluation.<\/p>\n<p>If <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(a\\in E\\)\">\\(a\\in E\\)<\/span> and <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(k=f|_H\\)\">\\(k=f|_H\\)<\/span>, equations (RU13) and (RU17) yield\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\nS_f(a)=\\varphi(\\beta_k(a))=\\beta&#x27;_k(\\varphi(a)).\n\\tag{RU21}\n\\]\">\\[\nS_f(a)=\\varphi(\\beta_k(a))=\\beta&#x27;_k(\\varphi(a)).\n\\tag{RU21}\n\\]<\/span>\nThe last identity is legitimate in operator norm: the compactly supported orbit integral of <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(a\\in E\\)\">\\(a\\in E\\)<\/span> is a Banach-space integral, and the bounded intertwiner <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\varphi\\)\">\\(\\varphi\\)<\/span> passes through it.<\/p>\n","source":"docs/courses/OA-FLOW/src/OA-FLOW-L47.md","start_offset":19539,"end_offset":20942,"role":"registered_statement_and_proof_passage"},"d744e3b7715528b286864dfd874b25fffec4a8d0732249ce93ac7be2e692f082":{"markdown":"<a id=\"isys-separable-matrices\"><\/a>\n### Decomposability from countable matrices\n\nFirst recall an elementary scalar fact. If \\(T\\in B(L^2(Y))\\) commutes with every scalar multiplier, partition \\(Y\\) into countably many finite-measure sets \\(E_n\\). On \\(E_n\\), put \\(b_n=T1_{E_n}\\). For a bounded function \\(f\\) supported there, commutation gives \\(Tf=fb_n\\). Testing indicators of the sets where \\( |b_n|>\\|T\\|+\\varepsilon\\) shows \\( |b_n|\\le\\|T\\|\\) almost everywhere. The functions \\(b_n\\) combine to a bounded measurable \\(b\\), and density of simple functions gives \\(T=M_b\\). This is the finite-partition proof in [IS4, immediately before (IS25)](OA-FLOW-IS.md#is-4); its argument applies to any sigma-finite measure space.\n\nNow let \\(T\\in B(L^2(Y;K))\\) commute with all scalar multipliers. Choose an orthonormal basis \\((e_j)\\) of the separable \\(K\\). Every matrix entry \\(T_{ij}\\) is a scalar multiplier \\(M_{f_{ij}}\\). For a finite vector \\(u\\) with rational complex coordinates and each finite number \\(r\\) of output coordinates, the operator bound, tested on \\(1_Eu\\) for finite-measure \\(E\\), implies\n\n\\[\n \\sum_{i\\le r}\\left|\\sum_j f_{ij}(y)u_j\\right|^2\n \\le \\|T\\|^2\\|u\\|^2\n \\quad\\text{for almost every }y.\n \\tag{SC5}\n\\]\n\nA positive-measure violation would have a positive finite-measure subset and contradict the same integrated inequality. There are countably many tests in (SC5). Remove their common null exception. The resulting matrix extends uniquely to an operator \\(X(y)\\in B(K)\\) with \\(\\|X(y)\\|\\le\\|T\\|\\); set it equal to zero on that exception. Its matrix coefficients are measurable, and finite sums of simple vector functions show\n\n\\[\n (T\\eta)(y)=X(y)\\eta(y).\n \\tag{SC6}\n\\]\n\nCountably many coefficients also prove uniqueness almost everywhere. Conversely any such bounded measurable matrix field defines a bounded operator by (SC6). Measurability of its action on vectors follows by finite-coordinate approximation. Its operator norm is the essential supremum of the fibre norms, by the same finite-vector tests. Finite basis compressions show that these operators form \\(B(K)\\bar\\otimes L^\\infty(Y)\\): each compression is a finite matrix of scalar multipliers, and the compressions converge strongly to the operator.\n\nSince the quotient algebra is central in \\(M\\), every element of \\(M\\) has this description in the coordinates (SC2). Its corresponding field on \\(G\\) is\n\n\\[\n \\widetilde X(s)=V_{h(s)}^*X(q(s))V_{h(s)}.\n \\tag{SC7}\n\\]\n\nThis formula is well defined on almost-everywhere classes by (SC1). It has the exact right-subgroup covariance \\(\\widetilde X(sh)=V_h^*\\widetilde X(s)V_h\\), and intertwining with induced left translation gives the field of \\(\\alpha_t(X)\\) as \\(\\widetilde X(t^{-1}s)\\).\n\nThe field map (SC7) is a faithful normal unital *-homomorphism into \\(B(K)\\bar\\otimes L^\\infty(G)\\). Indeed [IS2's full coordinate unitary](OA-FLOW-IS.md#is-2) identifies it with amplification \\(X\\mapsto X\\otimes1_{L^2(H)}\\), followed by conjugation by the measurable unitary field \\(V_h\\). Amplification is normal: on finite sums of elementary tensor vectors, every vector coefficient pulls back to a finite sum of normal vector coefficients of \\(X\\). Approximation of arbitrary vectors and the uniform operator bound give convergence of those pullbacks in functional norm. The predual norm closure and vector-series test of [CP4–6](OA-FLOW-CP.md#oa-flow.cp.6) give normality on the whole algebra. It is faithful since \\(L^2(H)\\ne0\\). The unitary conjugations preserve these properties. This proves the needed normality without a theorem on fields of von Neumann algebras.","html":"<h3>Decomposability from countable matrices<\/h3>\n<p>First recall an elementary scalar fact. If <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(T\\in B(L^2(Y))\\)\">\\(T\\in B(L^2(Y))\\)<\/span> commutes with every scalar multiplier, partition <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(Y\\)\">\\(Y\\)<\/span> into countably many finite-measure sets <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(E_n\\)\">\\(E_n\\)<\/span>. On <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(E_n\\)\">\\(E_n\\)<\/span>, put <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(b_n=T1_{E_n}\\)\">\\(b_n=T1_{E_n}\\)<\/span>. For a bounded function <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(f\\)\">\\(f\\)<\/span> supported there, commutation gives <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(Tf=fb_n\\)\">\\(Tf=fb_n\\)<\/span>. Testing indicators of the sets where <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\( |b_n|&gt;\\|T\\|+\\varepsilon\\)\">\\( |b_n|&gt;\\|T\\|+\\varepsilon\\)<\/span> shows <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\( |b_n|\\le\\|T\\|\\)\">\\( |b_n|\\le\\|T\\|\\)<\/span> almost everywhere. The functions <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(b_n\\)\">\\(b_n\\)<\/span> combine to a bounded measurable <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(b\\)\">\\(b\\)<\/span>, and density of simple functions gives <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(T=M_b\\)\">\\(T=M_b\\)<\/span>. This is the finite-partition proof in <a href=\"../courses/OA-FLOW/OA-FLOW-IS.html#is-4\">IS4, immediately before (IS25)<\/a>; its argument applies to any sigma-finite measure space.<\/p>\n<p>Now let <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(T\\in B(L^2(Y;K))\\)\">\\(T\\in B(L^2(Y;K))\\)<\/span> commute with all scalar multipliers. Choose an orthonormal basis <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\((e_j)\\)\">\\((e_j)\\)<\/span> of the separable <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(K\\)\">\\(K\\)<\/span>. Every matrix entry <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(T_{ij}\\)\">\\(T_{ij}\\)<\/span> is a scalar multiplier <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(M_{f_{ij}}\\)\">\\(M_{f_{ij}}\\)<\/span>. For a finite vector <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(u\\)\">\\(u\\)<\/span> with rational complex coordinates and each finite number <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(r\\)\">\\(r\\)<\/span> of output coordinates, the operator bound, tested on <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(1_Eu\\)\">\\(1_Eu\\)<\/span> for finite-measure <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(E\\)\">\\(E\\)<\/span>, implies<\/p>\n<p><span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n \\sum_{i\\le r}\\left|\\sum_j f_{ij}(y)u_j\\right|^2\n \\le \\|T\\|^2\\|u\\|^2\n \\quad\\text{for almost every }y.\n \\tag{SC5}\n\\]\">\\[\n \\sum_{i\\le r}\\left|\\sum_j f_{ij}(y)u_j\\right|^2\n \\le \\|T\\|^2\\|u\\|^2\n \\quad\\text{for almost every }y.\n \\tag{SC5}\n\\]<\/span><\/p>\n<p>A positive-measure violation would have a positive finite-measure subset and contradict the same integrated inequality. There are countably many tests in (SC5). Remove their common null exception. The resulting matrix extends uniquely to an operator <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(X(y)\\in B(K)\\)\">\\(X(y)\\in B(K)\\)<\/span> with <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\|X(y)\\|\\le\\|T\\|\\)\">\\(\\|X(y)\\|\\le\\|T\\|\\)<\/span>; set it equal to zero on that exception. Its matrix coefficients are measurable, and finite sums of simple vector functions show<\/p>\n<p><span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n (T\\eta)(y)=X(y)\\eta(y).\n \\tag{SC6}\n\\]\">\\[\n (T\\eta)(y)=X(y)\\eta(y).\n \\tag{SC6}\n\\]<\/span><\/p>\n<p>Countably many coefficients also prove uniqueness almost everywhere. Conversely any such bounded measurable matrix field defines a bounded operator by (SC6). Measurability of its action on vectors follows by finite-coordinate approximation. Its operator norm is the essential supremum of the fibre norms, by the same finite-vector tests. Finite basis compressions show that these operators form <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(B(K)\\bar\\otimes L^\\infty(Y)\\)\">\\(B(K)\\bar\\otimes L^\\infty(Y)\\)<\/span>: each compression is a finite matrix of scalar multipliers, and the compressions converge strongly to the operator.<\/p>\n<p>Since the quotient algebra is central in <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(M\\)\">\\(M\\)<\/span>, every element of <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(M\\)\">\\(M\\)<\/span> has this description in the coordinates (SC2). Its corresponding field on <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(G\\)\">\\(G\\)<\/span> is<\/p>\n<p><span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n \\widetilde X(s)=V_{h(s)}^*X(q(s))V_{h(s)}.\n \\tag{SC7}\n\\]\">\\[\n \\widetilde X(s)=V_{h(s)}^*X(q(s))V_{h(s)}.\n \\tag{SC7}\n\\]<\/span><\/p>\n<p>This formula is well defined on almost-everywhere classes by (SC1). It has the exact right-subgroup covariance <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\widetilde X(sh)=V_h^*\\widetilde X(s)V_h\\)\">\\(\\widetilde X(sh)=V_h^*\\widetilde X(s)V_h\\)<\/span>, and intertwining with induced left translation gives the field of <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\alpha_t(X)\\)\">\\(\\alpha_t(X)\\)<\/span> as <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\widetilde X(t^{-1}s)\\)\">\\(\\widetilde X(t^{-1}s)\\)<\/span>.<\/p>\n<p>The field map (SC7) is a faithful normal unital *-homomorphism into <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(B(K)\\bar\\otimes L^\\infty(G)\\)\">\\(B(K)\\bar\\otimes L^\\infty(G)\\)<\/span>. Indeed <a href=\"../courses/OA-FLOW/OA-FLOW-IS.html#is-2\">IS2's full coordinate unitary<\/a> identifies it with amplification <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(X\\mapsto X\\otimes1_{L^2(H)}\\)\">\\(X\\mapsto X\\otimes1_{L^2(H)}\\)<\/span>, followed by conjugation by the measurable unitary field <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(V_h\\)\">\\(V_h\\)<\/span>. Amplification is normal: on finite sums of elementary tensor vectors, every vector coefficient pulls back to a finite sum of normal vector coefficients of <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(X\\)\">\\(X\\)<\/span>. Approximation of arbitrary vectors and the uniform operator bound give convergence of those pullbacks in functional norm. The predual norm closure and vector-series test of <a href=\"../courses/OA-FLOW/OA-FLOW-CP.html#oa-flow.cp.6\">CP4–6<\/a> give normality on the whole algebra. It is faithful since <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(L^2(H)\\ne0\\)\">\\(L^2(H)\\ne0\\)<\/span>. The unitary conjugations preserve these properties. This proves the needed normality without a theorem on fields of von Neumann algebras.<\/p>\n","source":"docs/courses/OA-FLOW/src/OA-FLOW-L47.md","start_offset":30433,"end_offset":34034,"role":"registered_statement_and_proof_passage"},"d777f7da253296c1c9601f09d7e4f6afb7b647d21394256ff024373c54ef7acb":{"markdown":"<a id=\"oa-flow.compactcomm.example\"><\/a>\n## A finite kernel shows the displacement\n\nTake \\(H=S_3=\\langle a,b:a^3=b^2=e,\\ bab=a^{-1}\\rangle\\), \\(N=\\mathbb C\\), and normalized Haar measure \\(1/6\\) per element. The vectors \\(e_x=\\sqrt6\\,1_{\\{x\\}}\\) are orthonormal. For \\(Q=|e_e\\rangle\\langle e_a|\\),\n\n\\[\n R_r Q R_r^*=|e_{r^{-1}}\\rangle\\langle e_{ar^{-1}}|,\n \\qquad\n \\mathcal E(Q)=\\frac16\\sum_{r\\in S_3}\n              |e_{r^{-1}}\\rangle\\langle e_{ar^{-1}}|\n             =\\frac16 L_{a^{-1}}.\n \\tag{C12}\n\\]\n\nEach surviving matrix position has row \\(s=r^{-1}\\), column \\(t=ar^{-1}\\), and hence \\(st^{-1}=a^{-1}\\). In contrast, \\(t^{-1}s=ra^{-1}r^{-1}\\) changes with \\(r\\); the noncommutativity is visible. Formula (C7) gives \\(a_h=1_{\\{a^{-1}\\}}(h)\\), whose normalized integral (C9) is exactly \\(\\frac16L_{a^{-1}}\\).\n\n![Right-translation averaging of a rank-one operator on the six elements of S3.](../assets/induction-alternatives/compact/compact-kernel-averaging.svg)\n\n*Figure 52.1. Exact matrices in the orthonormal basis \\(e,a,a^2,b,ab,a^2b\\). The rank-one matrix has one entry equal to \\(1\\). Averaging its six right-translation conjugates places \\(1/6\\) at each of the six positions with \\(st^{-1}=a^{-1}\\), giving \\(\\frac16L_{a^{-1}}\\). This is the scalar finite instance of the kernel identity (C6)–(C10), with the Haar normalization included.*\n\n**Problem.** Why does the integrated coefficient use \\(h=st^{-1}\\) rather than \\(t^{-1}s\\)?\n\n**Solution.** The regular group operator acts by \\(\\lambda_h\\xi(s)=\\xi(h^{-1}s)\\). Its input coordinate is therefore \\(t=h^{-1}s\\), which gives \\(h=st^{-1}\\). That quantity is unchanged by simultaneous right translation of \\(s,t\\); the finite example shows that \\(t^{-1}s\\) need not be unchanged.\n\n**Problem.** Does the average in (C5) establish the same proof for a noncompact subgroup?\n\n**Solution.** No normalized finite Haar measure is available there. The argument proving (CE2) as a bounded preadjoint uses total mass one. The noncompact equality has the separate proof linked after (C11).\n\nThe classical fixed-algebra identification occurs in Masamichi Takesaki, [*Theory of Operator Algebras II*](https://doi.org/10.1007/978-3-662-10451-4), proof of Theorem X.4.12. The compact kernel-averaging proof above gives its own normal expectation, coefficient formula and density argument. Cited works retain their own rights.","html":"<h2>A finite kernel shows the displacement<\/h2>\n<p>Take <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(H=S_3=\\langle a,b:a^3=b^2=e,\\ bab=a^{-1}\\rangle\\)\">\\(H=S_3=\\langle a,b:a^3=b^2=e,\\ bab=a^{-1}\\rangle\\)<\/span>, <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(N=\\mathbb C\\)\">\\(N=\\mathbb C\\)<\/span>, and normalized Haar measure <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(1/6\\)\">\\(1/6\\)<\/span> per element. The vectors <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(e_x=\\sqrt6\\,1_{\\{x\\}}\\)\">\\(e_x=\\sqrt6\\,1_{\\{x\\}}\\)<\/span> are orthonormal. For <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(Q=|e_e\\rangle\\langle e_a|\\)\">\\(Q=|e_e\\rangle\\langle e_a|\\)<\/span>,<\/p>\n<p><span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n R_r Q R_r^*=|e_{r^{-1}}\\rangle\\langle e_{ar^{-1}}|,\n \\qquad\n \\mathcal E(Q)=\\frac16\\sum_{r\\in S_3}\n              |e_{r^{-1}}\\rangle\\langle e_{ar^{-1}}|\n             =\\frac16 L_{a^{-1}}.\n \\tag{C12}\n\\]\">\\[\n R_r Q R_r^*=|e_{r^{-1}}\\rangle\\langle e_{ar^{-1}}|,\n \\qquad\n \\mathcal E(Q)=\\frac16\\sum_{r\\in S_3}\n              |e_{r^{-1}}\\rangle\\langle e_{ar^{-1}}|\n             =\\frac16 L_{a^{-1}}.\n \\tag{C12}\n\\]<\/span><\/p>\n<p>Each surviving matrix position has row <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(s=r^{-1}\\)\">\\(s=r^{-1}\\)<\/span>, column <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(t=ar^{-1}\\)\">\\(t=ar^{-1}\\)<\/span>, and hence <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(st^{-1}=a^{-1}\\)\">\\(st^{-1}=a^{-1}\\)<\/span>. In contrast, <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(t^{-1}s=ra^{-1}r^{-1}\\)\">\\(t^{-1}s=ra^{-1}r^{-1}\\)<\/span> changes with <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(r\\)\">\\(r\\)<\/span>; the noncommutativity is visible. Formula (C7) gives <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(a_h=1_{\\{a^{-1}\\}}(h)\\)\">\\(a_h=1_{\\{a^{-1}\\}}(h)\\)<\/span>, whose normalized integral (C9) is exactly <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\frac16L_{a^{-1}}\\)\">\\(\\frac16L_{a^{-1}}\\)<\/span>.<\/p>\n<p><a class=\"figure-link\" href=\"../courses/OA-FLOW/assets/induction-alternatives/compact/compact-kernel-averaging.svg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><img src=\"../courses/OA-FLOW/assets/induction-alternatives/compact/compact-kernel-averaging.svg\" alt=\"Right-translation averaging of a rank-one operator on the six elements of S3.\" /><span>Open figure at full size<\/span><\/a><\/p>\n<p><em>Figure 52.1. Exact matrices in the orthonormal basis <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(e,a,a^2,b,ab,a^2b\\)\">\\(e,a,a^2,b,ab,a^2b\\)<\/span>. The rank-one matrix has one entry equal to <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(1\\)\">\\(1\\)<\/span>. Averaging its six right-translation conjugates places <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(1/6\\)\">\\(1/6\\)<\/span> at each of the six positions with <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(st^{-1}=a^{-1}\\)\">\\(st^{-1}=a^{-1}\\)<\/span>, giving <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\frac16L_{a^{-1}}\\)\">\\(\\frac16L_{a^{-1}}\\)<\/span>. This is the scalar finite instance of the kernel identity (C6)–(C10), with the Haar normalization included.<\/em><\/p>\n<p><strong>Problem.<\/strong> Why does the integrated coefficient use <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(h=st^{-1}\\)\">\\(h=st^{-1}\\)<\/span> rather than <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(t^{-1}s\\)\">\\(t^{-1}s\\)<\/span>?<\/p>\n<p><strong>Solution.<\/strong> The regular group operator acts by <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\lambda_h\\xi(s)=\\xi(h^{-1}s)\\)\">\\(\\lambda_h\\xi(s)=\\xi(h^{-1}s)\\)<\/span>. Its input coordinate is therefore <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(t=h^{-1}s\\)\">\\(t=h^{-1}s\\)<\/span>, which gives <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(h=st^{-1}\\)\">\\(h=st^{-1}\\)<\/span>. That quantity is unchanged by simultaneous right translation of <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(s,t\\)\">\\(s,t\\)<\/span>; the finite example shows that <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(t^{-1}s\\)\">\\(t^{-1}s\\)<\/span> need not be unchanged.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Problem.<\/strong> Does the average in (C5) establish the same proof for a noncompact subgroup?<\/p>\n<p><strong>Solution.<\/strong> No normalized finite Haar measure is available there. The argument proving (CE2) as a bounded preadjoint uses total mass one. The noncompact equality has the separate proof linked after (C11).<\/p>\n<p>The classical fixed-algebra identification occurs in Masamichi Takesaki, <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1007/978-3-662-10451-4\"><em>Theory of Operator Algebras II<\/em><\/a>, proof of Theorem X.4.12. The compact kernel-averaging proof above gives its own normal expectation, coefficient formula and density argument. Cited works retain their own rights.<\/p>\n","source":"docs/courses/OA-FLOW/src/OA-FLOW-L52.md","start_offset":9457,"end_offset":11825,"role":"registered_statement_and_proof_passage"},"d71610f7654804489e1ea72071425a52479329f241e2738c3e5cae4566d1cb15":{"markdown":"<a id=\"sec-6\"><\/a>\n## 6. The section algebra is intrinsic and functorial\n\nSections 1–2 give the Banach space of integrable locally Lusin sections, modulo almost-everywhere equality. Sections 4–5 construct its convolution and involution in a faithful weight chart. We now show that these operations are independent of that chart, and that normal isomorphisms of algebras transport the entire Banach involutive algebra.\n\n**Changing the faithful weight.** Let \\(\\varphi,\\psi\\) be faithful normal semifinite weights on \\(M\\), and put\n\\(u(t)=[D\\varphi:D\\psi]_t\\).\nThe same section has coefficients\n\\[\n a^\\psi(t)=a^\\varphi(t)u(t).\n \\tag{SEC6.a}\n\\]\nThe [whole-space chart theorem](OA-FLOW-GRD.md#grd-5) makes this a homeomorphism of the Mackey total spaces, preserving the degree and the coefficient norm. It therefore preserves local Lusin measurability, integrability and the integral norm. It preserves almost-everywhere equality, with inverse supplied by the adjoint cocycle at the same parameter. Thus it identifies the entire Banach spaces constructed in the two charts.\n\nFix an output degree \\(t\\). Right multiplication by \\(u(t)\\) is now one fixed normal linear isometry on the coefficient algebra, so it commutes with the weak-star integral by Section 3. The [ordered graded product identity](OA-FLOW-GRD.md#grd-3) says pointwise in \\(s\\) that\n\\[\n \\begin{aligned}\n &[a(s)u(s)]\\,\\sigma_s^\\psi(b(t-s)u(t-s))\\\\\n &\\qquad=a(s)\\sigma_s^\\varphi(b(t-s))\n                   u(s)\\sigma_s^\\psi(u(t-s))\\\\\n &\\qquad=a(s)\\sigma_s^\\varphi(b(t-s))u(t).\n \\end{aligned}\n \\tag{SEC6.b}\n\\]\nThe last equality is the cocycle law with parameters \\(s,t-s\\). Integrating in \\(s\\) gives\n\\[\n (a^\\psi*_\\psi b^\\psi)(t)\n      =(a*_\\varphi b)(t)u(t).\n \\tag{SEC6.c}\n\\]\nThe scalar majorant is the same in both charts, because the coefficient norms are unchanged:\n\\(\\|a(s)u(s)\\|\\|b(t-s)u(t-s)\\|=\\|a(s)\\|\\|b(t-s)\\|\\).\nHence the single null set where this majorant has infinite integral can be used for both constructions. Formula (SEC6.c) is an equality of the almost-everywhere section classes.\n\nFor the involution, the cocycle law at \\(t,-t\\) gives\n\\(\\sigma_t^\\psi(u(-t)^*)=u(t)\\). Modular implementation then gives\n\\[\n \\begin{aligned}\n (a^\\psi)^{\\sharp_\\psi}(t)\n &=\\sigma_t^\\psi\\bigl((a(-t)u(-t))^*\\bigr)\\\\\n &=u(t)\\sigma_t^\\psi(a(-t)^*)\\\\\n &=\\sigma_t^\\varphi(a(-t)^*)u(t)\n   =a^{\\sharp_\\varphi}(t)u(t).\n \\end{aligned}\n \\tag{SEC6.d}\n\\]\nThus both operations agree under the coordinate transition. Their Banach-space and involution norms agree as well.\n\nEquivalently, in intrinsic notation their definitions are\n\\[\n (x*y)(t)=\\int_{\\mathbb R}^{M(t)}x(s)y(t-s)\\,ds,\n \\qquad x^\\sharp(t)=x(-t)^*.\n \\tag{SEC6.e}\n\\]\nThe superscript on the integral records its one fixed output fiber. The [canonical normal predual charts](OA-FLOW-GRD.md#grd-2) commute with this integral, so it is precisely the coefficient integral already constructed. The ordered cocycle chain rule makes changes through an intermediate faithful weight equal the direct change. Consequently (SEC6.e), the norm and the almost-everywhere quotient define one intrinsic Banach involutive algebra, denoted \\(\\Gamma^1(\\mathcal F(M))\\).\n\n**Transport under a normal isomorphism.** Let \\(f:M\\to N\\) be a normal \\(*\\)-isomorphism with normal inverse. The [graded functor](OA-FLOW-GRD.md#grd-6) gives, for every \\(t\\), a normal onto linear isometry\n\\[\n \\begin{aligned}\n F_t(f):M(t)&\\longrightarrow N(t),\\\\\n x\\varphi^{it}&\\longmapsto\n       f(x)(\\varphi\\circ f^{-1})^{it}.\n \\end{aligned}\n \\tag{SEC6.f}\n\\]\nIt preserves graded products and adjoints, and its total map is a Mackey homeomorphism preserving degree. Define on sections\n\\[\n [\\Gamma^1(f)x](t)=F_t(f)(x(t)).\n \\tag{SEC6.g}\n\\]\nA Lusin compact restriction for \\(x\\), followed by this total homeomorphism, is a Lusin compact restriction for its image. The inverse has the same property. Pointwise fiber isometry gives\n\\[\n \\|\\Gamma^1(f)x\\|_1=\\|x\\|_1.\n \\tag{SEC6.h}\n\\]\nThe maps preserve almost-everywhere equality. Their pointwise inverse is \\(F_t(f^{-1})\\), so (SEC6.g) induces an onto linear isometry of the complete section spaces.\n\nFor the product fix \\(t\\) outside the scalar norm-majorant exceptional set for \\(x,y\\). The same set works for their images, by (SEC6.h)'s pointwise norm equality. The output map \\(F_t(f)\\) is fixed while the integration variable \\(s\\) varies; its normality therefore gives\n\\[\n \\begin{aligned}\n [\\Gamma^1(f)(x*y)](t)\n &=F_t(f)\\!\\left(\\int^{M(t)}x(s)y(t-s)\\,ds\\right)\\\\\n &=\\int^{N(t)}F_t(f)(x(s)y(t-s))\\,ds\\\\\n &=\\int^{N(t)}\n       F_s(f)(x(s))F_{t-s}(f)(y(t-s))\\,ds\\\\\n &=[\\Gamma^1(f)x*\\Gamma^1(f)y](t).\n \\end{aligned}\n \\tag{SEC6.i}\n\\]\nThe third equality is the graded product identity, not a passage of a varying family of maps through an integral. Similarly, the graded adjoint identity gives\n\\[\n \\begin{aligned}\n [\\Gamma^1(f)(x^\\sharp)](t)\n &=F_t(f)(x(-t)^*)\\\\\n &=(F_{-t}(f)(x(-t)))^*\n   =[\\Gamma^1(f)x]^\\sharp(t).\n \\end{aligned}\n \\tag{SEC6.j}\n\\]\nThus \\(\\Gamma^1(f)\\) is an isometric \\(*\\)-isomorphism of Banach involutive algebras. In the \\(\\varphi\\) and \\(\\varphi\\circ f^{-1}\\) coefficient charts it is simply \\(a(t)\\mapsto f(a(t))\\). Chart independence of (SEC6.f) and of (SEC6.e) makes this description independent of the chosen faithful weight.\n\n**Exact identity and composition.** If \\(g:N\\to P\\) is another normal isomorphism, GRD6 proves\n\\(F_t(g)F_t(f)=F_t(g\\circ f)\\) for every degree. Applying this equality to each section value proves\n\\[\n \\begin{aligned}\n \\Gamma^1(g)\\Gamma^1(f)&=\\Gamma^1(g\\circ f),\\\\\n \\Gamma^1(\\operatorname{id}_M)\n      &=\\operatorname{id}_{\\Gamma^1(\\mathcal F(M))},\\\\\n \\Gamma^1(f)^{-1}&=\\Gamma^1(f^{-1}).\n \\end{aligned}\n \\tag{SEC6.k}\n\\]\nThese are exact equalities on the almost-everywhere classes. They introduce no scalar choice: the transported weights satisfy\n\\((\\varphi\\circ f^{-1})\\circ g^{-1}\n =\\varphi\\circ(g\\circ f)^{-1}\\).\nFor the zero algebra every section value is zero and \\(\\Gamma^1(\\mathcal F(0))=\\{0\\}\\); the unique maps satisfy the same formulas. We obtain a functor from arbitrary von Neumann algebras and normal isomorphisms to Banach involutive algebras and isometric \\(*\\)-isomorphisms.","html":"<h2>6. The section algebra is intrinsic and functorial<\/h2>\n<p>Sections 1–2 give the Banach space of integrable locally Lusin sections, modulo almost-everywhere equality. Sections 4–5 construct its convolution and involution in a faithful weight chart. We now show that these operations are independent of that chart, and that normal isomorphisms of algebras transport the entire Banach involutive algebra.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Changing the faithful weight.<\/strong> Let <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\varphi,\\psi\\)\">\\(\\varphi,\\psi\\)<\/span> be faithful normal semifinite weights on <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(M\\)\">\\(M\\)<\/span>, and put\n<span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(u(t)=[D\\varphi:D\\psi]_t\\)\">\\(u(t)=[D\\varphi:D\\psi]_t\\)<\/span>.\nThe same section has coefficients\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n a^\\psi(t)=a^\\varphi(t)u(t).\n \\tag{SEC6.a}\n\\]\">\\[\n a^\\psi(t)=a^\\varphi(t)u(t).\n \\tag{SEC6.a}\n\\]<\/span>\nThe <a href=\"../courses/OA-FLOW/OA-FLOW-GRD.html#grd-5\">whole-space chart theorem<\/a> makes this a homeomorphism of the Mackey total spaces, preserving the degree and the coefficient norm. It therefore preserves local Lusin measurability, integrability and the integral norm. It preserves almost-everywhere equality, with inverse supplied by the adjoint cocycle at the same parameter. Thus it identifies the entire Banach spaces constructed in the two charts.<\/p>\n<p>Fix an output degree <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(t\\)\">\\(t\\)<\/span>. Right multiplication by <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(u(t)\\)\">\\(u(t)\\)<\/span> is now one fixed normal linear isometry on the coefficient algebra, so it commutes with the weak-star integral by Section 3. The <a href=\"../courses/OA-FLOW/OA-FLOW-GRD.html#grd-3\">ordered graded product identity<\/a> says pointwise in <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(s\\)\">\\(s\\)<\/span> that\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n \\begin{aligned}\n &amp;[a(s)u(s)]\\,\\sigma_s^\\psi(b(t-s)u(t-s))\\\\\n &amp;\\qquad=a(s)\\sigma_s^\\varphi(b(t-s))\n                   u(s)\\sigma_s^\\psi(u(t-s))\\\\\n &amp;\\qquad=a(s)\\sigma_s^\\varphi(b(t-s))u(t).\n \\end{aligned}\n \\tag{SEC6.b}\n\\]\">\\[\n \\begin{aligned}\n &amp;[a(s)u(s)]\\,\\sigma_s^\\psi(b(t-s)u(t-s))\\\\\n &amp;\\qquad=a(s)\\sigma_s^\\varphi(b(t-s))\n                   u(s)\\sigma_s^\\psi(u(t-s))\\\\\n &amp;\\qquad=a(s)\\sigma_s^\\varphi(b(t-s))u(t).\n \\end{aligned}\n \\tag{SEC6.b}\n\\]<\/span>\nThe last equality is the cocycle law with parameters <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(s,t-s\\)\">\\(s,t-s\\)<\/span>. Integrating in <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(s\\)\">\\(s\\)<\/span> gives\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n (a^\\psi*_\\psi b^\\psi)(t)\n      =(a*_\\varphi b)(t)u(t).\n \\tag{SEC6.c}\n\\]\">\\[\n (a^\\psi*_\\psi b^\\psi)(t)\n      =(a*_\\varphi b)(t)u(t).\n \\tag{SEC6.c}\n\\]<\/span>\nThe scalar majorant is the same in both charts, because the coefficient norms are unchanged:\n<span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\|a(s)u(s)\\|\\|b(t-s)u(t-s)\\|=\\|a(s)\\|\\|b(t-s)\\|\\)\">\\(\\|a(s)u(s)\\|\\|b(t-s)u(t-s)\\|=\\|a(s)\\|\\|b(t-s)\\|\\)<\/span>.\nHence the single null set where this majorant has infinite integral can be used for both constructions. Formula (SEC6.c) is an equality of the almost-everywhere section classes.<\/p>\n<p>For the involution, the cocycle law at <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(t,-t\\)\">\\(t,-t\\)<\/span> gives\n<span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\sigma_t^\\psi(u(-t)^*)=u(t)\\)\">\\(\\sigma_t^\\psi(u(-t)^*)=u(t)\\)<\/span>. Modular implementation then gives\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n \\begin{aligned}\n (a^\\psi)^{\\sharp_\\psi}(t)\n &amp;=\\sigma_t^\\psi\\bigl((a(-t)u(-t))^*\\bigr)\\\\\n &amp;=u(t)\\sigma_t^\\psi(a(-t)^*)\\\\\n &amp;=\\sigma_t^\\varphi(a(-t)^*)u(t)\n   =a^{\\sharp_\\varphi}(t)u(t).\n \\end{aligned}\n \\tag{SEC6.d}\n\\]\">\\[\n \\begin{aligned}\n (a^\\psi)^{\\sharp_\\psi}(t)\n &amp;=\\sigma_t^\\psi\\bigl((a(-t)u(-t))^*\\bigr)\\\\\n &amp;=u(t)\\sigma_t^\\psi(a(-t)^*)\\\\\n &amp;=\\sigma_t^\\varphi(a(-t)^*)u(t)\n   =a^{\\sharp_\\varphi}(t)u(t).\n \\end{aligned}\n \\tag{SEC6.d}\n\\]<\/span>\nThus both operations agree under the coordinate transition. Their Banach-space and involution norms agree as well.<\/p>\n<p>Equivalently, in intrinsic notation their definitions are\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n (x*y)(t)=\\int_{\\mathbb R}^{M(t)}x(s)y(t-s)\\,ds,\n \\qquad x^\\sharp(t)=x(-t)^*.\n \\tag{SEC6.e}\n\\]\">\\[\n (x*y)(t)=\\int_{\\mathbb R}^{M(t)}x(s)y(t-s)\\,ds,\n \\qquad x^\\sharp(t)=x(-t)^*.\n \\tag{SEC6.e}\n\\]<\/span>\nThe superscript on the integral records its one fixed output fiber. The <a href=\"../courses/OA-FLOW/OA-FLOW-GRD.html#grd-2\">canonical normal predual charts<\/a> commute with this integral, so it is precisely the coefficient integral already constructed. The ordered cocycle chain rule makes changes through an intermediate faithful weight equal the direct change. Consequently (SEC6.e), the norm and the almost-everywhere quotient define one intrinsic Banach involutive algebra, denoted <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\Gamma^1(\\mathcal F(M))\\)\">\\(\\Gamma^1(\\mathcal F(M))\\)<\/span>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Transport under a normal isomorphism.<\/strong> Let <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(f:M\\to N\\)\">\\(f:M\\to N\\)<\/span> be a normal <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(*\\)\">\\(*\\)<\/span>-isomorphism with normal inverse. The <a href=\"../courses/OA-FLOW/OA-FLOW-GRD.html#grd-6\">graded functor<\/a> gives, for every <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(t\\)\">\\(t\\)<\/span>, a normal onto linear isometry\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n \\begin{aligned}\n F_t(f):M(t)&amp;\\longrightarrow N(t),\\\\\n x\\varphi^{it}&amp;\\longmapsto\n       f(x)(\\varphi\\circ f^{-1})^{it}.\n \\end{aligned}\n \\tag{SEC6.f}\n\\]\">\\[\n \\begin{aligned}\n F_t(f):M(t)&amp;\\longrightarrow N(t),\\\\\n x\\varphi^{it}&amp;\\longmapsto\n       f(x)(\\varphi\\circ f^{-1})^{it}.\n \\end{aligned}\n \\tag{SEC6.f}\n\\]<\/span>\nIt preserves graded products and adjoints, and its total map is a Mackey homeomorphism preserving degree. Define on sections\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n [\\Gamma^1(f)x](t)=F_t(f)(x(t)).\n \\tag{SEC6.g}\n\\]\">\\[\n [\\Gamma^1(f)x](t)=F_t(f)(x(t)).\n \\tag{SEC6.g}\n\\]<\/span>\nA Lusin compact restriction for <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(x\\)\">\\(x\\)<\/span>, followed by this total homeomorphism, is a Lusin compact restriction for its image. The inverse has the same property. Pointwise fiber isometry gives\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n \\|\\Gamma^1(f)x\\|_1=\\|x\\|_1.\n \\tag{SEC6.h}\n\\]\">\\[\n \\|\\Gamma^1(f)x\\|_1=\\|x\\|_1.\n \\tag{SEC6.h}\n\\]<\/span>\nThe maps preserve almost-everywhere equality. Their pointwise inverse is <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(F_t(f^{-1})\\)\">\\(F_t(f^{-1})\\)<\/span>, so (SEC6.g) induces an onto linear isometry of the complete section spaces.<\/p>\n<p>For the product fix <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(t\\)\">\\(t\\)<\/span> outside the scalar norm-majorant exceptional set for <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(x,y\\)\">\\(x,y\\)<\/span>. The same set works for their images, by (SEC6.h)'s pointwise norm equality. The output map <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(F_t(f)\\)\">\\(F_t(f)\\)<\/span> is fixed while the integration variable <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(s\\)\">\\(s\\)<\/span> varies; its normality therefore gives\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n \\begin{aligned}\n [\\Gamma^1(f)(x*y)](t)\n &amp;=F_t(f)\\!\\left(\\int^{M(t)}x(s)y(t-s)\\,ds\\right)\\\\\n &amp;=\\int^{N(t)}F_t(f)(x(s)y(t-s))\\,ds\\\\\n &amp;=\\int^{N(t)}\n       F_s(f)(x(s))F_{t-s}(f)(y(t-s))\\,ds\\\\\n &amp;=[\\Gamma^1(f)x*\\Gamma^1(f)y](t).\n \\end{aligned}\n \\tag{SEC6.i}\n\\]\">\\[\n \\begin{aligned}\n [\\Gamma^1(f)(x*y)](t)\n &amp;=F_t(f)\\!\\left(\\int^{M(t)}x(s)y(t-s)\\,ds\\right)\\\\\n &amp;=\\int^{N(t)}F_t(f)(x(s)y(t-s))\\,ds\\\\\n &amp;=\\int^{N(t)}\n       F_s(f)(x(s))F_{t-s}(f)(y(t-s))\\,ds\\\\\n &amp;=[\\Gamma^1(f)x*\\Gamma^1(f)y](t).\n \\end{aligned}\n \\tag{SEC6.i}\n\\]<\/span>\nThe third equality is the graded product identity, not a passage of a varying family of maps through an integral. Similarly, the graded adjoint identity gives\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n \\begin{aligned}\n [\\Gamma^1(f)(x^\\sharp)](t)\n &amp;=F_t(f)(x(-t)^*)\\\\\n &amp;=(F_{-t}(f)(x(-t)))^*\n   =[\\Gamma^1(f)x]^\\sharp(t).\n \\end{aligned}\n \\tag{SEC6.j}\n\\]\">\\[\n \\begin{aligned}\n [\\Gamma^1(f)(x^\\sharp)](t)\n &amp;=F_t(f)(x(-t)^*)\\\\\n &amp;=(F_{-t}(f)(x(-t)))^*\n   =[\\Gamma^1(f)x]^\\sharp(t).\n \\end{aligned}\n \\tag{SEC6.j}\n\\]<\/span>\nThus <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\Gamma^1(f)\\)\">\\(\\Gamma^1(f)\\)<\/span> is an isometric <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(*\\)\">\\(*\\)<\/span>-isomorphism of Banach involutive algebras. In the <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\varphi\\)\">\\(\\varphi\\)<\/span> and <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\varphi\\circ f^{-1}\\)\">\\(\\varphi\\circ f^{-1}\\)<\/span> coefficient charts it is simply <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(a(t)\\mapsto f(a(t))\\)\">\\(a(t)\\mapsto f(a(t))\\)<\/span>. Chart independence of (SEC6.f) and of (SEC6.e) makes this description independent of the chosen faithful weight.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Exact identity and composition.<\/strong> If <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(g:N\\to P\\)\">\\(g:N\\to P\\)<\/span> is another normal isomorphism, GRD6 proves\n<span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(F_t(g)F_t(f)=F_t(g\\circ f)\\)\">\\(F_t(g)F_t(f)=F_t(g\\circ f)\\)<\/span> for every degree. Applying this equality to each section value proves\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n \\begin{aligned}\n \\Gamma^1(g)\\Gamma^1(f)&amp;=\\Gamma^1(g\\circ f),\\\\\n \\Gamma^1(\\operatorname{id}_M)\n      &amp;=\\operatorname{id}_{\\Gamma^1(\\mathcal F(M))},\\\\\n \\Gamma^1(f)^{-1}&amp;=\\Gamma^1(f^{-1}).\n \\end{aligned}\n \\tag{SEC6.k}\n\\]\">\\[\n \\begin{aligned}\n \\Gamma^1(g)\\Gamma^1(f)&amp;=\\Gamma^1(g\\circ f),\\\\\n \\Gamma^1(\\operatorname{id}_M)\n      &amp;=\\operatorname{id}_{\\Gamma^1(\\mathcal F(M))},\\\\\n \\Gamma^1(f)^{-1}&amp;=\\Gamma^1(f^{-1}).\n \\end{aligned}\n \\tag{SEC6.k}\n\\]<\/span>\nThese are exact equalities on the almost-everywhere classes. They introduce no scalar choice: the transported weights satisfy\n<span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\((\\varphi\\circ f^{-1})\\circ g^{-1}\n =\\varphi\\circ(g\\circ f)^{-1}\\)\">\\((\\varphi\\circ f^{-1})\\circ g^{-1}\n =\\varphi\\circ(g\\circ f)^{-1}\\)<\/span>.\nFor the zero algebra every section value is zero and <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\Gamma^1(\\mathcal F(0))=\\{0\\}\\)\">\\(\\Gamma^1(\\mathcal F(0))=\\{0\\}\\)<\/span>; the unique maps satisfy the same formulas. We obtain a functor from arbitrary von Neumann algebras and normal isomorphisms to Banach involutive algebras and isometric <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(*\\)\">\\(*\\)<\/span>-isomorphisms.<\/p>\n","source":"docs/courses/OA-FLOW/src/OA-FLOW-SEC.md","start_offset":43154,"end_offset":49297,"role":"registered_statement_and_proof_passage"},"d7ff9aa9df598997c4dc94f79c4aa6970abfb9b0ad9e9db56abba3efdd86f131":{"markdown":"# An irrational characteristic class with no AFD realization\n\n*Original exposition, arguments and reproducible illustration: CC0-1.0 to the extent of rights held. Cited publications and existing components retain their own terms.*\n\nThere is a countable group \\(G\\), a proper infinite nonamenable normal subgroup \\(N\\), and a normalized compatible pair in the convention of [compatible lifts](OA-FLOW-CPP.md#cp-compatible) for which no action on any AFD factor of type \\(\\mathrm{II}_1\\) has the prescribed characteristic class. The obstruction excludes every AFD realization of this pair. It does not merely invalidate one crossed-product recipe.\n\nThe motivating realization question is Masamichi Takesaki, *Theory of Operator Algebras III*, XVII.3 Exercise 5, printed pp.293–294. It separately specifies a countable discrete group, a normal subgroup and a compatible pair, without an amenability hypothesis. The preceding Proposition 3.22 has its own abelian-group hypothesis; that hypothesis does not carry into the exercise. The conclusion below concerns the intended realization assertion in the coherent characteristic convention derived from the defining implementer identity. One printed action identity needs correction, as we explain first. The amenable-subgroup theorem of [ARP](OA-FLOW-ARP.md) and the trivial-class construction for arbitrary normal subgroups remain valid. No type-III replacement is asserted.\n\n<a id=\"nnr-convention\"><\/a>\n## The convention and the printed action identity\n\nFor a section \\(s\\) of a central extension of \\(N\\), the conjugation convention is\n\\[\n \\beta_g\\bigl(s(g^{-1}ng)\\bigr)=\\lambda(n,g)s(n).\n \\tag{NNRC1}\n\\]\nAn action satisfies \\(\\beta_g\\beta_h=\\beta_{gh}\\). Apply the two maps successively to \\(s(h^{-1}g^{-1}ngh)\\): first \\(\\beta_h\\) gives \\(\\lambda(g^{-1}ng,h)s(g^{-1}ng)\\), and then \\(\\beta_g\\) gives \\(\\lambda(g^{-1}ng,h)\\lambda(n,g)s(n)\\). Comparing with \\(\\beta_{gh}\\) forces\n\\[\n \\lambda(n,gh)=\\lambda(n,g)\\lambda(g^{-1}ng,h).\n \\tag{NNRC2}\n\\]\nThis is [CPP (CP1)](OA-FLOW-CPP.md#cp-compatible). In XVII.3 Exercise 1(b), printed p.292, the defining identity uses inverse conjugation as in (NNRC1), but Exercise 1(c) writes forward conjugation in the action line. Those two printed formulas are inconsistent. The printed list also omits the inner-action identity, which CPP derives and includes for actual inner implementers. The inverse-conjugation identity (NNRC2), the multiplication identity, compatibility, normalization and inner action are the convention used throughout this lesson. The pair below satisfies all five coherent CPP identities, so retaining inner compatibility does not remove the counterexample. We do not assert that it satisfies every literal printed formula.\n\nThe inconsistency can be seen inside the example itself. Write \\(e_2=(0,1)^t\\), take\n\\[\n n=(e_2,I),\\qquad\n g=\\left(0,\\begin{pmatrix}1&1\\\\0&1\\end{pmatrix}\\right),\n \\qquad h=(e_2,I),\\qquad q=e^{2\\pi i\\sqrt2}.\n \\tag{NNRC3}\n\\]\nFor the pair constructed in Section 1, its restriction to a translation has the explicit formula\n\\[\n \\lambda\\bigl((v,I),(z,A)\\bigr)\n       =e^{2\\pi i\\sqrt2\\,\\omega(z,v)}.\n \\tag{NNRC4}\n\\]\nIndeed the two multiplier exponents in its defining ratio are \\(\\omega(z,v)\\) and \\(\\omega(v,z)=-\\omega(z,v)\\). Here \\(g^{-1}ng=((-1,1)^t,I)\\), whereas \\(gng^{-1}=((1,1)^t,I)\\), and \\(gh=((1,1)^t,A)\\). Consequently\n\\[\n \\lambda(n,gh)=q,\\qquad\n \\lambda(n,g)\\lambda(g^{-1}ng,h)=1\\cdot q=q,\n \\qquad\n \\lambda(n,g)\\lambda(gng^{-1},h)=1\\cdot q^{-1}=q^{-1}.\n \\tag{NNRC5}\n\\]\nSince \\(q\\) has infinite order, \\(q\\ne q^{-1}\\). The theorem refutes the unrestricted realization assertion after this necessary convention correction.\n\n<a id=\"nnr-pair\"><\/a>\n## 1. The group and the admissible pair\n\nLet \\(T=\\mathbb Z^2\\), let \\(K=\\mathrm{SL}_2(\\mathbb Z)\\), and use column vectors. Set\n\\[\n \\Gamma=T\\rtimes K,\\qquad\n (v,A)(w,B)=(v+Aw,AB),\\qquad\n (v,A)^{-1}=(-A^{-1}v,A^{-1}).\n \\tag{NNR1}\n\\]\n\nTake the ambient group \\(G=\\Gamma\\times C_2\\) and its proper normal subgroup \\(N=\\Gamma\\times\\{e\\}\\), identified with \\(\\Gamma\\). Both groups are countable discrete. The subgroup \\(N\\) is infinite and nonamenable, as proved in Section 7. The extra \\(C_2\\) ensures that the prescribed normal subgroup is proper.\n\nWrite \\(\\omega(v,w)=v_1w_2-v_2w_1\\) and fix \\(\\theta=\\sqrt2\\). For \\(n=(v,A)\\), \\(m=(w,B)\\) in \\(N\\), define\n\\[\n \\mu_\\theta(n,m)\n   =\\exp\\bigl(\\pi i\\theta\\,\\omega(v,Aw)\\bigr).\n \\tag{NNR2}\n\\]\nFor \\(h=(g,c)\\) in \\(G\\) and \\(n\\in N\\), put\n\\[\n \\lambda_\\theta(n,h)\n   =\\frac{\\mu_\\theta(g,g^{-1}ng)}{\\mu_\\theta(n,g)}.\n \\tag{NNR3}\n\\]\nThe \\(C_2\\) coordinate is absent because it centralizes \\(N\\).\n\n**Proposition 1.** This is a normalized compatible pair in the full CPP convention, including the inner-action equation.\n\n**Proof.** Every \\(A\\in\\mathrm{SL}_2(\\mathbb Z)\\) preserves \\(\\omega\\). For \\(n=(v,A)\\), \\(m=(w,B)\\), \\(\\ell=(z,C)\\), the two exponents in the multiplier identity agree:\n\\[\n \\begin{aligned}\n \\omega(v,Aw)+\\omega(v+Aw,ABz)\n &=\\omega(v,Aw)+\\omega(v,ABz)+\\omega(w,Bz)\\\\\n &=\\omega(v,A(w+Bz))+\\omega(w,Bz).\n \\end{aligned}\n \\tag{NNR4}\n\\]\nThus \\(\\mu(n,m)\\mu(nm,\\ell)=\\mu(n,m\\ell)\\mu(m,\\ell)\\), and an identity argument gives the value one. Form \\(E=\\mathbb T\\times N\\) with multiplication\n\\[\n (z,n)(w,m)=(zw\\mu(n,m),nm),\\qquad s(n)=(1,n).\n\\]\nAssociativity and inverses follow from the multiplier identity just verified. Its kernel \\(\\mathbb T\\times\\{e\\}\\) is central.\n\nDefine \\(\\beta_{(g,c)}=\\operatorname{Ad}_E s(g)\\). Since \\(s(g)s(h)\\) is a central multiple of \\(s(gh)\\), we have \\(\\beta_{(g,c)}\\beta_{(h,d)}=\\beta_{(gh,cd)}\\). These maps fix the central circle and cover conjugation on \\(N\\). Comparing \\(s(g)s(g^{-1}ng)\\) and \\(s(n)s(g)\\) gives\n\\[\n \\beta_{(g,c)}\\bigl(s(g^{-1}ng)\\bigr)\n       =\\lambda_\\theta(n,(g,c))s(n).\n \\tag{NNR5}\n\\]\nAssociating three section elements gives the multiplication equation. Composing the maps \\(\\beta\\) gives the action equation, and applying \\(\\beta\\) to a product gives compatibility. Taking the acting element to be \\((m,e)\\) gives the inner-action equation. Explicitly,\n\\[\n \\begin{aligned}\n \\lambda(n,hk)&=\\lambda(n,h)\\lambda(h^{-1}nh,k),\\\\\n \\frac{\\lambda(n,h)\\lambda(m,h)}{\\lambda(nm,h)}\n   &=\\frac{\\mu(h^{-1}nh,h^{-1}mh)}{\\mu(n,m)},\\\\\n \\lambda(n,m)&=\\frac{\\mu(m,m^{-1}nm)}{\\mu(n,m)}.\n \\end{aligned}\n \\tag{NNR6}\n\\]\nAll identity values are one. This is also the proved compatible-extension calculation [CPP (CP4)–(CP7)](OA-FLOW-CPP.md#cp-compatible): the extension and its action have been constructed here. Every function is Borel because its domain is countable discrete. \\(\\square\\)\n\nLet \\(a=((1,0)^t,I)\\), \\(b=((0,1)^t,I)\\). They commute in \\(N\\), and\n\\[\n \\frac{\\mu_\\theta(a,b)}{\\mu_\\theta(b,a)}\n       =q,\\qquad q=\\exp(2\\pi i\\sqrt2).\n \\tag{NNR7}\n\\]\nA gauge \\(\\mu^f(n,m)=\\mu(n,m)f(n)f(m)/f(nm)\\) leaves this ratio unchanged when \\(n,m\\) commute. Consequently every projective unitary lift with multiplier cohomologous to \\(\\mu_\\theta\\) satisfies\n\\[\n UV=qVU,\\qquad U=u_a,\\quad V=u_b.\n \\tag{NNR8}\n\\]\nFor every integer \\(d\\ge1\\), \\(q^d\\ne1\\): otherwise \\(d\\sqrt2\\) would be an integer. To recall the elementary irrationality proof, a reduced fraction \\(p/r\\) satisfying \\(p^2=2r^2\\) has even \\(p\\), and then even \\(r\\), a contradiction. In particular \\([\\mu_\\theta]\\) has infinite order. If \\(\\mu_\\theta^d\\) were a coboundary, its commuting-pair ratio would be one.\n\n<a id=\"nnr-afd-vectors\"><\/a>\n## 2. What AFD supplies to a projective representation\n\nWe work more generally with a finite von Neumann algebra \\(M\\) carrying a **supplied faithful normal tracial state** \\(\\tau\\), with \\(\\tau(1)=1\\). Assume \\(M\\) is AFD: an increasing directed family of finite-dimensional unital star algebras \\(F\\) has weakly dense union. If the definition permits nonunital finite algebras, adjoining \\(1\\) preserves finite dimension, the star-algebra property, directedness and density. A sequential exhaustion is enough, but is not assumed. Put \\(H=L^2(M,\\tau)\\), with left multiplication \\(L_x\\). The normal faithful concrete trace representation is the argument of [TRP Section 1](OA-FLOW-TRP.md#trp-setting); its GNS proof uses the supplied trace and does not require the algebra to be a factor.\n\nWe construct the Hilbert–Schmidt space without a trace-class normality theorem. For a finite-rank operator \\(S\\) and an orthonormal basis \\((h_k)\\), put\n\\[\n \\|S\\|_{\\mathrm{HS}}^2=\\sum_k\\|Sh_k\\|^2.\n\\]\nParseval on its finite-dimensional range gives basis independence and the Hilbert inner product. Rank-one operators identify the completion with \\(H\\otimes\\overline H\\). The inequality \\(\\|S\\|\\le\\|S\\|_{\\mathrm{HS}}\\) extends this completion to bounded operators. This extension is faithful: pairing \\(S\\) with the rank-one operator \\(R_{\\eta,\\xi}(\\zeta)=\\langle\\zeta,\\xi\\rangle\\eta\\) gives \\(\\langle S\\xi,\\eta\\rangle\\). If the resulting bounded operator vanishes, all these pairings vanish, and density of rank-one tensors forces the completed vector to vanish. Finite-rank approximation makes every resulting operator compact. Conjugation by a Hilbert-space unitary sends the rank-one operator indexed by \\((\\xi,\\eta)\\) to the one indexed by \\((u\\xi,u\\eta)\\), and hence is unitary on this completion. All sums defining \\(D_F\\) below are finite. These arguments also cover a nonseparable \\(H\\).\n\nThe finite-dimensional block and matrix-unit proof is [TRP Section 10](OA-FLOW-TRP.md#trp-obstruction). For each \\(F\\),\n\\[\n F=\\bigoplus_j M_{d_j},\\qquad\n p_j=1_{M_{d_j}},\\quad t_j=\\tau(p_j)>0,\\qquad\n \\tau(e^j_{rs})=(t_j/d_j)\\,\\mathbf1_{r=s}.\n \\tag{NNR9}\n\\]\nTraciality makes the diagonal matrix units in a block have equal trace and makes every off-diagonal unit have trace zero. Summing the diagonal gives \\(t_j/d_j\\).\n\nLet \\(P_j\\) be the orthogonal projection of \\(H\\) onto the \\(d_j^2\\)-dimensional subspace \\(Fp_j\\), represented as trace vectors. Define the finite-rank positive operator\n\\[\n D_F=\\sum_j\\frac{\\sqrt{t_j}}{d_j}P_j\n       \\quad\\text{on }H.\n \\tag{NNR10}\n\\]\nAn orthonormal basis of \\(Fp_j\\) is \\(f^j_{rs}=\\sqrt{d_j/t_j}\\,e^j_{rs}\\). Matrix-unit summation gives\n\\[\n \\begin{aligned}\n \\|D_F\\|_{\\mathrm{HS}}^2\n   &=\\sum_j(t_j/d_j^2)d_j^2=\\sum_jt_j=1,\\\\\n \\|L_xD_F\\|_{\\mathrm{HS}}^2\n   &=\\sum_j(t_j/d_j^2)\\sum_{r,s}\n       \\tau((f^j_{rs})^*x^*xf^j_{rs})\\\\\n   &=\\sum_j\\tau(x^*xp_j)=\\|x\\|_2^2\n       \\qquad(x\\in M).\n \\end{aligned}\n \\tag{NNR11}\n\\]\nFor the second identity use \\(\\sum_{r,s}f^j_{rs}(f^j_{rs})^*=(d_j^2/t_j)p_j\\) and the trace. Taking adjoints also gives \\(\\|D_FL_x\\|_{\\mathrm{HS}}=\\|x\\|_2\\), since \\((D_FL_x)^*=L_{x^*}D_F\\) and \\(\\|x^*\\|_2=\\|x\\|_2\\). The weights in (NNR10) are essential: an unweighted finite-rank projection generally gives the wrong block traces.\n\nFor \\(y\\in F\\), both \\(L_y\\) and its adjoint preserve every \\(Fp_j\\). Thus \\(L_y\\) commutes with \\(P_j\\) and \\(D_F\\). For \\(u\\in\\mathcal U(M)\\) and any \\(y\\in F\\), (NNR11) therefore gives\n\\[\n \\|L_uD_F-D_FL_u\\|_{\\mathrm{HS}}\n       \\le2\\|u-y\\|_2.\n \\tag{NNR12}\n\\]\nNo unitary approximation or rational block-trace assumption is used. The element \\(y\\) may be the ordinary Hilbert projection of \\(u\\) onto \\(F\\).\n\nThose projections converge. TRP Section 1's bounded approximation argument transfers weak density to the normal trace representation using uniformly bounded approximants; it does not pass an unbounded weakly convergent net through that representation. The closure of \\((\\bigcup F)\\widehat1\\) reduces every algebra in the union. Its projection commutes with their generated algebra \\(M\\) in this representation. It fixes \\(\\widehat1\\), so it fixes the dense space \\(M\\widehat1\\). The closure is therefore all of \\(H\\). Orthogonal projections onto the increasing finite-dimensional spaces \\(F\\widehat1\\) converge strongly to \\(1\\). The corresponding actual elements \\(y_F\\in F\\) satisfy \\(\\|u-y_F\\|_2\\to0\\). This is the same density mechanism proved in [TRP Section 10](OA-FLOW-TRP.md#trp-obstruction).\n\nSuppose \\(u:\\Gamma\\to\\mathcal U(M)\\) is a normalized projective representation, so \\(u_gu_h=\\nu(g,h)u_{gh}\\) with scalar \\(\\nu\\). On the Hilbert space \\(\\mathrm{HS}(H)\\) set\n\\[\n \\Pi_g(S)=L_{u_g}S L_{u_g}^*.\n \\tag{NNR13}\n\\]\nConjugation preserves the Hilbert–Schmidt inner product. Scalar phases cancel, so \\(\\Pi\\) is an ordinary unitary representation of \\(\\Gamma\\). By (NNR11)–(NNR12), its unit vectors \\(D_F\\) satisfy \\(\\|\\Pi_g(D_F)-D_F\\|_{\\mathrm{HS}}\\to0\\) for every \\(g\\). Enumerate \\(\\Gamma\\). At step \\(k\\), choose a finite algebra sufficiently far along the directed family to make this error less than \\(1/k\\) for the first \\(k\\) elements. This gives a sequence of almost invariant unit vectors. Countability is used only for this choice; neither the factor's predual nor the AFD definition is narrowed.\n\n<a id=\"nnr-measure\"><\/a>\n## 3. Two elementary measure facts\n\nThe following arguments supply the measure tools needed for rigidity.\n\n**Compact probability lemma.** Every sequence of Borel probability measures on a compact metric space \\(X\\) has a weakly convergent subsequence.\n\n**Proof.** Construct nested finite Borel partitions \\(\\mathcal P_k\\) whose nonempty cells have diameter at most \\(2^{-k}\\): take finite small-ball covers, disjointize them by successive differences and refine the preceding partition. Choose \\(x_C\\) in each nonempty cell. A diagonal subsequence makes the mass of every cell converge, say to \\(p_C\\). There are countably many cells. The limits are nonnegative, sum to one at each level and satisfy \\(p_C=\\sum_Dp_D\\), where \\(D\\) ranges over the children of \\(C\\).\n\nPartition \\([0,1)\\) into half-open intervals \\(I_C\\) of lengths \\(p_C\\), nested with the cell tree, subdividing each parent interval by its finitely many children. Discard zero-length intervals and the countable set of endpoints. Almost every \\(r\\) specifies an infinite branch of nonempty nested cells. Their closures are nested nonempty compact sets with diameters tending to zero, so they have a unique common point \\(x(r)\\). The level-\\(k\\) simple map \\(r\\mapsto x_{C_k(r)}\\) converges uniformly to \\(x(r)\\) outside the discarded endpoints. Hence \\(x\\) is measurable, after assigning any point on that null set. Push Lebesgue probability forward by \\(x\\).\n\nFor continuous \\(f\\), the integral of the level-\\(k\\) simple map is \\(\\sum_Cp_Cf(x_C)\\). Uniform continuity bounds its error from the pushforward integral by the oscillation of \\(f\\) over distances at most \\(2^{-k}\\). The integrals against the chosen original measures have the same error bound from their finite cell sums; those sums converge to the displayed sum for fixed \\(k\\). Let first the subsequence index and then \\(k\\) tend to infinity. This proves weak convergence using only elementary Lebesgue measure, finite partitions and continuity of integrals. \\(\\square\\)\n\n**Regular-test lemma.** If finite Borel measures \\(\\nu,\\nu'\\) on compact metric \\(X\\) satisfy\n\\[\n \\left|\\int f\\,d(\\nu-\\nu')\\right|\\le c\\|f\\|_\\infty\n\\]\nfor every continuous real \\(f\\), the same bound holds for every bounded real Borel \\(f\\).\n\n**Proof.** We first prove regularity directly. An open set \\(O\\ne X\\) is the increasing union of its compact subsets \\(\\{x:\\operatorname{dist}(x,X\\setminus O)\\ge1/k\\}\\); the case \\(O=X\\) is immediate. Continuity from below gives inner regularity of open sets, and complementation gives outer regularity of closed sets. The family of sets approximable from inside by compact sets and from outside by open sets is closed under complementation. It is closed under countable unions: choose open supersets with summable errors, and use finite unions of compact approximants and finite-measure continuity to approximate from inside. It contains the open sets and therefore all Borel sets.\n\nApply this to \\(\\nu+\\nu'\\). For a bounded real Borel simple function, choose disjoint compact approximants inside its finitely many level sets. They have positive pairwise distances. Choose disjoint open neighborhoods with arbitrarily small extra measure; distance functions provide continuous cutoffs equal to one on the compact sets and zero outside their neighborhoods. The weighted sum of these cutoffs stays within the simple function's supremum norm and approximates it in \\(L^1(\\nu+\\nu')\\). The remaining outside region has arbitrarily small measure. Pass the inequality to this limit. Uniform simple approximation proves it for every bounded real Borel function. \\(\\square\\)\n\n<a id=\"nnr-rigidity\"><\/a>\n## 4. The exact relative rigidity of \\(\\Gamma\\) and \\(T\\)\n\n**Lemma 2.** Every ordinary unitary representation \\(\\pi\\) of \\(\\Gamma=\\mathbb Z^2\\rtimes\\mathrm{SL}_2(\\mathbb Z)\\) with a sequence of almost invariant unit vectors has a nonzero \\(T\\)-invariant vector.\n\n**Proof.** First construct coefficient probability measures from finite sums. For a unit vector \\(\\xi\\) and \\(t\\in X=(\\mathbb R/\\mathbb Z)^2\\), put\n\\[\n F_k^\\xi(t)=\\frac1{(k+1)^2}\n  \\left\\|\\sum_{r,s=0}^k\n       e^{-2\\pi i(rt_1+st_2)}\\pi(r,s)\\xi\\right\\|^2.\n \\tag{NNR14}\n\\]\nThis function is nonnegative and has integral one by orthogonality of scalar characters. When \\(|m|,|n|\\le k\\), its Fourier coefficient at \\((m,n)\\) is\n\\[\n \\left(1-\\frac{|m|}{k+1}\\right)\n \\left(1-\\frac{|n|}{k+1}\\right)\n       \\langle\\pi(m,n)\\xi,\\xi\\rangle.\n\\]\nThe compact probability lemma gives a subsequential weak limit \\(\\nu_\\xi\\) satisfying\n\\[\n \\int_X e^{2\\pi i a\\cdot t}\\,d\\nu_\\xi(t)\n       =\\langle\\pi(a)\\xi,\\xi\\rangle\n       \\qquad(a\\in\\mathbb Z^2).\n \\tag{NNR15}\n\\]\n\nThese coefficients determine the measure. Indeed the scalar Fejér kernels\n\\[\n K_k(t)=\\frac1{k+1}\\left|\\sum_{r=0}^k e^{2\\pi i rt}\\right|^2\n\\]\nhave integral one. Away from the integers by distance at least \\(\\delta>0\\), they are bounded by \\(1/((k+1)\\sin^2(\\pi\\delta))\\). Their product kernels on \\(X\\) concentrate at zero. Uniform continuity gives \\(f*(K_k\\otimes K_k)\\to f\\) uniformly for every continuous \\(f\\); these convolutions are trigonometric polynomials. Equal Fourier coefficients give equal continuous integrals and then equal measures by the regular-test approximation of Borel indicators. Thus uniqueness requires no joint spectral theorem.\n\nFor unit vectors \\(\\xi,\\eta\\), the vector sums in (NNR14), Cauchy–Schwarz for their torus integral and scalar orthogonality give\n\\[\n \\int_X|F_k^\\xi-F_k^\\eta|\\le2\\|\\xi-\\eta\\|.\n \\tag{NNR16}\n\\]\nIn detail, the squared integral norms of the two sums, divided by \\((k+1)^2\\), are one, while that of their difference is \\(\\|\\xi-\\eta\\|^2\\). Use\n\\[\n \\bigl|\\|v\\|^2-\\|w\\|^2\\bigr|\n       \\le(\\|v\\|+\\|w\\|)\\|v-w\\|\n\\]\nand Cauchy–Schwarz. Pass to the coefficient-measure limits, first for continuous tests and then by the regular-test lemma, to obtain\n\\[\n \\left|\\int_X f\\,d(\\nu_\\xi-\\nu_\\eta)\\right|\n       \\le2\\|\\xi-\\eta\\|\\|f\\|_\\infty\n       \\quad(f\\text{ bounded real Borel}).\n \\tag{NNR17}\n\\]\n\nWrite \\(M\\in\\mathrm{SL}_2(\\mathbb Z)\\) also for the group element \\((0,M)\\in\\Gamma\\). The group law and (NNR15) imply\n\\[\n \\nu_{\\pi(M)\\xi}=(M^{-t})_*\\nu_\\xi.\n \\tag{NNR18}\n\\]\nIndeed \\(\\pi(M)^*\\pi(a)\\pi(M)=\\pi(M^{-1}a)\\), and the coefficient is the Fourier coefficient of the displayed pushforward. The uniqueness just proved gives equality.\n\nWe next identify the atom at zero. Put \\(U_i=\\pi(e_i)\\), \\(i=1,2\\), and\n\\[\n C_k=\\frac1{(k+1)^2}\\sum_{r,s=0}^kU_1^rU_2^s.\n\\]\nThese contractions converge strongly to the orthogonal projection \\(P_T\\) onto the common fixed vectors. They act as the identity on that space, while \\(\\|C_k(I-U_i)\\|\\le2/(k+1)\\). The orthogonal complement of the closed span of the ranges of \\(I-U_i\\) is the common fixed space, by taking adjoints and using unitarity. Thus convergence holds on a dense subspace and then everywhere. Expanding the finite polynomial in (NNR15) gives\n\\[\n \\|C_k\\xi\\|^2=\n \\int_X\\prod_{j=1}^2\n \\left|\\frac1{k+1}\\sum_{r=0}^k e^{2\\pi i rt_j}\\right|^2d\\nu_\\xi(t).\n \\tag{NNR19}\n\\]\nThe integrand is bounded by one and converges pointwise to the indicator of \\(\\{0\\}\\). Dominated convergence proves \\(\\nu_\\xi(\\{0\\})=\\|P_T\\xi\\|^2\\).\n\nAssume that there is no nonzero \\(T\\)-fixed vector, and let \\(\\xi_l\\) be almost invariant unit vectors. Put \\(\\nu_l=\\nu_{\\xi_l}\\). They have no atom at zero. Moreover,\n\\[\n \\int_X\\sum_{j=1}^2|e^{2\\pi i t_j}-1|^2d\\nu_l(t)\n       =\\sum_{j=1}^2\\|\\pi(e_j)\\xi_l-\\xi_l\\|^2\n       \\longrightarrow0.\n \\tag{NNR20}\n\\]\nThe continuous integrand vanishes only at zero. Compactness gives a positive minimum on the complement of every neighborhood of zero, so the mass outside each such neighborhood tends to zero.\n\nChoose the representatives \\(\\widetilde t\\in[-1/2,1/2)^2\\), and define the Borel direction map\n\\[\n d:X\\setminus\\{0\\}\\longrightarrow\\mathbb{RP}^1,\n \\qquad d(t)=\\mathbb R\\widetilde t.\n\\]\nPut \\(\\eta_l=d_*\\nu_l\\). These are probabilities because the omitted atom is zero. For each fixed integral invertible matrix \\(S\\), sufficiently small \\(\\|\\widetilde t\\|_\\infty\\) prevents wraparound when multiplying by \\(S\\), and then \\(d(St)=S\\,d(t)\\). By (NNR20), the mass where this equality may fail tends to zero. Equations (NNR17)–(NNR18) and almost invariance under the linear group imply\n\\[\n \\int_{\\mathbb{RP}^1}f(S\\ell)\\,d\\eta_l(\\ell)\n       -\\int_{\\mathbb{RP}^1}f(\\ell)\\,d\\eta_l(\\ell)\n       \\longrightarrow0\n       \\qquad(f\\in C(\\mathbb{RP}^1),\\ S\\in\\mathrm{SL}_2(\\mathbb Z)).\n \\tag{NNR21}\n\\]\nFor real \\(f\\), the wraparound error is at most \\(2\\|f\\|_\\infty\\) times the mass outside one fixed small neighborhood, and the measure-change error is at most \\(2\\|f\\|_\\infty\\|\\pi(S^{-t})\\xi_l-\\xi_l\\|\\). Apply the argument to real and imaginary parts for complex \\(f\\). This fixes the inverse-transpose convention: the dual action of \\(S\\) is supplied by \\(M=S^{-t}\\) in the original linear group.\n\nThe space \\(\\mathbb{RP}^1\\) is the unit circle modulo antipodal identification, with metric \\(\\min(\\|v-w\\|,\\|v+w\\|)\\) on unit representatives; its continuous quotient map proves compactness. Take a weakly convergent subsequence of \\(\\eta_l\\). Its limit \\(\\eta\\) is a probability invariant under every \\(S\\): composition with \\(S\\) preserves continuity, and (NNR21) passes to the limit.\n\nSuch a probability cannot exist. For\n\\[\n T_0=\\begin{pmatrix}1&1\\\\0&1\\end{pmatrix},\n\\]\nuse the coordinate \\(r=x/y\\) on \\(\\mathbb{RP}^1\\setminus\\{\\mathbb R(1,0)^t\\}\\). The map \\(T_0\\) sends \\(r\\) to \\(r+1\\). The disjoint intervals \\([k,k+1)\\), \\(k\\in\\mathbb Z\\), have equal \\(\\eta\\)-mass; finiteness forces that mass to be zero. Their union is the whole finite coordinate line. Thus \\(\\eta\\) is the point mass at \\(\\mathbb R(1,0)^t\\). But\n\\[\n L_0=\\begin{pmatrix}1&0\\\\1&1\\end{pmatrix}\n\\]\nsends this point to \\(\\mathbb R(1,1)^t\\), a different point, contradicting \\(L_0\\)-invariance.\n\nThis proves the lemma. It is the relative-rigidity assertion for this specific pair, proved from finite coefficient sums and projective-line dynamics. No general property-(T) theorem, lattice theorem, Deligne extension, injective-factor classification or spectral disintegration is used. \\(\\square\\)\n\n<a id=\"nnr-finite-dimension\"><\/a>\n## 5. A finite-dimensional subspace forced by AFD\n\nContinue with a finite AFD von Neumann algebra \\(M\\) and the supplied faithful normal tracial state \\(\\tau\\) of Section 2. Apply Lemma 2 to the ordinary representation \\(\\Pi\\) in (NNR13). Its almost invariant vectors were constructed there. We obtain a nonzero Hilbert–Schmidt operator \\(S\\) such that\n\\[\n L_{u_a}S=SL_{u_a}\\qquad(a\\in T).\n \\tag{NNR22}\n\\]\nThe positive operator \\(C=S^*S\\) is nonzero, compact and commutes with every \\(L_{u_a}\\), \\(a\\in T\\). It has a positive eigenvalue with a nonzero finite-dimensional eigenspace \\(E\\).\n\nHere is the full compact-operator argument. A nonzero compact positive operator has its positive norm as an eigenvalue. Choose unit vectors maximizing its quadratic form. In their separable closed span, choose a countable orthonormal basis and a diagonal subsequence of all coordinates. Finite Parseval bounds give a limit vector of norm at most one. Boundedness and finite-coordinate approximation give weak convergence to it; tests on the orthogonal complement vanish. Compactness, or finite-rank norm approximation, makes the images converge strongly. The limit attains the norm. Its norm is one because \\(\\|C\\|>0\\). Positivity of \\(\\|C\\|I-C\\) and its square root gives the eigenvector equation. An infinite-dimensional eigenspace for a positive eigenvalue would contain infinitely many orthonormal vectors whose images stay separated, contradicting compactness. Hence \\(E\\) has finite positive dimension \\(d\\).\n\nCommutation makes \\(E\\) invariant under \\(L_U,L_V\\) and their adjoints. Their restrictions \\(U_E,V_E\\) are therefore unitaries and retain (NNR8). Ordinary finite-dimensional determinants give\n\\[\n \\det(U_EV_E)=q^d\\det(V_EU_E),\\qquad q^d=1.\n \\tag{NNR23}\n\\]\nThe determinants are nonzero, and their products commute. But \\(q^d\\ne1\\) for every \\(d\\ge1\\) by (NNR7), a contradiction.\n\nWe have proved the following exact statement: **there is no projective representation of \\(\\Gamma\\), with multiplier cohomologous to \\(\\mu_\\theta\\), in the unitary group of a finite AFD von Neumann algebra carrying a supplied faithful normal tracial state.** This hypothesis is part of the broader finite-algebra assertion. A factor of type \\(\\mathrm{II}_1\\) supplies such a state.\n\n<a id=\"nnr-nonrealization\"><\/a>\n## 6. The intended unrestricted realization is impossible\n\n**Theorem 3.** For \\(G=\\Gamma\\times C_2\\), \\(N=\\Gamma\\times\\{e\\}\\), \\(\\theta=\\sqrt2\\), and the normalized compatible pair \\((\\lambda_\\theta,\\mu_\\theta)\\) of (NNR2)–(NNR3) in the coherent CPP convention, there is no action \\(\\alpha\\) of \\(G\\) on any AFD factor of type \\(\\mathrm{II}_1\\) with inner kernel \\(N\\) and characteristic class \\([\\lambda_\\theta,\\mu_\\theta]\\).\n\n**Proof.** A factor of type \\(\\mathrm{II}_1\\) has a faithful normal normalized trace. If type is defined through finite projections, the earlier [FCT6–8 construction](OA-FLOW-FCT.md#oa-flow.fct.6) supplies that trace, including normality and faithfulness, without a separability assumption. Choose \\(u_n\\in\\mathcal U(M)\\) implementing \\(\\alpha_n\\) for each \\(n\\in N\\), with \\(u_e=1\\). Because \\(\\alpha\\) is an action and \\(M\\) is a factor, \\(u_nu_mu_{nm}^*\\) is scalar. Thus \\(u\\) is a projective representation of \\(N\\).\n\nThe characteristic convention is [CPP (CP23)–(CP25)](OA-FLOW-CPP.md#cp-actions), with the defining inverse-conjugation identity. Equality of characteristic classes means that the multiplier differs from \\(\\mu_\\theta\\) by a normalized scalar coboundary, as in [CPP (CP8)](OA-FLOW-CPP.md#cp-gauge). The commuting translation implementers therefore satisfy (NNR8), regardless of their phases. Sections 2–5 give the contradiction. The action on \\(G\\setminus N\\) cannot avoid it.\n\nIn fact, the argument excludes any action for which \\(N\\) merely acts innerly and the restricted multiplication class is \\([\\mu_\\theta]\\). Exactness of the outer quotient is not needed for the contradiction. The intended realization assertion imposes stronger requirements and is consequently excluded. \\(\\square\\)\n\nThe recipe correction and this impossibility are distinct. [TRP](OA-FLOW-TRP.md) constructs a finite factor, its faithful trace, the full \\(G\\)-action, the exact inner kernel and the requested pair for arbitrary countable \\(G,N\\) and compatible twists. Its invariant-mean argument shows that the regular product is not AFD when \\(N\\) is nonamenable. [ARP](OA-FLOW-ARP.md) proves that the same regular product is AFD for infinite amenable \\(N\\), arbitrary countable \\(G\\) and every compatible twist. Finite \\(N\\) is covered by TRP Section 9. Theorem 3 proves the stronger impossibility only for the explicit pair here: every other AFD realization of that pair is impossible too.\n\n<a id=\"nnr-boundary\"><\/a>\n## 7. Nonamenability and the precise surviving boundary\n\nThe matrices\n\\[\n A_0=\\begin{pmatrix}1&2\\\\0&1\\end{pmatrix},\n \\qquad B_0=\\begin{pmatrix}1&0\\\\2&1\\end{pmatrix}\n\\]\ngenerate a free group of rank two in \\(\\mathrm{SL}_2(\\mathbb Z)\\). In projective coordinate \\(r=x/y\\), let \\(D_A=\\{|r|>1\\}\\), including infinity, and \\(D_B=\\{|r|<1\\}\\). For every nonzero integer \\(k\\), \\(A_0^k\\) sends \\(D_B\\) into \\(D_A\\). Also \\(B_0^k\\) sends \\(D_A\\) into \\(D_B\\): its formula is \\(r/(2kr+1)\\), and \\(|2kr+1|>|r|\\) when \\(|r|>1\\); infinity goes to \\(1/(2k)\\).\n\nFor a reduced alternating word whose first and last powers have the same generator, applying the word to the other domain sends that domain into the first generator's domain. Hence the word is not the identity. If the first and last generators differ, conjugate by a suitable nonzero power of the first, avoiding cancellation of its initial exponent. The resulting reduced word starts and ends with that generator. A nonzero power of one generator is directly nonidentity from its matrix. Therefore every nonempty reduced word is nonidentity, proving freeness.\n\n[TRP (TR32)](OA-FLOW-TRP.md#trp-obstruction) proves by the four first-letter sets that \\(F_2\\) has no invariant mean. To pass from \\(N\\) to this subgroup \\(H\\), choose one representative \\(r\\) of each left \\(H\\)-orbit \\(Hr\\) in \\(N\\). Extend \\(f\\in\\ell^\\infty(H)\\) by \\(\\widetilde f(hr)=f(h)\\). This map is unital, positive and intertwines left \\(H\\)-translations. Composing an invariant mean on \\(N\\) with this map would give one on \\(H\\), a contradiction. Thus \\(N\\) is nonamenable.\n\nNonamenability alone does not prohibit an inner kernel on an AFD factor of type \\(\\mathrm{II}_1\\). For any countable \\(G\\) and normal \\(N\\), put \\(Q=G/N\\). [TRP Section 8](OA-FLOW-TRP.md#trp-bernoulli) constructs an AFD tensor factor \\(P_Q\\) of type \\(\\mathrm{II}_1\\) over \\(Q\\times\\mathbb N_{>0}\\), with an action \\(\\beta\\) of \\(Q\\) outer at every nonidentity coset, including finite and trivial \\(Q\\). Set \\(\\alpha_g=\\beta_{gN}\\). Its inner kernel is exactly \\(N\\). Choosing \\(u_n=1\\) makes both characteristic functions one. Thus the trivial class is realizable for arbitrary \\(N\\), including the subgroup in Theorem 3.\n\nThe obstruction requires inner implementers for the whole \\(\\Gamma\\). The same irrational translation multiplier is realizable when the prescribed inner subgroup is only the amenable \\(T=\\mathbb Z^2\\) inside the ambient \\(\\Gamma\\). Restrict (NNR2) to translations:\n\\[\n \\mu_T(v,w)=e^{\\pi i\\sqrt2\\,\\omega(v,w)},\\qquad\n \\lambda_T(v,(z,A))=e^{2\\pi i\\sqrt2\\,\\omega(z,v)}.\n\\]\nThese are the restriction of the compatible extension and action already constructed. Inverse conjugation sends \\(v\\) to \\(A^{-1}v\\), and the ratio (NNR3) gives the displayed \\(\\lambda_T\\). They satisfy CPP, and ARP gives an AFD action of \\(\\Gamma\\) on a factor of type \\(\\mathrm{II}_1\\) with exact inner kernel \\(T\\). Its \\(\\mathrm{SL}_2(\\mathbb Z)\\) automorphisms need not be inner and therefore supply no projective lift of all \\(\\Gamma\\) inside the factor. This identifies the hypothesis responsible for the contradiction.\n\nThe quantifiers and conclusions are:\n\n- For arbitrary countable ambient \\(G\\), amenable \\(N\\), and every normalized compatible pair, the finite-subgroup or ARP regular construction gives an AFD realization of type \\(\\mathrm{II}_1\\).\n- For arbitrary \\(N\\) and the trivial class, the quotient Bernoulli construction gives an AFD realization of type \\(\\mathrm{II}_1\\) with exact inner kernel.\n- For the explicit nonamenable \\(N\\) and irrational pair of Theorem 3, no AFD realization of type \\(\\mathrm{II}_1\\) exists.\n- This proof does not characterize all realizable compatible classes for arbitrary nonamenable \\(N\\). CPP, TRP, NB and ARP do not provide that characterization either. The unrestricted positive assertion is refuted by the counterexample.\n\nThese quantifiers cannot be interchanged. A change of factor type, group labels, twist class or normal-subgroup scope would change the original realization question.","html":"<h1>An irrational characteristic class with no AFD realization<\/h1>\n<p><em>Original exposition, arguments and reproducible illustration: CC0-1.0 to the extent of rights held. Cited publications and existing components retain their own terms.<\/em><\/p>\n<p>There is a countable group <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(G\\)\">\\(G\\)<\/span>, a proper infinite nonamenable normal subgroup <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(N\\)\">\\(N\\)<\/span>, and a normalized compatible pair in the convention of <a href=\"../courses/OA-FLOW/OA-FLOW-CPP.html#cp-compatible\">compatible lifts<\/a> for which no action on any AFD factor of type <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\mathrm{II}_1\\)\">\\(\\mathrm{II}_1\\)<\/span> has the prescribed characteristic class. The obstruction excludes every AFD realization of this pair. It does not merely invalidate one crossed-product recipe.<\/p>\n<p>The motivating realization question is Masamichi Takesaki, <em>Theory of Operator Algebras III<\/em>, XVII.3 Exercise 5, printed pp.293–294. It separately specifies a countable discrete group, a normal subgroup and a compatible pair, without an amenability hypothesis. The preceding Proposition 3.22 has its own abelian-group hypothesis; that hypothesis does not carry into the exercise. The conclusion below concerns the intended realization assertion in the coherent characteristic convention derived from the defining implementer identity. One printed action identity needs correction, as we explain first. The amenable-subgroup theorem of <a href=\"../courses/OA-FLOW/OA-FLOW-ARP.html\">ARP<\/a> and the trivial-class construction for arbitrary normal subgroups remain valid. No type-III replacement is asserted.<\/p>\n<h2>The convention and the printed action identity<\/h2>\n<p>For a section <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(s\\)\">\\(s\\)<\/span> of a central extension of <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(N\\)\">\\(N\\)<\/span>, the conjugation convention is\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n \\beta_g\\bigl(s(g^{-1}ng)\\bigr)=\\lambda(n,g)s(n).\n \\tag{NNRC1}\n\\]\">\\[\n \\beta_g\\bigl(s(g^{-1}ng)\\bigr)=\\lambda(n,g)s(n).\n \\tag{NNRC1}\n\\]<\/span>\nAn action satisfies <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\beta_g\\beta_h=\\beta_{gh}\\)\">\\(\\beta_g\\beta_h=\\beta_{gh}\\)<\/span>. Apply the two maps successively to <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(s(h^{-1}g^{-1}ngh)\\)\">\\(s(h^{-1}g^{-1}ngh)\\)<\/span>: first <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\beta_h\\)\">\\(\\beta_h\\)<\/span> gives <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\lambda(g^{-1}ng,h)s(g^{-1}ng)\\)\">\\(\\lambda(g^{-1}ng,h)s(g^{-1}ng)\\)<\/span>, and then <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\beta_g\\)\">\\(\\beta_g\\)<\/span> gives <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\lambda(g^{-1}ng,h)\\lambda(n,g)s(n)\\)\">\\(\\lambda(g^{-1}ng,h)\\lambda(n,g)s(n)\\)<\/span>. Comparing with <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\beta_{gh}\\)\">\\(\\beta_{gh}\\)<\/span> forces\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n \\lambda(n,gh)=\\lambda(n,g)\\lambda(g^{-1}ng,h).\n \\tag{NNRC2}\n\\]\">\\[\n \\lambda(n,gh)=\\lambda(n,g)\\lambda(g^{-1}ng,h).\n \\tag{NNRC2}\n\\]<\/span>\nThis is <a href=\"../courses/OA-FLOW/OA-FLOW-CPP.html#cp-compatible\">CPP (CP1)<\/a>. In XVII.3 Exercise 1(b), printed p.292, the defining identity uses inverse conjugation as in (NNRC1), but Exercise 1(c) writes forward conjugation in the action line. Those two printed formulas are inconsistent. The printed list also omits the inner-action identity, which CPP derives and includes for actual inner implementers. The inverse-conjugation identity (NNRC2), the multiplication identity, compatibility, normalization and inner action are the convention used throughout this lesson. The pair below satisfies all five coherent CPP identities, so retaining inner compatibility does not remove the counterexample. We do not assert that it satisfies every literal printed formula.<\/p>\n<p>The inconsistency can be seen inside the example itself. Write <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(e_2=(0,1)^t\\)\">\\(e_2=(0,1)^t\\)<\/span>, take\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n n=(e_2,I),\\qquad\n g=\\left(0,\\begin{pmatrix}1&amp;1\\\\0&amp;1\\end{pmatrix}\\right),\n \\qquad h=(e_2,I),\\qquad q=e^{2\\pi i\\sqrt2}.\n \\tag{NNRC3}\n\\]\">\\[\n n=(e_2,I),\\qquad\n g=\\left(0,\\begin{pmatrix}1&amp;1\\\\0&amp;1\\end{pmatrix}\\right),\n \\qquad h=(e_2,I),\\qquad q=e^{2\\pi i\\sqrt2}.\n \\tag{NNRC3}\n\\]<\/span>\nFor the pair constructed in Section 1, its restriction to a translation has the explicit formula\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n \\lambda\\bigl((v,I),(z,A)\\bigr)\n       =e^{2\\pi i\\sqrt2\\,\\omega(z,v)}.\n \\tag{NNRC4}\n\\]\">\\[\n \\lambda\\bigl((v,I),(z,A)\\bigr)\n       =e^{2\\pi i\\sqrt2\\,\\omega(z,v)}.\n \\tag{NNRC4}\n\\]<\/span>\nIndeed the two multiplier exponents in its defining ratio are <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\omega(z,v)\\)\">\\(\\omega(z,v)\\)<\/span> and <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\omega(v,z)=-\\omega(z,v)\\)\">\\(\\omega(v,z)=-\\omega(z,v)\\)<\/span>. Here <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(g^{-1}ng=((-1,1)^t,I)\\)\">\\(g^{-1}ng=((-1,1)^t,I)\\)<\/span>, whereas <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(gng^{-1}=((1,1)^t,I)\\)\">\\(gng^{-1}=((1,1)^t,I)\\)<\/span>, and <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(gh=((1,1)^t,A)\\)\">\\(gh=((1,1)^t,A)\\)<\/span>. Consequently\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n \\lambda(n,gh)=q,\\qquad\n \\lambda(n,g)\\lambda(g^{-1}ng,h)=1\\cdot q=q,\n \\qquad\n \\lambda(n,g)\\lambda(gng^{-1},h)=1\\cdot q^{-1}=q^{-1}.\n \\tag{NNRC5}\n\\]\">\\[\n \\lambda(n,gh)=q,\\qquad\n \\lambda(n,g)\\lambda(g^{-1}ng,h)=1\\cdot q=q,\n \\qquad\n \\lambda(n,g)\\lambda(gng^{-1},h)=1\\cdot q^{-1}=q^{-1}.\n \\tag{NNRC5}\n\\]<\/span>\nSince <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(q\\)\">\\(q\\)<\/span> has infinite order, <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(q\\ne q^{-1}\\)\">\\(q\\ne q^{-1}\\)<\/span>. The theorem refutes the unrestricted realization assertion after this necessary convention correction.<\/p>\n<h2>1. The group and the admissible pair<\/h2>\n<p>Let <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(T=\\mathbb Z^2\\)\">\\(T=\\mathbb Z^2\\)<\/span>, let <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(K=\\mathrm{SL}_2(\\mathbb Z)\\)\">\\(K=\\mathrm{SL}_2(\\mathbb Z)\\)<\/span>, and use column vectors. Set\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n \\Gamma=T\\rtimes K,\\qquad\n (v,A)(w,B)=(v+Aw,AB),\\qquad\n (v,A)^{-1}=(-A^{-1}v,A^{-1}).\n \\tag{NNR1}\n\\]\">\\[\n \\Gamma=T\\rtimes K,\\qquad\n (v,A)(w,B)=(v+Aw,AB),\\qquad\n (v,A)^{-1}=(-A^{-1}v,A^{-1}).\n \\tag{NNR1}\n\\]<\/span><\/p>\n<p>Take the ambient group <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(G=\\Gamma\\times C_2\\)\">\\(G=\\Gamma\\times C_2\\)<\/span> and its proper normal subgroup <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(N=\\Gamma\\times\\{e\\}\\)\">\\(N=\\Gamma\\times\\{e\\}\\)<\/span>, identified with <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\Gamma\\)\">\\(\\Gamma\\)<\/span>. Both groups are countable discrete. The subgroup <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(N\\)\">\\(N\\)<\/span> is infinite and nonamenable, as proved in Section 7. The extra <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(C_2\\)\">\\(C_2\\)<\/span> ensures that the prescribed normal subgroup is proper.<\/p>\n<p>Write <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\omega(v,w)=v_1w_2-v_2w_1\\)\">\\(\\omega(v,w)=v_1w_2-v_2w_1\\)<\/span> and fix <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\theta=\\sqrt2\\)\">\\(\\theta=\\sqrt2\\)<\/span>. For <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(n=(v,A)\\)\">\\(n=(v,A)\\)<\/span>, <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(m=(w,B)\\)\">\\(m=(w,B)\\)<\/span> in <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(N\\)\">\\(N\\)<\/span>, define\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n \\mu_\\theta(n,m)\n   =\\exp\\bigl(\\pi i\\theta\\,\\omega(v,Aw)\\bigr).\n \\tag{NNR2}\n\\]\">\\[\n \\mu_\\theta(n,m)\n   =\\exp\\bigl(\\pi i\\theta\\,\\omega(v,Aw)\\bigr).\n \\tag{NNR2}\n\\]<\/span>\nFor <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(h=(g,c)\\)\">\\(h=(g,c)\\)<\/span> in <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(G\\)\">\\(G\\)<\/span> and <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(n\\in N\\)\">\\(n\\in N\\)<\/span>, put\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n \\lambda_\\theta(n,h)\n   =\\frac{\\mu_\\theta(g,g^{-1}ng)}{\\mu_\\theta(n,g)}.\n \\tag{NNR3}\n\\]\">\\[\n \\lambda_\\theta(n,h)\n   =\\frac{\\mu_\\theta(g,g^{-1}ng)}{\\mu_\\theta(n,g)}.\n \\tag{NNR3}\n\\]<\/span>\nThe <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(C_2\\)\">\\(C_2\\)<\/span> coordinate is absent because it centralizes <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(N\\)\">\\(N\\)<\/span>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Proposition 1.<\/strong> This is a normalized compatible pair in the full CPP convention, including the inner-action equation.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Proof.<\/strong> Every <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(A\\in\\mathrm{SL}_2(\\mathbb Z)\\)\">\\(A\\in\\mathrm{SL}_2(\\mathbb Z)\\)<\/span> preserves <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\omega\\)\">\\(\\omega\\)<\/span>. For <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(n=(v,A)\\)\">\\(n=(v,A)\\)<\/span>, <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(m=(w,B)\\)\">\\(m=(w,B)\\)<\/span>, <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\ell=(z,C)\\)\">\\(\\ell=(z,C)\\)<\/span>, the two exponents in the multiplier identity agree:\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n \\begin{aligned}\n \\omega(v,Aw)+\\omega(v+Aw,ABz)\n &amp;=\\omega(v,Aw)+\\omega(v,ABz)+\\omega(w,Bz)\\\\\n &amp;=\\omega(v,A(w+Bz))+\\omega(w,Bz).\n \\end{aligned}\n \\tag{NNR4}\n\\]\">\\[\n \\begin{aligned}\n \\omega(v,Aw)+\\omega(v+Aw,ABz)\n &amp;=\\omega(v,Aw)+\\omega(v,ABz)+\\omega(w,Bz)\\\\\n &amp;=\\omega(v,A(w+Bz))+\\omega(w,Bz).\n \\end{aligned}\n \\tag{NNR4}\n\\]<\/span>\nThus <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\mu(n,m)\\mu(nm,\\ell)=\\mu(n,m\\ell)\\mu(m,\\ell)\\)\">\\(\\mu(n,m)\\mu(nm,\\ell)=\\mu(n,m\\ell)\\mu(m,\\ell)\\)<\/span>, and an identity argument gives the value one. Form <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(E=\\mathbb T\\times N\\)\">\\(E=\\mathbb T\\times N\\)<\/span> with multiplication\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n (z,n)(w,m)=(zw\\mu(n,m),nm),\\qquad s(n)=(1,n).\n\\]\">\\[\n (z,n)(w,m)=(zw\\mu(n,m),nm),\\qquad s(n)=(1,n).\n\\]<\/span>\nAssociativity and inverses follow from the multiplier identity just verified. Its kernel <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\mathbb T\\times\\{e\\}\\)\">\\(\\mathbb T\\times\\{e\\}\\)<\/span> is central.<\/p>\n<p>Define <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\beta_{(g,c)}=\\operatorname{Ad}_E s(g)\\)\">\\(\\beta_{(g,c)}=\\operatorname{Ad}_E s(g)\\)<\/span>. Since <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(s(g)s(h)\\)\">\\(s(g)s(h)\\)<\/span> is a central multiple of <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(s(gh)\\)\">\\(s(gh)\\)<\/span>, we have <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\beta_{(g,c)}\\beta_{(h,d)}=\\beta_{(gh,cd)}\\)\">\\(\\beta_{(g,c)}\\beta_{(h,d)}=\\beta_{(gh,cd)}\\)<\/span>. These maps fix the central circle and cover conjugation on <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(N\\)\">\\(N\\)<\/span>. Comparing <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(s(g)s(g^{-1}ng)\\)\">\\(s(g)s(g^{-1}ng)\\)<\/span> and <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(s(n)s(g)\\)\">\\(s(n)s(g)\\)<\/span> gives\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n \\beta_{(g,c)}\\bigl(s(g^{-1}ng)\\bigr)\n       =\\lambda_\\theta(n,(g,c))s(n).\n \\tag{NNR5}\n\\]\">\\[\n \\beta_{(g,c)}\\bigl(s(g^{-1}ng)\\bigr)\n       =\\lambda_\\theta(n,(g,c))s(n).\n \\tag{NNR5}\n\\]<\/span>\nAssociating three section elements gives the multiplication equation. Composing the maps <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\beta\\)\">\\(\\beta\\)<\/span> gives the action equation, and applying <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\beta\\)\">\\(\\beta\\)<\/span> to a product gives compatibility. Taking the acting element to be <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\((m,e)\\)\">\\((m,e)\\)<\/span> gives the inner-action equation. Explicitly,\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n \\begin{aligned}\n \\lambda(n,hk)&amp;=\\lambda(n,h)\\lambda(h^{-1}nh,k),\\\\\n \\frac{\\lambda(n,h)\\lambda(m,h)}{\\lambda(nm,h)}\n   &amp;=\\frac{\\mu(h^{-1}nh,h^{-1}mh)}{\\mu(n,m)},\\\\\n \\lambda(n,m)&amp;=\\frac{\\mu(m,m^{-1}nm)}{\\mu(n,m)}.\n \\end{aligned}\n \\tag{NNR6}\n\\]\">\\[\n \\begin{aligned}\n \\lambda(n,hk)&amp;=\\lambda(n,h)\\lambda(h^{-1}nh,k),\\\\\n \\frac{\\lambda(n,h)\\lambda(m,h)}{\\lambda(nm,h)}\n   &amp;=\\frac{\\mu(h^{-1}nh,h^{-1}mh)}{\\mu(n,m)},\\\\\n \\lambda(n,m)&amp;=\\frac{\\mu(m,m^{-1}nm)}{\\mu(n,m)}.\n \\end{aligned}\n \\tag{NNR6}\n\\]<\/span>\nAll identity values are one. This is also the proved compatible-extension calculation <a href=\"../courses/OA-FLOW/OA-FLOW-CPP.html#cp-compatible\">CPP (CP4)–(CP7)<\/a>: the extension and its action have been constructed here. Every function is Borel because its domain is countable discrete. <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\square\\)\">\\(\\square\\)<\/span><\/p>\n<p>Let <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(a=((1,0)^t,I)\\)\">\\(a=((1,0)^t,I)\\)<\/span>, <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(b=((0,1)^t,I)\\)\">\\(b=((0,1)^t,I)\\)<\/span>. They commute in <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(N\\)\">\\(N\\)<\/span>, and\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n \\frac{\\mu_\\theta(a,b)}{\\mu_\\theta(b,a)}\n       =q,\\qquad q=\\exp(2\\pi i\\sqrt2).\n \\tag{NNR7}\n\\]\">\\[\n \\frac{\\mu_\\theta(a,b)}{\\mu_\\theta(b,a)}\n       =q,\\qquad q=\\exp(2\\pi i\\sqrt2).\n \\tag{NNR7}\n\\]<\/span>\nA gauge <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\mu^f(n,m)=\\mu(n,m)f(n)f(m)/f(nm)\\)\">\\(\\mu^f(n,m)=\\mu(n,m)f(n)f(m)/f(nm)\\)<\/span> leaves this ratio unchanged when <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(n,m\\)\">\\(n,m\\)<\/span> commute. Consequently every projective unitary lift with multiplier cohomologous to <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\mu_\\theta\\)\">\\(\\mu_\\theta\\)<\/span> satisfies\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n UV=qVU,\\qquad U=u_a,\\quad V=u_b.\n \\tag{NNR8}\n\\]\">\\[\n UV=qVU,\\qquad U=u_a,\\quad V=u_b.\n \\tag{NNR8}\n\\]<\/span>\nFor every integer <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(d\\ge1\\)\">\\(d\\ge1\\)<\/span>, <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(q^d\\ne1\\)\">\\(q^d\\ne1\\)<\/span>: otherwise <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(d\\sqrt2\\)\">\\(d\\sqrt2\\)<\/span> would be an integer. To recall the elementary irrationality proof, a reduced fraction <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(p/r\\)\">\\(p/r\\)<\/span> satisfying <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(p^2=2r^2\\)\">\\(p^2=2r^2\\)<\/span> has even <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(p\\)\">\\(p\\)<\/span>, and then even <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(r\\)\">\\(r\\)<\/span>, a contradiction. In particular <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\([\\mu_\\theta]\\)\">\\([\\mu_\\theta]\\)<\/span> has infinite order. If <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\mu_\\theta^d\\)\">\\(\\mu_\\theta^d\\)<\/span> were a coboundary, its commuting-pair ratio would be one.<\/p>\n<h2>2. What AFD supplies to a projective representation<\/h2>\n<p>We work more generally with a finite von Neumann algebra <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(M\\)\">\\(M\\)<\/span> carrying a <strong>supplied faithful normal tracial state<\/strong> <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\tau\\)\">\\(\\tau\\)<\/span>, with <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\tau(1)=1\\)\">\\(\\tau(1)=1\\)<\/span>. Assume <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(M\\)\">\\(M\\)<\/span> is AFD: an increasing directed family of finite-dimensional unital star algebras <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(F\\)\">\\(F\\)<\/span> has weakly dense union. If the definition permits nonunital finite algebras, adjoining <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(1\\)\">\\(1\\)<\/span> preserves finite dimension, the star-algebra property, directedness and density. A sequential exhaustion is enough, but is not assumed. Put <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(H=L^2(M,\\tau)\\)\">\\(H=L^2(M,\\tau)\\)<\/span>, with left multiplication <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(L_x\\)\">\\(L_x\\)<\/span>. The normal faithful concrete trace representation is the argument of <a href=\"../courses/OA-FLOW/OA-FLOW-TRP.html#trp-setting\">TRP Section 1<\/a>; its GNS proof uses the supplied trace and does not require the algebra to be a factor.<\/p>\n<p>We construct the Hilbert–Schmidt space without a trace-class normality theorem. For a finite-rank operator <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(S\\)\">\\(S\\)<\/span> and an orthonormal basis <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\((h_k)\\)\">\\((h_k)\\)<\/span>, put\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n \\|S\\|_{\\mathrm{HS}}^2=\\sum_k\\|Sh_k\\|^2.\n\\]\">\\[\n \\|S\\|_{\\mathrm{HS}}^2=\\sum_k\\|Sh_k\\|^2.\n\\]<\/span>\nParseval on its finite-dimensional range gives basis independence and the Hilbert inner product. Rank-one operators identify the completion with <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(H\\otimes\\overline H\\)\">\\(H\\otimes\\overline H\\)<\/span>. The inequality <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\|S\\|\\le\\|S\\|_{\\mathrm{HS}}\\)\">\\(\\|S\\|\\le\\|S\\|_{\\mathrm{HS}}\\)<\/span> extends this completion to bounded operators. This extension is faithful: pairing <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(S\\)\">\\(S\\)<\/span> with the rank-one operator <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(R_{\\eta,\\xi}(\\zeta)=\\langle\\zeta,\\xi\\rangle\\eta\\)\">\\(R_{\\eta,\\xi}(\\zeta)=\\langle\\zeta,\\xi\\rangle\\eta\\)<\/span> gives <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\langle S\\xi,\\eta\\rangle\\)\">\\(\\langle S\\xi,\\eta\\rangle\\)<\/span>. If the resulting bounded operator vanishes, all these pairings vanish, and density of rank-one tensors forces the completed vector to vanish. Finite-rank approximation makes every resulting operator compact. Conjugation by a Hilbert-space unitary sends the rank-one operator indexed by <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\((\\xi,\\eta)\\)\">\\((\\xi,\\eta)\\)<\/span> to the one indexed by <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\((u\\xi,u\\eta)\\)\">\\((u\\xi,u\\eta)\\)<\/span>, and hence is unitary on this completion. All sums defining <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(D_F\\)\">\\(D_F\\)<\/span> below are finite. These arguments also cover a nonseparable <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(H\\)\">\\(H\\)<\/span>.<\/p>\n<p>The finite-dimensional block and matrix-unit proof is <a href=\"../courses/OA-FLOW/OA-FLOW-TRP.html#trp-obstruction\">TRP Section 10<\/a>. For each <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(F\\)\">\\(F\\)<\/span>,\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n F=\\bigoplus_j M_{d_j},\\qquad\n p_j=1_{M_{d_j}},\\quad t_j=\\tau(p_j)&gt;0,\\qquad\n \\tau(e^j_{rs})=(t_j/d_j)\\,\\mathbf1_{r=s}.\n \\tag{NNR9}\n\\]\">\\[\n F=\\bigoplus_j M_{d_j},\\qquad\n p_j=1_{M_{d_j}},\\quad t_j=\\tau(p_j)&gt;0,\\qquad\n \\tau(e^j_{rs})=(t_j/d_j)\\,\\mathbf1_{r=s}.\n \\tag{NNR9}\n\\]<\/span>\nTraciality makes the diagonal matrix units in a block have equal trace and makes every off-diagonal unit have trace zero. Summing the diagonal gives <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(t_j/d_j\\)\">\\(t_j/d_j\\)<\/span>.<\/p>\n<p>Let <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(P_j\\)\">\\(P_j\\)<\/span> be the orthogonal projection of <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(H\\)\">\\(H\\)<\/span> onto the <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(d_j^2\\)\">\\(d_j^2\\)<\/span>-dimensional subspace <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(Fp_j\\)\">\\(Fp_j\\)<\/span>, represented as trace vectors. Define the finite-rank positive operator\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n D_F=\\sum_j\\frac{\\sqrt{t_j}}{d_j}P_j\n       \\quad\\text{on }H.\n \\tag{NNR10}\n\\]\">\\[\n D_F=\\sum_j\\frac{\\sqrt{t_j}}{d_j}P_j\n       \\quad\\text{on }H.\n \\tag{NNR10}\n\\]<\/span>\nAn orthonormal basis of <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(Fp_j\\)\">\\(Fp_j\\)<\/span> is <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(f^j_{rs}=\\sqrt{d_j/t_j}\\,e^j_{rs}\\)\">\\(f^j_{rs}=\\sqrt{d_j/t_j}\\,e^j_{rs}\\)<\/span>. Matrix-unit summation gives\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n \\begin{aligned}\n \\|D_F\\|_{\\mathrm{HS}}^2\n   &amp;=\\sum_j(t_j/d_j^2)d_j^2=\\sum_jt_j=1,\\\\\n \\|L_xD_F\\|_{\\mathrm{HS}}^2\n   &amp;=\\sum_j(t_j/d_j^2)\\sum_{r,s}\n       \\tau((f^j_{rs})^*x^*xf^j_{rs})\\\\\n   &amp;=\\sum_j\\tau(x^*xp_j)=\\|x\\|_2^2\n       \\qquad(x\\in M).\n \\end{aligned}\n \\tag{NNR11}\n\\]\">\\[\n \\begin{aligned}\n \\|D_F\\|_{\\mathrm{HS}}^2\n   &amp;=\\sum_j(t_j/d_j^2)d_j^2=\\sum_jt_j=1,\\\\\n \\|L_xD_F\\|_{\\mathrm{HS}}^2\n   &amp;=\\sum_j(t_j/d_j^2)\\sum_{r,s}\n       \\tau((f^j_{rs})^*x^*xf^j_{rs})\\\\\n   &amp;=\\sum_j\\tau(x^*xp_j)=\\|x\\|_2^2\n       \\qquad(x\\in M).\n \\end{aligned}\n \\tag{NNR11}\n\\]<\/span>\nFor the second identity use <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\sum_{r,s}f^j_{rs}(f^j_{rs})^*=(d_j^2/t_j)p_j\\)\">\\(\\sum_{r,s}f^j_{rs}(f^j_{rs})^*=(d_j^2/t_j)p_j\\)<\/span> and the trace. Taking adjoints also gives <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\|D_FL_x\\|_{\\mathrm{HS}}=\\|x\\|_2\\)\">\\(\\|D_FL_x\\|_{\\mathrm{HS}}=\\|x\\|_2\\)<\/span>, since <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\((D_FL_x)^*=L_{x^*}D_F\\)\">\\((D_FL_x)^*=L_{x^*}D_F\\)<\/span> and <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\|x^*\\|_2=\\|x\\|_2\\)\">\\(\\|x^*\\|_2=\\|x\\|_2\\)<\/span>. The weights in (NNR10) are essential: an unweighted finite-rank projection generally gives the wrong block traces.<\/p>\n<p>For <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(y\\in F\\)\">\\(y\\in F\\)<\/span>, both <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(L_y\\)\">\\(L_y\\)<\/span> and its adjoint preserve every <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(Fp_j\\)\">\\(Fp_j\\)<\/span>. Thus <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(L_y\\)\">\\(L_y\\)<\/span> commutes with <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(P_j\\)\">\\(P_j\\)<\/span> and <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(D_F\\)\">\\(D_F\\)<\/span>. For <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(u\\in\\mathcal U(M)\\)\">\\(u\\in\\mathcal U(M)\\)<\/span> and any <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(y\\in F\\)\">\\(y\\in F\\)<\/span>, (NNR11) therefore gives\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n \\|L_uD_F-D_FL_u\\|_{\\mathrm{HS}}\n       \\le2\\|u-y\\|_2.\n \\tag{NNR12}\n\\]\">\\[\n \\|L_uD_F-D_FL_u\\|_{\\mathrm{HS}}\n       \\le2\\|u-y\\|_2.\n \\tag{NNR12}\n\\]<\/span>\nNo unitary approximation or rational block-trace assumption is used. The element <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(y\\)\">\\(y\\)<\/span> may be the ordinary Hilbert projection of <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(u\\)\">\\(u\\)<\/span> onto <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(F\\)\">\\(F\\)<\/span>.<\/p>\n<p>Those projections converge. TRP Section 1's bounded approximation argument transfers weak density to the normal trace representation using uniformly bounded approximants; it does not pass an unbounded weakly convergent net through that representation. The closure of <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\((\\bigcup F)\\widehat1\\)\">\\((\\bigcup F)\\widehat1\\)<\/span> reduces every algebra in the union. Its projection commutes with their generated algebra <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(M\\)\">\\(M\\)<\/span> in this representation. It fixes <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\widehat1\\)\">\\(\\widehat1\\)<\/span>, so it fixes the dense space <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(M\\widehat1\\)\">\\(M\\widehat1\\)<\/span>. The closure is therefore all of <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(H\\)\">\\(H\\)<\/span>. Orthogonal projections onto the increasing finite-dimensional spaces <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(F\\widehat1\\)\">\\(F\\widehat1\\)<\/span> converge strongly to <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(1\\)\">\\(1\\)<\/span>. The corresponding actual elements <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(y_F\\in F\\)\">\\(y_F\\in F\\)<\/span> satisfy <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\|u-y_F\\|_2\\to0\\)\">\\(\\|u-y_F\\|_2\\to0\\)<\/span>. This is the same density mechanism proved in <a href=\"../courses/OA-FLOW/OA-FLOW-TRP.html#trp-obstruction\">TRP Section 10<\/a>.<\/p>\n<p>Suppose <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(u:\\Gamma\\to\\mathcal U(M)\\)\">\\(u:\\Gamma\\to\\mathcal U(M)\\)<\/span> is a normalized projective representation, so <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(u_gu_h=\\nu(g,h)u_{gh}\\)\">\\(u_gu_h=\\nu(g,h)u_{gh}\\)<\/span> with scalar <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\nu\\)\">\\(\\nu\\)<\/span>. On the Hilbert space <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\mathrm{HS}(H)\\)\">\\(\\mathrm{HS}(H)\\)<\/span> set\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n \\Pi_g(S)=L_{u_g}S L_{u_g}^*.\n \\tag{NNR13}\n\\]\">\\[\n \\Pi_g(S)=L_{u_g}S L_{u_g}^*.\n \\tag{NNR13}\n\\]<\/span>\nConjugation preserves the Hilbert–Schmidt inner product. Scalar phases cancel, so <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\Pi\\)\">\\(\\Pi\\)<\/span> is an ordinary unitary representation of <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\Gamma\\)\">\\(\\Gamma\\)<\/span>. By (NNR11)–(NNR12), its unit vectors <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(D_F\\)\">\\(D_F\\)<\/span> satisfy <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\|\\Pi_g(D_F)-D_F\\|_{\\mathrm{HS}}\\to0\\)\">\\(\\|\\Pi_g(D_F)-D_F\\|_{\\mathrm{HS}}\\to0\\)<\/span> for every <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(g\\)\">\\(g\\)<\/span>. Enumerate <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\Gamma\\)\">\\(\\Gamma\\)<\/span>. At step <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(k\\)\">\\(k\\)<\/span>, choose a finite algebra sufficiently far along the directed family to make this error less than <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(1/k\\)\">\\(1/k\\)<\/span> for the first <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(k\\)\">\\(k\\)<\/span> elements. This gives a sequence of almost invariant unit vectors. Countability is used only for this choice; neither the factor's predual nor the AFD definition is narrowed.<\/p>\n<h2>3. Two elementary measure facts<\/h2>\n<p>The following arguments supply the measure tools needed for rigidity.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Compact probability lemma.<\/strong> Every sequence of Borel probability measures on a compact metric space <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(X\\)\">\\(X\\)<\/span> has a weakly convergent subsequence.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Proof.<\/strong> Construct nested finite Borel partitions <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\mathcal P_k\\)\">\\(\\mathcal P_k\\)<\/span> whose nonempty cells have diameter at most <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(2^{-k}\\)\">\\(2^{-k}\\)<\/span>: take finite small-ball covers, disjointize them by successive differences and refine the preceding partition. Choose <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(x_C\\)\">\\(x_C\\)<\/span> in each nonempty cell. A diagonal subsequence makes the mass of every cell converge, say to <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(p_C\\)\">\\(p_C\\)<\/span>. There are countably many cells. The limits are nonnegative, sum to one at each level and satisfy <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(p_C=\\sum_Dp_D\\)\">\\(p_C=\\sum_Dp_D\\)<\/span>, where <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(D\\)\">\\(D\\)<\/span> ranges over the children of <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(C\\)\">\\(C\\)<\/span>.<\/p>\n<p>Partition <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\([0,1)\\)\">\\([0,1)\\)<\/span> into half-open intervals <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(I_C\\)\">\\(I_C\\)<\/span> of lengths <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(p_C\\)\">\\(p_C\\)<\/span>, nested with the cell tree, subdividing each parent interval by its finitely many children. Discard zero-length intervals and the countable set of endpoints. Almost every <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(r\\)\">\\(r\\)<\/span> specifies an infinite branch of nonempty nested cells. Their closures are nested nonempty compact sets with diameters tending to zero, so they have a unique common point <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(x(r)\\)\">\\(x(r)\\)<\/span>. The level-<span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(k\\)\">\\(k\\)<\/span> simple map <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(r\\mapsto x_{C_k(r)}\\)\">\\(r\\mapsto x_{C_k(r)}\\)<\/span> converges uniformly to <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(x(r)\\)\">\\(x(r)\\)<\/span> outside the discarded endpoints. Hence <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(x\\)\">\\(x\\)<\/span> is measurable, after assigning any point on that null set. Push Lebesgue probability forward by <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(x\\)\">\\(x\\)<\/span>.<\/p>\n<p>For continuous <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(f\\)\">\\(f\\)<\/span>, the integral of the level-<span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(k\\)\">\\(k\\)<\/span> simple map is <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\sum_Cp_Cf(x_C)\\)\">\\(\\sum_Cp_Cf(x_C)\\)<\/span>. Uniform continuity bounds its error from the pushforward integral by the oscillation of <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(f\\)\">\\(f\\)<\/span> over distances at most <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(2^{-k}\\)\">\\(2^{-k}\\)<\/span>. The integrals against the chosen original measures have the same error bound from their finite cell sums; those sums converge to the displayed sum for fixed <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(k\\)\">\\(k\\)<\/span>. Let first the subsequence index and then <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(k\\)\">\\(k\\)<\/span> tend to infinity. This proves weak convergence using only elementary Lebesgue measure, finite partitions and continuity of integrals. <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\square\\)\">\\(\\square\\)<\/span><\/p>\n<p><strong>Regular-test lemma.<\/strong> If finite Borel measures <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\nu,\\nu&#x27;\\)\">\\(\\nu,\\nu&#x27;\\)<\/span> on compact metric <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(X\\)\">\\(X\\)<\/span> satisfy\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n \\left|\\int f\\,d(\\nu-\\nu&#x27;)\\right|\\le c\\|f\\|_\\infty\n\\]\">\\[\n \\left|\\int f\\,d(\\nu-\\nu&#x27;)\\right|\\le c\\|f\\|_\\infty\n\\]<\/span>\nfor every continuous real <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(f\\)\">\\(f\\)<\/span>, the same bound holds for every bounded real Borel <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(f\\)\">\\(f\\)<\/span>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Proof.<\/strong> We first prove regularity directly. An open set <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(O\\ne X\\)\">\\(O\\ne X\\)<\/span> is the increasing union of its compact subsets <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\{x:\\operatorname{dist}(x,X\\setminus O)\\ge1/k\\}\\)\">\\(\\{x:\\operatorname{dist}(x,X\\setminus O)\\ge1/k\\}\\)<\/span>; the case <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(O=X\\)\">\\(O=X\\)<\/span> is immediate. Continuity from below gives inner regularity of open sets, and complementation gives outer regularity of closed sets. The family of sets approximable from inside by compact sets and from outside by open sets is closed under complementation. It is closed under countable unions: choose open supersets with summable errors, and use finite unions of compact approximants and finite-measure continuity to approximate from inside. It contains the open sets and therefore all Borel sets.<\/p>\n<p>Apply this to <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\nu+\\nu&#x27;\\)\">\\(\\nu+\\nu&#x27;\\)<\/span>. For a bounded real Borel simple function, choose disjoint compact approximants inside its finitely many level sets. They have positive pairwise distances. Choose disjoint open neighborhoods with arbitrarily small extra measure; distance functions provide continuous cutoffs equal to one on the compact sets and zero outside their neighborhoods. The weighted sum of these cutoffs stays within the simple function's supremum norm and approximates it in <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(L^1(\\nu+\\nu&#x27;)\\)\">\\(L^1(\\nu+\\nu&#x27;)\\)<\/span>. The remaining outside region has arbitrarily small measure. Pass the inequality to this limit. Uniform simple approximation proves it for every bounded real Borel function. <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\square\\)\">\\(\\square\\)<\/span><\/p>\n<h2>4. The exact relative rigidity of <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\Gamma\\)\">\\(\\Gamma\\)<\/span> and <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(T\\)\">\\(T\\)<\/span><\/h2>\n<p><strong>Lemma 2.<\/strong> Every ordinary unitary representation <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\pi\\)\">\\(\\pi\\)<\/span> of <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\Gamma=\\mathbb Z^2\\rtimes\\mathrm{SL}_2(\\mathbb Z)\\)\">\\(\\Gamma=\\mathbb Z^2\\rtimes\\mathrm{SL}_2(\\mathbb Z)\\)<\/span> with a sequence of almost invariant unit vectors has a nonzero <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(T\\)\">\\(T\\)<\/span>-invariant vector.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Proof.<\/strong> First construct coefficient probability measures from finite sums. For a unit vector <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\xi\\)\">\\(\\xi\\)<\/span> and <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(t\\in X=(\\mathbb R/\\mathbb Z)^2\\)\">\\(t\\in X=(\\mathbb R/\\mathbb Z)^2\\)<\/span>, put\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n F_k^\\xi(t)=\\frac1{(k+1)^2}\n  \\left\\|\\sum_{r,s=0}^k\n       e^{-2\\pi i(rt_1+st_2)}\\pi(r,s)\\xi\\right\\|^2.\n \\tag{NNR14}\n\\]\">\\[\n F_k^\\xi(t)=\\frac1{(k+1)^2}\n  \\left\\|\\sum_{r,s=0}^k\n       e^{-2\\pi i(rt_1+st_2)}\\pi(r,s)\\xi\\right\\|^2.\n \\tag{NNR14}\n\\]<\/span>\nThis function is nonnegative and has integral one by orthogonality of scalar characters. When <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(|m|,|n|\\le k\\)\">\\(|m|,|n|\\le k\\)<\/span>, its Fourier coefficient at <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\((m,n)\\)\">\\((m,n)\\)<\/span> is\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n \\left(1-\\frac{|m|}{k+1}\\right)\n \\left(1-\\frac{|n|}{k+1}\\right)\n       \\langle\\pi(m,n)\\xi,\\xi\\rangle.\n\\]\">\\[\n \\left(1-\\frac{|m|}{k+1}\\right)\n \\left(1-\\frac{|n|}{k+1}\\right)\n       \\langle\\pi(m,n)\\xi,\\xi\\rangle.\n\\]<\/span>\nThe compact probability lemma gives a subsequential weak limit <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\nu_\\xi\\)\">\\(\\nu_\\xi\\)<\/span> satisfying\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n \\int_X e^{2\\pi i a\\cdot t}\\,d\\nu_\\xi(t)\n       =\\langle\\pi(a)\\xi,\\xi\\rangle\n       \\qquad(a\\in\\mathbb Z^2).\n \\tag{NNR15}\n\\]\">\\[\n \\int_X e^{2\\pi i a\\cdot t}\\,d\\nu_\\xi(t)\n       =\\langle\\pi(a)\\xi,\\xi\\rangle\n       \\qquad(a\\in\\mathbb Z^2).\n \\tag{NNR15}\n\\]<\/span><\/p>\n<p>These coefficients determine the measure. Indeed the scalar Fejér kernels\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n K_k(t)=\\frac1{k+1}\\left|\\sum_{r=0}^k e^{2\\pi i rt}\\right|^2\n\\]\">\\[\n K_k(t)=\\frac1{k+1}\\left|\\sum_{r=0}^k e^{2\\pi i rt}\\right|^2\n\\]<\/span>\nhave integral one. Away from the integers by distance at least <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\delta&gt;0\\)\">\\(\\delta&gt;0\\)<\/span>, they are bounded by <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(1/((k+1)\\sin^2(\\pi\\delta))\\)\">\\(1/((k+1)\\sin^2(\\pi\\delta))\\)<\/span>. Their product kernels on <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(X\\)\">\\(X\\)<\/span> concentrate at zero. Uniform continuity gives <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(f*(K_k\\otimes K_k)\\to f\\)\">\\(f*(K_k\\otimes K_k)\\to f\\)<\/span> uniformly for every continuous <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(f\\)\">\\(f\\)<\/span>; these convolutions are trigonometric polynomials. Equal Fourier coefficients give equal continuous integrals and then equal measures by the regular-test approximation of Borel indicators. Thus uniqueness requires no joint spectral theorem.<\/p>\n<p>For unit vectors <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\xi,\\eta\\)\">\\(\\xi,\\eta\\)<\/span>, the vector sums in (NNR14), Cauchy–Schwarz for their torus integral and scalar orthogonality give\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n \\int_X|F_k^\\xi-F_k^\\eta|\\le2\\|\\xi-\\eta\\|.\n \\tag{NNR16}\n\\]\">\\[\n \\int_X|F_k^\\xi-F_k^\\eta|\\le2\\|\\xi-\\eta\\|.\n \\tag{NNR16}\n\\]<\/span>\nIn detail, the squared integral norms of the two sums, divided by <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\((k+1)^2\\)\">\\((k+1)^2\\)<\/span>, are one, while that of their difference is <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\|\\xi-\\eta\\|^2\\)\">\\(\\|\\xi-\\eta\\|^2\\)<\/span>. Use\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n \\bigl|\\|v\\|^2-\\|w\\|^2\\bigr|\n       \\le(\\|v\\|+\\|w\\|)\\|v-w\\|\n\\]\">\\[\n \\bigl|\\|v\\|^2-\\|w\\|^2\\bigr|\n       \\le(\\|v\\|+\\|w\\|)\\|v-w\\|\n\\]<\/span>\nand Cauchy–Schwarz. Pass to the coefficient-measure limits, first for continuous tests and then by the regular-test lemma, to obtain\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n \\left|\\int_X f\\,d(\\nu_\\xi-\\nu_\\eta)\\right|\n       \\le2\\|\\xi-\\eta\\|\\|f\\|_\\infty\n       \\quad(f\\text{ bounded real Borel}).\n \\tag{NNR17}\n\\]\">\\[\n \\left|\\int_X f\\,d(\\nu_\\xi-\\nu_\\eta)\\right|\n       \\le2\\|\\xi-\\eta\\|\\|f\\|_\\infty\n       \\quad(f\\text{ bounded real Borel}).\n \\tag{NNR17}\n\\]<\/span><\/p>\n<p>Write <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(M\\in\\mathrm{SL}_2(\\mathbb Z)\\)\">\\(M\\in\\mathrm{SL}_2(\\mathbb Z)\\)<\/span> also for the group element <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\((0,M)\\in\\Gamma\\)\">\\((0,M)\\in\\Gamma\\)<\/span>. The group law and (NNR15) imply\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n \\nu_{\\pi(M)\\xi}=(M^{-t})_*\\nu_\\xi.\n \\tag{NNR18}\n\\]\">\\[\n \\nu_{\\pi(M)\\xi}=(M^{-t})_*\\nu_\\xi.\n \\tag{NNR18}\n\\]<\/span>\nIndeed <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\pi(M)^*\\pi(a)\\pi(M)=\\pi(M^{-1}a)\\)\">\\(\\pi(M)^*\\pi(a)\\pi(M)=\\pi(M^{-1}a)\\)<\/span>, and the coefficient is the Fourier coefficient of the displayed pushforward. The uniqueness just proved gives equality.<\/p>\n<p>We next identify the atom at zero. Put <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(U_i=\\pi(e_i)\\)\">\\(U_i=\\pi(e_i)\\)<\/span>, <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(i=1,2\\)\">\\(i=1,2\\)<\/span>, and\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n C_k=\\frac1{(k+1)^2}\\sum_{r,s=0}^kU_1^rU_2^s.\n\\]\">\\[\n C_k=\\frac1{(k+1)^2}\\sum_{r,s=0}^kU_1^rU_2^s.\n\\]<\/span>\nThese contractions converge strongly to the orthogonal projection <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(P_T\\)\">\\(P_T\\)<\/span> onto the common fixed vectors. They act as the identity on that space, while <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\|C_k(I-U_i)\\|\\le2/(k+1)\\)\">\\(\\|C_k(I-U_i)\\|\\le2/(k+1)\\)<\/span>. The orthogonal complement of the closed span of the ranges of <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(I-U_i\\)\">\\(I-U_i\\)<\/span> is the common fixed space, by taking adjoints and using unitarity. Thus convergence holds on a dense subspace and then everywhere. Expanding the finite polynomial in (NNR15) gives\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n \\|C_k\\xi\\|^2=\n \\int_X\\prod_{j=1}^2\n \\left|\\frac1{k+1}\\sum_{r=0}^k e^{2\\pi i rt_j}\\right|^2d\\nu_\\xi(t).\n \\tag{NNR19}\n\\]\">\\[\n \\|C_k\\xi\\|^2=\n \\int_X\\prod_{j=1}^2\n \\left|\\frac1{k+1}\\sum_{r=0}^k e^{2\\pi i rt_j}\\right|^2d\\nu_\\xi(t).\n \\tag{NNR19}\n\\]<\/span>\nThe integrand is bounded by one and converges pointwise to the indicator of <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\{0\\}\\)\">\\(\\{0\\}\\)<\/span>. Dominated convergence proves <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\nu_\\xi(\\{0\\})=\\|P_T\\xi\\|^2\\)\">\\(\\nu_\\xi(\\{0\\})=\\|P_T\\xi\\|^2\\)<\/span>.<\/p>\n<p>Assume that there is no nonzero <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(T\\)\">\\(T\\)<\/span>-fixed vector, and let <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\xi_l\\)\">\\(\\xi_l\\)<\/span> be almost invariant unit vectors. Put <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\nu_l=\\nu_{\\xi_l}\\)\">\\(\\nu_l=\\nu_{\\xi_l}\\)<\/span>. They have no atom at zero. Moreover,\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n \\int_X\\sum_{j=1}^2|e^{2\\pi i t_j}-1|^2d\\nu_l(t)\n       =\\sum_{j=1}^2\\|\\pi(e_j)\\xi_l-\\xi_l\\|^2\n       \\longrightarrow0.\n \\tag{NNR20}\n\\]\">\\[\n \\int_X\\sum_{j=1}^2|e^{2\\pi i t_j}-1|^2d\\nu_l(t)\n       =\\sum_{j=1}^2\\|\\pi(e_j)\\xi_l-\\xi_l\\|^2\n       \\longrightarrow0.\n \\tag{NNR20}\n\\]<\/span>\nThe continuous integrand vanishes only at zero. Compactness gives a positive minimum on the complement of every neighborhood of zero, so the mass outside each such neighborhood tends to zero.<\/p>\n<p>Choose the representatives <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\widetilde t\\in[-1/2,1/2)^2\\)\">\\(\\widetilde t\\in[-1/2,1/2)^2\\)<\/span>, and define the Borel direction map\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n d:X\\setminus\\{0\\}\\longrightarrow\\mathbb{RP}^1,\n \\qquad d(t)=\\mathbb R\\widetilde t.\n\\]\">\\[\n d:X\\setminus\\{0\\}\\longrightarrow\\mathbb{RP}^1,\n \\qquad d(t)=\\mathbb R\\widetilde t.\n\\]<\/span>\nPut <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\eta_l=d_*\\nu_l\\)\">\\(\\eta_l=d_*\\nu_l\\)<\/span>. These are probabilities because the omitted atom is zero. For each fixed integral invertible matrix <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(S\\)\">\\(S\\)<\/span>, sufficiently small <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\|\\widetilde t\\|_\\infty\\)\">\\(\\|\\widetilde t\\|_\\infty\\)<\/span> prevents wraparound when multiplying by <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(S\\)\">\\(S\\)<\/span>, and then <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(d(St)=S\\,d(t)\\)\">\\(d(St)=S\\,d(t)\\)<\/span>. By (NNR20), the mass where this equality may fail tends to zero. Equations (NNR17)–(NNR18) and almost invariance under the linear group imply\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n \\int_{\\mathbb{RP}^1}f(S\\ell)\\,d\\eta_l(\\ell)\n       -\\int_{\\mathbb{RP}^1}f(\\ell)\\,d\\eta_l(\\ell)\n       \\longrightarrow0\n       \\qquad(f\\in C(\\mathbb{RP}^1),\\ S\\in\\mathrm{SL}_2(\\mathbb Z)).\n \\tag{NNR21}\n\\]\">\\[\n \\int_{\\mathbb{RP}^1}f(S\\ell)\\,d\\eta_l(\\ell)\n       -\\int_{\\mathbb{RP}^1}f(\\ell)\\,d\\eta_l(\\ell)\n       \\longrightarrow0\n       \\qquad(f\\in C(\\mathbb{RP}^1),\\ S\\in\\mathrm{SL}_2(\\mathbb Z)).\n \\tag{NNR21}\n\\]<\/span>\nFor real <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(f\\)\">\\(f\\)<\/span>, the wraparound error is at most <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(2\\|f\\|_\\infty\\)\">\\(2\\|f\\|_\\infty\\)<\/span> times the mass outside one fixed small neighborhood, and the measure-change error is at most <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(2\\|f\\|_\\infty\\|\\pi(S^{-t})\\xi_l-\\xi_l\\|\\)\">\\(2\\|f\\|_\\infty\\|\\pi(S^{-t})\\xi_l-\\xi_l\\|\\)<\/span>. Apply the argument to real and imaginary parts for complex <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(f\\)\">\\(f\\)<\/span>. This fixes the inverse-transpose convention: the dual action of <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(S\\)\">\\(S\\)<\/span> is supplied by <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(M=S^{-t}\\)\">\\(M=S^{-t}\\)<\/span> in the original linear group.<\/p>\n<p>The space <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\mathbb{RP}^1\\)\">\\(\\mathbb{RP}^1\\)<\/span> is the unit circle modulo antipodal identification, with metric <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\min(\\|v-w\\|,\\|v+w\\|)\\)\">\\(\\min(\\|v-w\\|,\\|v+w\\|)\\)<\/span> on unit representatives; its continuous quotient map proves compactness. Take a weakly convergent subsequence of <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\eta_l\\)\">\\(\\eta_l\\)<\/span>. Its limit <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\eta\\)\">\\(\\eta\\)<\/span> is a probability invariant under every <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(S\\)\">\\(S\\)<\/span>: composition with <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(S\\)\">\\(S\\)<\/span> preserves continuity, and (NNR21) passes to the limit.<\/p>\n<p>Such a probability cannot exist. For\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n T_0=\\begin{pmatrix}1&amp;1\\\\0&amp;1\\end{pmatrix},\n\\]\">\\[\n T_0=\\begin{pmatrix}1&amp;1\\\\0&amp;1\\end{pmatrix},\n\\]<\/span>\nuse the coordinate <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(r=x/y\\)\">\\(r=x/y\\)<\/span> on <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\mathbb{RP}^1\\setminus\\{\\mathbb R(1,0)^t\\}\\)\">\\(\\mathbb{RP}^1\\setminus\\{\\mathbb R(1,0)^t\\}\\)<\/span>. The map <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(T_0\\)\">\\(T_0\\)<\/span> sends <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(r\\)\">\\(r\\)<\/span> to <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(r+1\\)\">\\(r+1\\)<\/span>. The disjoint intervals <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\([k,k+1)\\)\">\\([k,k+1)\\)<\/span>, <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(k\\in\\mathbb Z\\)\">\\(k\\in\\mathbb Z\\)<\/span>, have equal <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\eta\\)\">\\(\\eta\\)<\/span>-mass; finiteness forces that mass to be zero. Their union is the whole finite coordinate line. Thus <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\eta\\)\">\\(\\eta\\)<\/span> is the point mass at <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\mathbb R(1,0)^t\\)\">\\(\\mathbb R(1,0)^t\\)<\/span>. But\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n L_0=\\begin{pmatrix}1&amp;0\\\\1&amp;1\\end{pmatrix}\n\\]\">\\[\n L_0=\\begin{pmatrix}1&amp;0\\\\1&amp;1\\end{pmatrix}\n\\]<\/span>\nsends this point to <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\mathbb R(1,1)^t\\)\">\\(\\mathbb R(1,1)^t\\)<\/span>, a different point, contradicting <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(L_0\\)\">\\(L_0\\)<\/span>-invariance.<\/p>\n<p>This proves the lemma. It is the relative-rigidity assertion for this specific pair, proved from finite coefficient sums and projective-line dynamics. No general property-(T) theorem, lattice theorem, Deligne extension, injective-factor classification or spectral disintegration is used. <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\square\\)\">\\(\\square\\)<\/span><\/p>\n<h2>5. A finite-dimensional subspace forced by AFD<\/h2>\n<p>Continue with a finite AFD von Neumann algebra <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(M\\)\">\\(M\\)<\/span> and the supplied faithful normal tracial state <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\tau\\)\">\\(\\tau\\)<\/span> of Section 2. Apply Lemma 2 to the ordinary representation <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\Pi\\)\">\\(\\Pi\\)<\/span> in (NNR13). Its almost invariant vectors were constructed there. We obtain a nonzero Hilbert–Schmidt operator <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(S\\)\">\\(S\\)<\/span> such that\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n L_{u_a}S=SL_{u_a}\\qquad(a\\in T).\n \\tag{NNR22}\n\\]\">\\[\n L_{u_a}S=SL_{u_a}\\qquad(a\\in T).\n \\tag{NNR22}\n\\]<\/span>\nThe positive operator <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(C=S^*S\\)\">\\(C=S^*S\\)<\/span> is nonzero, compact and commutes with every <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(L_{u_a}\\)\">\\(L_{u_a}\\)<\/span>, <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(a\\in T\\)\">\\(a\\in T\\)<\/span>. It has a positive eigenvalue with a nonzero finite-dimensional eigenspace <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(E\\)\">\\(E\\)<\/span>.<\/p>\n<p>Here is the full compact-operator argument. A nonzero compact positive operator has its positive norm as an eigenvalue. Choose unit vectors maximizing its quadratic form. In their separable closed span, choose a countable orthonormal basis and a diagonal subsequence of all coordinates. Finite Parseval bounds give a limit vector of norm at most one. Boundedness and finite-coordinate approximation give weak convergence to it; tests on the orthogonal complement vanish. Compactness, or finite-rank norm approximation, makes the images converge strongly. The limit attains the norm. Its norm is one because <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\|C\\|&gt;0\\)\">\\(\\|C\\|&gt;0\\)<\/span>. Positivity of <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\|C\\|I-C\\)\">\\(\\|C\\|I-C\\)<\/span> and its square root gives the eigenvector equation. An infinite-dimensional eigenspace for a positive eigenvalue would contain infinitely many orthonormal vectors whose images stay separated, contradicting compactness. Hence <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(E\\)\">\\(E\\)<\/span> has finite positive dimension <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(d\\)\">\\(d\\)<\/span>.<\/p>\n<p>Commutation makes <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(E\\)\">\\(E\\)<\/span> invariant under <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(L_U,L_V\\)\">\\(L_U,L_V\\)<\/span> and their adjoints. Their restrictions <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(U_E,V_E\\)\">\\(U_E,V_E\\)<\/span> are therefore unitaries and retain (NNR8). Ordinary finite-dimensional determinants give\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n \\det(U_EV_E)=q^d\\det(V_EU_E),\\qquad q^d=1.\n \\tag{NNR23}\n\\]\">\\[\n \\det(U_EV_E)=q^d\\det(V_EU_E),\\qquad q^d=1.\n \\tag{NNR23}\n\\]<\/span>\nThe determinants are nonzero, and their products commute. But <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(q^d\\ne1\\)\">\\(q^d\\ne1\\)<\/span> for every <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(d\\ge1\\)\">\\(d\\ge1\\)<\/span> by (NNR7), a contradiction.<\/p>\n<p>We have proved the following exact statement: <strong>there is no projective representation of <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\Gamma\\)\">\\(\\Gamma\\)<\/span>, with multiplier cohomologous to <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\mu_\\theta\\)\">\\(\\mu_\\theta\\)<\/span>, in the unitary group of a finite AFD von Neumann algebra carrying a supplied faithful normal tracial state.<\/strong> This hypothesis is part of the broader finite-algebra assertion. A factor of type <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\mathrm{II}_1\\)\">\\(\\mathrm{II}_1\\)<\/span> supplies such a state.<\/p>\n<h2>6. The intended unrestricted realization is impossible<\/h2>\n<p><strong>Theorem 3.<\/strong> For <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(G=\\Gamma\\times C_2\\)\">\\(G=\\Gamma\\times C_2\\)<\/span>, <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(N=\\Gamma\\times\\{e\\}\\)\">\\(N=\\Gamma\\times\\{e\\}\\)<\/span>, <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\theta=\\sqrt2\\)\">\\(\\theta=\\sqrt2\\)<\/span>, and the normalized compatible pair <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\((\\lambda_\\theta,\\mu_\\theta)\\)\">\\((\\lambda_\\theta,\\mu_\\theta)\\)<\/span> of (NNR2)–(NNR3) in the coherent CPP convention, there is no action <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\alpha\\)\">\\(\\alpha\\)<\/span> of <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(G\\)\">\\(G\\)<\/span> on any AFD factor of type <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\mathrm{II}_1\\)\">\\(\\mathrm{II}_1\\)<\/span> with inner kernel <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(N\\)\">\\(N\\)<\/span> and characteristic class <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\([\\lambda_\\theta,\\mu_\\theta]\\)\">\\([\\lambda_\\theta,\\mu_\\theta]\\)<\/span>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Proof.<\/strong> A factor of type <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\mathrm{II}_1\\)\">\\(\\mathrm{II}_1\\)<\/span> has a faithful normal normalized trace. If type is defined through finite projections, the earlier <a href=\"../courses/OA-FLOW/OA-FLOW-FCT.html#oa-flow.fct.6\">FCT6–8 construction<\/a> supplies that trace, including normality and faithfulness, without a separability assumption. Choose <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(u_n\\in\\mathcal U(M)\\)\">\\(u_n\\in\\mathcal U(M)\\)<\/span> implementing <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\alpha_n\\)\">\\(\\alpha_n\\)<\/span> for each <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(n\\in N\\)\">\\(n\\in N\\)<\/span>, with <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(u_e=1\\)\">\\(u_e=1\\)<\/span>. Because <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\alpha\\)\">\\(\\alpha\\)<\/span> is an action and <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(M\\)\">\\(M\\)<\/span> is a factor, <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(u_nu_mu_{nm}^*\\)\">\\(u_nu_mu_{nm}^*\\)<\/span> is scalar. Thus <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(u\\)\">\\(u\\)<\/span> is a projective representation of <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(N\\)\">\\(N\\)<\/span>.<\/p>\n<p>The characteristic convention is <a href=\"../courses/OA-FLOW/OA-FLOW-CPP.html#cp-actions\">CPP (CP23)–(CP25)<\/a>, with the defining inverse-conjugation identity. Equality of characteristic classes means that the multiplier differs from <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\mu_\\theta\\)\">\\(\\mu_\\theta\\)<\/span> by a normalized scalar coboundary, as in <a href=\"../courses/OA-FLOW/OA-FLOW-CPP.html#cp-gauge\">CPP (CP8)<\/a>. The commuting translation implementers therefore satisfy (NNR8), regardless of their phases. Sections 2–5 give the contradiction. The action on <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(G\\setminus N\\)\">\\(G\\setminus N\\)<\/span> cannot avoid it.<\/p>\n<p>In fact, the argument excludes any action for which <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(N\\)\">\\(N\\)<\/span> merely acts innerly and the restricted multiplication class is <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\([\\mu_\\theta]\\)\">\\([\\mu_\\theta]\\)<\/span>. Exactness of the outer quotient is not needed for the contradiction. The intended realization assertion imposes stronger requirements and is consequently excluded. <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\square\\)\">\\(\\square\\)<\/span><\/p>\n<p>The recipe correction and this impossibility are distinct. <a href=\"../courses/OA-FLOW/OA-FLOW-TRP.html\">TRP<\/a> constructs a finite factor, its faithful trace, the full <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(G\\)\">\\(G\\)<\/span>-action, the exact inner kernel and the requested pair for arbitrary countable <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(G,N\\)\">\\(G,N\\)<\/span> and compatible twists. Its invariant-mean argument shows that the regular product is not AFD when <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(N\\)\">\\(N\\)<\/span> is nonamenable. <a href=\"../courses/OA-FLOW/OA-FLOW-ARP.html\">ARP<\/a> proves that the same regular product is AFD for infinite amenable <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(N\\)\">\\(N\\)<\/span>, arbitrary countable <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(G\\)\">\\(G\\)<\/span> and every compatible twist. Finite <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(N\\)\">\\(N\\)<\/span> is covered by TRP Section 9. Theorem 3 proves the stronger impossibility only for the explicit pair here: every other AFD realization of that pair is impossible too.<\/p>\n<h2>7. Nonamenability and the precise surviving boundary<\/h2>\n<p>The matrices\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n A_0=\\begin{pmatrix}1&amp;2\\\\0&amp;1\\end{pmatrix},\n \\qquad B_0=\\begin{pmatrix}1&amp;0\\\\2&amp;1\\end{pmatrix}\n\\]\">\\[\n A_0=\\begin{pmatrix}1&amp;2\\\\0&amp;1\\end{pmatrix},\n \\qquad B_0=\\begin{pmatrix}1&amp;0\\\\2&amp;1\\end{pmatrix}\n\\]<\/span>\ngenerate a free group of rank two in <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\mathrm{SL}_2(\\mathbb Z)\\)\">\\(\\mathrm{SL}_2(\\mathbb Z)\\)<\/span>. In projective coordinate <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(r=x/y\\)\">\\(r=x/y\\)<\/span>, let <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(D_A=\\{|r|&gt;1\\}\\)\">\\(D_A=\\{|r|&gt;1\\}\\)<\/span>, including infinity, and <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(D_B=\\{|r|&lt;1\\}\\)\">\\(D_B=\\{|r|&lt;1\\}\\)<\/span>. For every nonzero integer <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(k\\)\">\\(k\\)<\/span>, <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(A_0^k\\)\">\\(A_0^k\\)<\/span> sends <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(D_B\\)\">\\(D_B\\)<\/span> into <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(D_A\\)\">\\(D_A\\)<\/span>. Also <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(B_0^k\\)\">\\(B_0^k\\)<\/span> sends <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(D_A\\)\">\\(D_A\\)<\/span> into <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(D_B\\)\">\\(D_B\\)<\/span>: its formula is <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(r/(2kr+1)\\)\">\\(r/(2kr+1)\\)<\/span>, and <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(|2kr+1|&gt;|r|\\)\">\\(|2kr+1|&gt;|r|\\)<\/span> when <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(|r|&gt;1\\)\">\\(|r|&gt;1\\)<\/span>; infinity goes to <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(1/(2k)\\)\">\\(1/(2k)\\)<\/span>.<\/p>\n<p>For a reduced alternating word whose first and last powers have the same generator, applying the word to the other domain sends that domain into the first generator's domain. Hence the word is not the identity. If the first and last generators differ, conjugate by a suitable nonzero power of the first, avoiding cancellation of its initial exponent. The resulting reduced word starts and ends with that generator. A nonzero power of one generator is directly nonidentity from its matrix. Therefore every nonempty reduced word is nonidentity, proving freeness.<\/p>\n<p><a href=\"../courses/OA-FLOW/OA-FLOW-TRP.html#trp-obstruction\">TRP (TR32)<\/a> proves by the four first-letter sets that <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(F_2\\)\">\\(F_2\\)<\/span> has no invariant mean. To pass from <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(N\\)\">\\(N\\)<\/span> to this subgroup <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(H\\)\">\\(H\\)<\/span>, choose one representative <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(r\\)\">\\(r\\)<\/span> of each left <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(H\\)\">\\(H\\)<\/span>-orbit <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(Hr\\)\">\\(Hr\\)<\/span> in <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(N\\)\">\\(N\\)<\/span>. Extend <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(f\\in\\ell^\\infty(H)\\)\">\\(f\\in\\ell^\\infty(H)\\)<\/span> by <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\widetilde f(hr)=f(h)\\)\">\\(\\widetilde f(hr)=f(h)\\)<\/span>. This map is unital, positive and intertwines left <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(H\\)\">\\(H\\)<\/span>-translations. Composing an invariant mean on <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(N\\)\">\\(N\\)<\/span> with this map would give one on <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(H\\)\">\\(H\\)<\/span>, a contradiction. Thus <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(N\\)\">\\(N\\)<\/span> is nonamenable.<\/p>\n<p>Nonamenability alone does not prohibit an inner kernel on an AFD factor of type <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\mathrm{II}_1\\)\">\\(\\mathrm{II}_1\\)<\/span>. For any countable <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(G\\)\">\\(G\\)<\/span> and normal <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(N\\)\">\\(N\\)<\/span>, put <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(Q=G/N\\)\">\\(Q=G/N\\)<\/span>. <a href=\"../courses/OA-FLOW/OA-FLOW-TRP.html#trp-bernoulli\">TRP Section 8<\/a> constructs an AFD tensor factor <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(P_Q\\)\">\\(P_Q\\)<\/span> of type <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\mathrm{II}_1\\)\">\\(\\mathrm{II}_1\\)<\/span> over <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(Q\\times\\mathbb N_{&gt;0}\\)\">\\(Q\\times\\mathbb N_{&gt;0}\\)<\/span>, with an action <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\beta\\)\">\\(\\beta\\)<\/span> of <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(Q\\)\">\\(Q\\)<\/span> outer at every nonidentity coset, including finite and trivial <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(Q\\)\">\\(Q\\)<\/span>. Set <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\alpha_g=\\beta_{gN}\\)\">\\(\\alpha_g=\\beta_{gN}\\)<\/span>. Its inner kernel is exactly <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(N\\)\">\\(N\\)<\/span>. Choosing <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(u_n=1\\)\">\\(u_n=1\\)<\/span> makes both characteristic functions one. Thus the trivial class is realizable for arbitrary <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(N\\)\">\\(N\\)<\/span>, including the subgroup in Theorem 3.<\/p>\n<p>The obstruction requires inner implementers for the whole <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\Gamma\\)\">\\(\\Gamma\\)<\/span>. The same irrational translation multiplier is realizable when the prescribed inner subgroup is only the amenable <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(T=\\mathbb Z^2\\)\">\\(T=\\mathbb Z^2\\)<\/span> inside the ambient <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\Gamma\\)\">\\(\\Gamma\\)<\/span>. Restrict (NNR2) to translations:\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n \\mu_T(v,w)=e^{\\pi i\\sqrt2\\,\\omega(v,w)},\\qquad\n \\lambda_T(v,(z,A))=e^{2\\pi i\\sqrt2\\,\\omega(z,v)}.\n\\]\">\\[\n \\mu_T(v,w)=e^{\\pi i\\sqrt2\\,\\omega(v,w)},\\qquad\n \\lambda_T(v,(z,A))=e^{2\\pi i\\sqrt2\\,\\omega(z,v)}.\n\\]<\/span>\nThese are the restriction of the compatible extension and action already constructed. Inverse conjugation sends <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(v\\)\">\\(v\\)<\/span> to <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(A^{-1}v\\)\">\\(A^{-1}v\\)<\/span>, and the ratio (NNR3) gives the displayed <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\lambda_T\\)\">\\(\\lambda_T\\)<\/span>. They satisfy CPP, and ARP gives an AFD action of <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\Gamma\\)\">\\(\\Gamma\\)<\/span> on a factor of type <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\mathrm{II}_1\\)\">\\(\\mathrm{II}_1\\)<\/span> with exact inner kernel <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(T\\)\">\\(T\\)<\/span>. Its <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\mathrm{SL}_2(\\mathbb Z)\\)\">\\(\\mathrm{SL}_2(\\mathbb Z)\\)<\/span> automorphisms need not be inner and therefore supply no projective lift of all <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\Gamma\\)\">\\(\\Gamma\\)<\/span> inside the factor. This identifies the hypothesis responsible for the contradiction.<\/p>\n<p>The quantifiers and conclusions are:<\/p>\n<ul>\n<li>For arbitrary countable ambient <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(G\\)\">\\(G\\)<\/span>, amenable <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(N\\)\">\\(N\\)<\/span>, and every normalized compatible pair, the finite-subgroup or ARP regular construction gives an AFD realization of type <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\mathrm{II}_1\\)\">\\(\\mathrm{II}_1\\)<\/span>.<\/li>\n<li>For arbitrary <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(N\\)\">\\(N\\)<\/span> and the trivial class, the quotient Bernoulli construction gives an AFD realization of type <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\mathrm{II}_1\\)\">\\(\\mathrm{II}_1\\)<\/span> with exact inner kernel.<\/li>\n<li>For the explicit nonamenable <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(N\\)\">\\(N\\)<\/span> and irrational pair of Theorem 3, no AFD realization of type <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\mathrm{II}_1\\)\">\\(\\mathrm{II}_1\\)<\/span> exists.<\/li>\n<li>This proof does not characterize all realizable compatible classes for arbitrary nonamenable <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(N\\)\">\\(N\\)<\/span>. CPP, TRP, NB and ARP do not provide that characterization either. The unrestricted positive assertion is refuted by the counterexample.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p>These quantifiers cannot be interchanged. A change of factor type, group labels, twist class or normal-subgroup scope would change the original realization question.<\/p>\n","source":"docs/courses/OA-FLOW/src/OA-FLOW-NNR.md","start_offset":0,"end_offset":31624,"role":"registered_statement_and_proof_passage"},"d7300230ee328755a36a0cac119e6fc9d0f2f03a9fd2622cb170ee1fd919cd52":{"markdown":"### The spectral radius formula\n\n**Theorem 5.4** (spectral radius formula). For every element \\(x\\) of a Banach algebra \\(A\\),\n\\[\n\\begin{gathered}\nr(x)\\\\\n=\\lim_{n\\to\\infty}\\|x^n\\|^{1/n}\\\\\n=\\inf_{n\\geq1}\\|x^n\\|^{1/n}.\n\\end{gathered}\n\\tag{5.1}\n\\]\n\nStep 1 below gives the lower spectral-radius bound directly, before any limit of roots is known to exist. Remark 5.5 supplies a second proof using submultiplicativity.\n\n**Proof.** By Proposition 4.2(1) and [Proposition 3.3(2)](#oa-fnd-bn-04) we may compute in \\(A_1\\), where \\(\\|j(x)^n\\|_1=\\|x^n\\|\\). So let \\(A\\) be unital. If \\(A=\\{0\\}\\), both sides are \\(0\\), since \\(\\sigma'_A(x)=\\{0\\}\\). Let \\(A\\) be nontrivial.\n\n*Step 1: \\(r(x)\\leq\\|x^n\\|^{1/n}\\) for every \\(n\\geq1\\).* Let \\(\\lambda\\in\\sigma_A(x)\\). In \\(A\\),\n\\[\n\\begin{gathered}\nx^n-\\lambda^n\\\\\n=(x-\\lambda)p(x)\\\\\n=p(x)(x-\\lambda),\\\\\np(x)\\\\\n=\\sum_{k=0}^{n-1}\\lambda^{n-1-k}x^k .\n\\end{gathered}\n\\]\nIf \\(x^n-\\lambda^n\\) had an inverse \\(w\\), then \\(p(x)w\\) would be a right inverse and \\(wp(x)\\) a left inverse of \\(x-\\lambda\\), which is not invertible. So \\(\\lambda^n\\in\\sigma_A(x^n)\\), and \\(|\\lambda|^n\\leq\\|x^n\\|\\) because the spectrum of \\(x^n\\) lies in the disc of radius \\(\\|x^n\\|\\) ([Proposition 4.4(1)](#oa-fnd-bn-07)). Take the supremum over \\(\\lambda\\).\n\n*Step 2: \\(\\limsup_n\\|x^n\\|^{1/n}\\leq r(x)\\).* Fix \\(n\\geq0\\) and \\(\\varphi\\in A^*\\). Since \\(\\sigma_A(x)\\) lies in the closed disc of radius \\(r(x)\\), the function \\(g(\\lambda)=\\lambda^{n+1}\\varphi(R_x(\\lambda))\\) is holomorphic with a continuous derivative on \\(\\{|\\lambda|>r(x)\\}\\) (Proposition 4.4(5)). For \\(\\rho>\\|x\\|\\) the series (4.1) converges uniformly on \\(|\\lambda|=\\rho\\), and integrating term by term gives\n\\[\n\\begin{gathered}\n\\frac1{2\\pi}\\int_0^{2\\pi}g(\\rho e^{i\\theta})\\,d\\theta\\\\\n=\\sum_{k\\geq0}\\varphi(x^k)\\,\\frac1{2\\pi}\\int_0^{2\\pi}(\\rho e^{i\\theta})^{n-k}\\,d\\theta\\\\\n=\\varphi(x^n).\n\\end{gathered}\n\\]\nBy Lemma 5.1, the left side is the same for every \\(\\rho>r(x)\\). So \\(|\\varphi(x^n)|\\leq\\rho^{n+1}m(\\rho)\\|\\varphi\\|\\) for every \\(\\rho>r(x)\\), where \\(m(\\rho)=\\max_{|\\lambda|=\\rho}\\|R_x(\\lambda)\\|\\) is finite because \\(R_x\\) is continuous. By the Hahn–Banach theorem, \\(\\|x^n\\|\\leq\\rho^{n+1}m(\\rho)\\) for all \\(n\\). Hence \\(\\limsup_n\\|x^n\\|^{1/n}\\leq\\rho\\), and we let \\(\\rho\\) decrease to \\(r(x)\\).\n\nSteps 1 and 2 give \\[\n\\begin{gathered}\nr(x)\\\\\n\\leq\\inf_n\\|x^n\\|^{1/n}\\\\\n\\leq\\liminf_n\\|x^n\\|^{1/n}\\\\\n\\leq\\limsup_n\\|x^n\\|^{1/n}\\\\\n\\leq r(x).\n\\end{gathered}\n\\] \\(\\square\\)\n\n**Remark 5.5.** Submultiplicativity alone shows that the limit in (5.1) exists. Here is the elementary argument often called Fekete’s lemma in this multiplicative form. Put \\(s_n=\\|x^n\\|\\). If some \\(s_k=0\\), all powers from \\(k\\) onwards are zero and the limit is zero. Otherwise fix \\(k\\), write \\(n=qk+r\\) with \\(0\\leq r<k\\), and put \\(C_k=\\max(1,s_1,\\ldots,s_{k-1})\\), with \\(C_1=1\\). Submultiplicativity gives \\(s_n\\leq C_ks_k^q\\), including \\(r=0\\) without an identity factor. Hence \\(\\limsup_ns_n^{1/n}\\leq s_k^{1/k}\\). Taking the infimum over \\(k\\), and noting that every \\(s_n^{1/n}\\) is at least that infimum, proves convergence to \\(\\inf_{k\\geq1}s_k^{1/k}\\). The content of Theorem 5.4 is that this limit is the spectral radius.\n\n**Corollary 5.6.**\n1. \\(r(x^k)=r(x)^k\\) and \\(r(cx)=|c|\\,r(x)\\).\n2. \\(r(xy)=r(yx)\\) for all \\(x,y\\).\n3. If \\(xy=yx\\), then \\(r(xy)\\leq r(x)r(y)\\) and \\(r(x+y)\\leq r(x)+r(y)\\).\n4. If \\(A\\) is unital and \\(r(1-x)<1\\), then \\(x\\) is invertible, and the series (2.1) converges absolutely.\n\n**Proof.** (1) follows from (5.1). (2) is Proposition 4.2(2). (3) \\((xy)^n=x^ny^n\\) gives the first bound. For the second, fix \\(\\varepsilon>0\\). By (5.1) there is \\(C\\geq1\\) with \\(\\|x^k\\|\\leq C(r(x)+\\varepsilon)^k\\) and \\(\\|y^k\\|\\leq C(r(y)+\\varepsilon)^k\\) for all \\(k\\geq0\\). The binomial theorem holds for commuting elements, and gives \\(\\|(x+y)^n\\|\\leq C^2(r(x)+r(y)+2\\varepsilon)^n\\). (4) Choose \\(\\rho\\) with \\(r(1-x)<\\rho<1\\). By (5.1), \\(\\|(1-x)^n\\|\\leq\\rho^n\\) for large \\(n\\), so the series converges absolutely, and the proof of Proposition 2.1 applies. \\(\\square\\)\n\n**Examples 5.7.**\n- A nonzero nilpotent element \\(N\\) (\\(N^m=0\\)) has \\(r(N)=0<\\|N\\|\\).\n- *The Volterra operator.* On \\(C([0,1])\\) with the supremum norm, let \\((Vf)(t)=\\int_0^tf(s)\\,ds\\). Put \\(W_n(t)=\\int_0^t(t-s)^{n-1}f(s)/(n-1)!\\,ds\\). The fundamental theorem gives \\(W_1=Vf\\). For \\(n\\geq2\\), the moving-endpoint formula in [Lemma 0.1(4) of the Cauchy lesson](cauchy-s-theorem-for-cycles-and-its-consequences.md#oa-fnd-ct-07) gives \\(W_n'=W_{n-1}\\) and \\(W_n(0)=0\\). Thus \\(W_n=VW_{n-1}\\) by the fundamental theorem, and induction gives \\(V^nf=W_n\\). So \\(\\|V^n\\|=1/n!\\), with equality at \\(f\\equiv1\\) and \\(t=1\\). Since at least half of the factors of \\(n!\\) are at least \\(n/2\\), \\((n!)^{1/n}\\geq(n/2)^{1/2}\\to\\infty\\). So \\(r(V)=0\\). The spectrum is not empty (Theorem 5.2) and lies in \\(\\{0\\}\\), so \\(\\sigma(V)=\\{0\\}\\), although \\(V\\neq0\\).\n- *Commutativity is needed in (3).* In \\(M_2(\\mathbb C)\\), \\(E_{12}\\) and \\(E_{21}\\) square to \\(0\\), so each has spectral radius \\(0\\). But \\(E_{12}+E_{21}\\) has eigenvalues \\(\\pm1\\), and \\(E_{12}E_{21}=E_{11}\\) has eigenvalues \\(0,1\\); both have spectral radius \\(1\\).\n- *Self-adjoint elements of a C\\*-algebra.* If \\(x=x^*\\), then \\(\\|x^2\\|=\\|x^*x\\|=\\|x\\|^2\\), so \\(\\|x^{2^k}\\|=\\|x\\|^{2^k}\\) for all \\(k\\), and (5.1) along the subsequence \\(2^k\\) gives \\(r(x)=\\|x\\|\\). The same holds for normal elements; this is proved in the [next lesson, on C\\*-algebras](c-star-algebras-continuous-functional-calculus-automatic-continuity-positive-cones.md#oa-fnd-cf-02).\n\n**Remark 5.8** (at most one C\\*-norm). A \\(*\\)-algebra carries at most one norm that makes it a C\\*-algebra. Indeed, let \\(N\\) be such a norm and \\(u\\) an element. Since \\(u^*u\\) is self-adjoint, the last example gives \\(N(u)^2=N(u^*u)=r(u^*u)\\). The spectral radius \\(r(u^*u)\\) is determined by the quasi-spectrum of \\(u^*u\\), which is defined algebraically and does not depend on the norm. In particular, for a nonunital C\\*-algebra \\(A\\), the C\\*-norm \\(p\\) on \\(A_1\\) of [Proposition 3.4](#oa-fnd-bn-05) is the only norm that makes \\(A_1\\) a C\\*-algebra.\n\n## 6. The holomorphic functional calculus\n\nFor a polynomial \\(p\\), the element \\(p(x)\\) makes sense in every unital algebra. This section defines \\(f(x)\\) for every function \\(f\\) that is holomorphic on a neighbourhood of \\(\\sigma_A(x)\\), by Cauchy's integral formula with the resolvent in place of \\((\\lambda-z)^{-1}\\). It then proves that \\(f\\mapsto f(x)\\) is a homomorphism, and that the spectrum of \\(f(x)\\) is \\(f(\\sigma_A(x))\\).\n\n### Paths, cycles and integrals\n\nA *path* is a piecewise continuously differentiable map \\(\\gamma:[a,b]\\to\\mathbb C\\). It is *closed* if \\(\\gamma(a)=\\gamma(b)\\). A *cycle* \\(\\Gamma\\) is a finite family of closed paths \\(\\gamma_1,\\dots,\\gamma_m\\), read as a formal sum. \\(\\Gamma^*\\) is the union of their images, a compact set, and \\(\\ell(\\Gamma)\\) is the total length. For a continuous map \\(F\\) from \\(\\Gamma^*\\) into a Banach space \\(X\\),\n\\[\n\\int_\\Gamma F(\\lambda)\\,d\\lambda=\\sum_{k=1}^m\\int_{a_k}^{b_k}F(\\gamma_k(t))\\,\\gamma_k'(t)\\,dt .\n\\]\nEach term is the Riemann integral of a piecewise continuous \\(X\\)-valued function on an interval. It exists for the same reason as for scalar functions: uniform continuity makes the Riemann sums a Cauchy net, and \\(X\\) is complete. The integral is linear in \\(F\\), satisfies \\(\\|\\int_\\Gamma F\\|\\leq\\ell(\\Gamma)\\sup_{\\Gamma^*}\\|F\\|\\), and commutes with bounded linear maps: \\(T\\int_\\Gamma F=\\int_\\Gamma T\\circ F\\). This applies to functionals, and to multiplication on either side by a fixed element of a Banach algebra. Uniform limits pass through the integral. For a continuous \\(F\\) on \\(\\Gamma_1^*\\times\\Gamma_2^*\\) the two iterated integrals agree: apply a functional, use the scalar statement for continuous functions on rectangles, and then the Hahn–Banach theorem. The *index* of a point \\(z\\notin\\Gamma^*\\) is\n\\[\n\\operatorname{Ind}_\\Gamma(z)=\\frac1{2\\pi i}\\int_\\Gamma\\frac{d\\lambda}{\\lambda-z}.\n\\]\nA cycle \\(\\Gamma\\) *surrounds* a compact set \\(K\\) in an open set \\(U\\supseteq K\\) if \\(\\Gamma^*\\subseteq U\\setminus K\\), \\(\\operatorname{Ind}_\\Gamma=1\\) on \\(K\\), and \\(\\operatorname{Ind}_\\Gamma=0\\) on \\(\\mathbb C\\setminus U\\). The empty cycle surrounds the empty set.\n\nWe use two facts proved in [Cauchy's theorem for cycles and its consequences](cauchy-s-theorem-for-cycles-and-its-consequences.md), cited as *the lesson on Cauchy's theorem*.\n\n**Theorem 6.1** (Cauchy's theorem). Let \\(U\\subseteq\\mathbb C\\) be open and let \\(\\Gamma\\) be a cycle in \\(U\\) with \\(\\operatorname{Ind}_\\Gamma(\\alpha)=0\\) for every \\(\\alpha\\notin U\\). Let \\(g:U\\to X\\) be a map into a Banach space such that \\(\\varphi\\circ g\\) is holomorphic for every \\(\\varphi\\in X^*\\). Then \\(g\\) is continuous, \\(\\int_\\Gamma g(\\lambda)\\,d\\lambda=0\\), and\n\\[\n\\begin{gathered}\ng(z)\\operatorname{Ind}_\\Gamma(z)\\\\\n=\\frac1{2\\pi i}\\int_\\Gamma\\frac{g(\\lambda)}{\\lambda-z}\\,d\\lambda\\\\\n(z\\in U\\setminus\\Gamma^*).\n\\end{gathered}\n\\]\n\nThis is Theorem 6.1(1)–(2) of the lesson on Cauchy's theorem. Its scalar case is Theorem 4.2 there. Applying a functional \\(\\varphi\\in X^*\\) reduces both formulas to the scalar case, and the Hahn–Banach theorem then gives them in \\(X\\).\n\nWe use the formula only at points of index \\(1\\) or \\(0\\). At a point \\(z\\) of index \\(0\\) it is the first conclusion, applied to \\(\\lambda\\mapsto g(\\lambda)/(\\lambda-z)\\) on \\(U\\setminus\\{z\\}\\). Applied on a disc, the formula gives the Taylor expansion of a holomorphic function at each point, in the usual way.\n\n**Theorem 6.2** (surrounding cycles). If \\(K\\subseteq U\\subseteq\\mathbb C\\) with \\(K\\) compact and \\(U\\) open, some cycle made of finitely many oriented segments surrounds \\(K\\) in \\(U\\).\n\nThis is Theorem 5.1 of the lesson on Cauchy's theorem.\n\n**Lemma 6.3** (index). Let \\(\\Gamma\\) be a cycle. On \\(\\mathbb C\\setminus\\Gamma^*\\), the function \\(\\operatorname{Ind}_\\Gamma\\) takes integer values, is constant on each connected component, and is \\(0\\) on the unbounded component. For the positively oriented circle \\(|\\lambda-c|=\\rho\\), the index is \\(1\\) on the open disc and \\(0\\) outside the closed disc.\n\n**Proof.** Let \\(\\gamma:[a,b]\\to\\mathbb C\\) be a closed path and \\(z\\notin\\gamma([a,b])\\). Put \\(h(t)=\\int_a^t\\gamma'(s)/(\\gamma(s)-z)\\,ds\\). The continuous function \\(F(t)=e^{-h(t)}(\\gamma(t)-z)\\) has derivative \\(e^{-h}\\big(-h'(\\gamma-z)+\\gamma'\\big)=0\\) wherever \\(\\gamma\\) is differentiable, so \\(F\\) is constant. Since \\(\\gamma(b)=\\gamma(a)\\neq z\\), \\(F(b)=F(a)\\) gives \\(e^{-h(b)}=1\\), so \\(h(b)/(2\\pi i)\\) is an integer. The index of a cycle is a sum of such integers. For \\(z,w\\notin\\Gamma^*\\), with \\(d(\\cdot)=\\operatorname{dist}(\\cdot,\\Gamma^*)\\),\n\\[\n|\\operatorname{Ind}_\\Gamma(z)-\\operatorname{Ind}_\\Gamma(w)|\\leq\\frac{\\ell(\\Gamma)\\,|z-w|}{2\\pi\\,d(z)\\,d(w)} .\n\\]\nSo \\(\\operatorname{Ind}_\\Gamma\\) is continuous and integer-valued, hence constant on components. Also \\(|\\operatorname{Ind}_\\Gamma(z)|\\leq\\ell(\\Gamma)/(2\\pi d(z))<1\\) when \\(d(z)\\) is large, so the index is \\(0\\) far out, hence on the whole unbounded component. For the circle, the index at the centre is \\(\\frac1{2\\pi i}\\int_0^{2\\pi}\\frac{i\\rho e^{it}}{\\rho e^{it}}\\,dt=1\\). \\(\\square\\)\n\nTwo sets built from a cycle \\(\\Gamma\\) that surrounds a compact set \\(K\\) in an open set \\(U\\) are used below:\n\\[\n\\begin{gathered}\nO_\\Gamma\\\\\n=\\{z\\notin\\Gamma^*:\\operatorname{Ind}_\\Gamma(z)=1\\},\\\\\nK_\\Gamma\\\\\n=\\Gamma^*\\cup\\{z\\notin\\Gamma^*:\\operatorname{Ind}_\\Gamma(z)\\neq0\\}.\n\\end{gathered}\n\\]\nBy Lemma 6.3, \\(O_\\Gamma\\) is open, and it contains \\(K\\). The set \\(K_\\Gamma\\) is closed, since its complement is the open set where the index is \\(0\\), and bounded, since the index is \\(0\\) on the unbounded component; so \\(K_\\Gamma\\) is compact. Both sets lie in \\(U\\), because \\(\\Gamma^*\\subseteq U\\) and the index is \\(0\\) off \\(U\\).","html":"<h3>The spectral radius formula<\/h3>\n<p><strong>Theorem 5.4<\/strong> (spectral radius formula). For every element <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(x\\)\">\\(x\\)<\/span> of a Banach algebra <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(A\\)\">\\(A\\)<\/span>,\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n\\begin{gathered}\nr(x)\\\\\n=\\lim_{n\\to\\infty}\\|x^n\\|^{1/n}\\\\\n=\\inf_{n\\geq1}\\|x^n\\|^{1/n}.\n\\end{gathered}\n\\tag{5.1}\n\\]\">\\[\n\\begin{gathered}\nr(x)\\\\\n=\\lim_{n\\to\\infty}\\|x^n\\|^{1/n}\\\\\n=\\inf_{n\\geq1}\\|x^n\\|^{1/n}.\n\\end{gathered}\n\\tag{5.1}\n\\]<\/span><\/p>\n<p>Step 1 below gives the lower spectral-radius bound directly, before any limit of roots is known to exist. Remark 5.5 supplies a second proof using submultiplicativity.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Proof.<\/strong> By Proposition 4.2(1) and <a title=\"Reference location unresolved in this edition\" data-unresolved-reference=\"#oa-fnd-bn-04\">Proposition 3.3(2)<\/a> we may compute in <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(A_1\\)\">\\(A_1\\)<\/span>, where <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\|j(x)^n\\|_1=\\|x^n\\|\\)\">\\(\\|j(x)^n\\|_1=\\|x^n\\|\\)<\/span>. So let <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(A\\)\">\\(A\\)<\/span> be unital. If <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(A=\\{0\\}\\)\">\\(A=\\{0\\}\\)<\/span>, both sides are <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(0\\)\">\\(0\\)<\/span>, since <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\sigma&#x27;_A(x)=\\{0\\}\\)\">\\(\\sigma&#x27;_A(x)=\\{0\\}\\)<\/span>. Let <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(A\\)\">\\(A\\)<\/span> be nontrivial.<\/p>\n<p><em>Step 1: <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(r(x)\\leq\\|x^n\\|^{1/n}\\)\">\\(r(x)\\leq\\|x^n\\|^{1/n}\\)<\/span> for every <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(n\\geq1\\)\">\\(n\\geq1\\)<\/span>.<\/em> Let <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\lambda\\in\\sigma_A(x)\\)\">\\(\\lambda\\in\\sigma_A(x)\\)<\/span>. In <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(A\\)\">\\(A\\)<\/span>,\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n\\begin{gathered}\nx^n-\\lambda^n\\\\\n=(x-\\lambda)p(x)\\\\\n=p(x)(x-\\lambda),\\\\\np(x)\\\\\n=\\sum_{k=0}^{n-1}\\lambda^{n-1-k}x^k .\n\\end{gathered}\n\\]\">\\[\n\\begin{gathered}\nx^n-\\lambda^n\\\\\n=(x-\\lambda)p(x)\\\\\n=p(x)(x-\\lambda),\\\\\np(x)\\\\\n=\\sum_{k=0}^{n-1}\\lambda^{n-1-k}x^k .\n\\end{gathered}\n\\]<\/span>\nIf <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(x^n-\\lambda^n\\)\">\\(x^n-\\lambda^n\\)<\/span> had an inverse <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(w\\)\">\\(w\\)<\/span>, then <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(p(x)w\\)\">\\(p(x)w\\)<\/span> would be a right inverse and <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(wp(x)\\)\">\\(wp(x)\\)<\/span> a left inverse of <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(x-\\lambda\\)\">\\(x-\\lambda\\)<\/span>, which is not invertible. So <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\lambda^n\\in\\sigma_A(x^n)\\)\">\\(\\lambda^n\\in\\sigma_A(x^n)\\)<\/span>, and <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(|\\lambda|^n\\leq\\|x^n\\|\\)\">\\(|\\lambda|^n\\leq\\|x^n\\|\\)<\/span> because the spectrum of <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(x^n\\)\">\\(x^n\\)<\/span> lies in the disc of radius <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\|x^n\\|\\)\">\\(\\|x^n\\|\\)<\/span> (<a title=\"Reference location unresolved in this edition\" data-unresolved-reference=\"#oa-fnd-bn-07\">Proposition 4.4(1)<\/a>). Take the supremum over <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\lambda\\)\">\\(\\lambda\\)<\/span>.<\/p>\n<p><em>Step 2: <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\limsup_n\\|x^n\\|^{1/n}\\leq r(x)\\)\">\\(\\limsup_n\\|x^n\\|^{1/n}\\leq r(x)\\)<\/span>.<\/em> Fix <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(n\\geq0\\)\">\\(n\\geq0\\)<\/span> and <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\varphi\\in A^*\\)\">\\(\\varphi\\in A^*\\)<\/span>. Since <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\sigma_A(x)\\)\">\\(\\sigma_A(x)\\)<\/span> lies in the closed disc of radius <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(r(x)\\)\">\\(r(x)\\)<\/span>, the function <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(g(\\lambda)=\\lambda^{n+1}\\varphi(R_x(\\lambda))\\)\">\\(g(\\lambda)=\\lambda^{n+1}\\varphi(R_x(\\lambda))\\)<\/span> is holomorphic with a continuous derivative on <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\{|\\lambda|&gt;r(x)\\}\\)\">\\(\\{|\\lambda|&gt;r(x)\\}\\)<\/span> (Proposition 4.4(5)). For <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\rho&gt;\\|x\\|\\)\">\\(\\rho&gt;\\|x\\|\\)<\/span> the series (4.1) converges uniformly on <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(|\\lambda|=\\rho\\)\">\\(|\\lambda|=\\rho\\)<\/span>, and integrating term by term gives\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n\\begin{gathered}\n\\frac1{2\\pi}\\int_0^{2\\pi}g(\\rho e^{i\\theta})\\,d\\theta\\\\\n=\\sum_{k\\geq0}\\varphi(x^k)\\,\\frac1{2\\pi}\\int_0^{2\\pi}(\\rho e^{i\\theta})^{n-k}\\,d\\theta\\\\\n=\\varphi(x^n).\n\\end{gathered}\n\\]\">\\[\n\\begin{gathered}\n\\frac1{2\\pi}\\int_0^{2\\pi}g(\\rho e^{i\\theta})\\,d\\theta\\\\\n=\\sum_{k\\geq0}\\varphi(x^k)\\,\\frac1{2\\pi}\\int_0^{2\\pi}(\\rho e^{i\\theta})^{n-k}\\,d\\theta\\\\\n=\\varphi(x^n).\n\\end{gathered}\n\\]<\/span>\nBy Lemma 5.1, the left side is the same for every <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\rho&gt;r(x)\\)\">\\(\\rho&gt;r(x)\\)<\/span>. So <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(|\\varphi(x^n)|\\leq\\rho^{n+1}m(\\rho)\\|\\varphi\\|\\)\">\\(|\\varphi(x^n)|\\leq\\rho^{n+1}m(\\rho)\\|\\varphi\\|\\)<\/span> for every <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\rho&gt;r(x)\\)\">\\(\\rho&gt;r(x)\\)<\/span>, where <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(m(\\rho)=\\max_{|\\lambda|=\\rho}\\|R_x(\\lambda)\\|\\)\">\\(m(\\rho)=\\max_{|\\lambda|=\\rho}\\|R_x(\\lambda)\\|\\)<\/span> is finite because <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(R_x\\)\">\\(R_x\\)<\/span> is continuous. By the Hahn–Banach theorem, <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\|x^n\\|\\leq\\rho^{n+1}m(\\rho)\\)\">\\(\\|x^n\\|\\leq\\rho^{n+1}m(\\rho)\\)<\/span> for all <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(n\\)\">\\(n\\)<\/span>. Hence <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\limsup_n\\|x^n\\|^{1/n}\\leq\\rho\\)\">\\(\\limsup_n\\|x^n\\|^{1/n}\\leq\\rho\\)<\/span>, and we let <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\rho\\)\">\\(\\rho\\)<\/span> decrease to <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(r(x)\\)\">\\(r(x)\\)<\/span>.<\/p>\n<p>Steps 1 and 2 give <span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n\\begin{gathered}\nr(x)\\\\\n\\leq\\inf_n\\|x^n\\|^{1/n}\\\\\n\\leq\\liminf_n\\|x^n\\|^{1/n}\\\\\n\\leq\\limsup_n\\|x^n\\|^{1/n}\\\\\n\\leq r(x).\n\\end{gathered}\n\\]\">\\[\n\\begin{gathered}\nr(x)\\\\\n\\leq\\inf_n\\|x^n\\|^{1/n}\\\\\n\\leq\\liminf_n\\|x^n\\|^{1/n}\\\\\n\\leq\\limsup_n\\|x^n\\|^{1/n}\\\\\n\\leq r(x).\n\\end{gathered}\n\\]<\/span> <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\square\\)\">\\(\\square\\)<\/span><\/p>\n<p><strong>Remark 5.5.<\/strong> Submultiplicativity alone shows that the limit in (5.1) exists. Here is the elementary argument often called Fekete’s lemma in this multiplicative form. Put <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(s_n=\\|x^n\\|\\)\">\\(s_n=\\|x^n\\|\\)<\/span>. If some <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(s_k=0\\)\">\\(s_k=0\\)<\/span>, all powers from <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(k\\)\">\\(k\\)<\/span> onwards are zero and the limit is zero. Otherwise fix <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(k\\)\">\\(k\\)<\/span>, write <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(n=qk+r\\)\">\\(n=qk+r\\)<\/span> with <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(0\\leq r&lt;k\\)\">\\(0\\leq r&lt;k\\)<\/span>, and put <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(C_k=\\max(1,s_1,\\ldots,s_{k-1})\\)\">\\(C_k=\\max(1,s_1,\\ldots,s_{k-1})\\)<\/span>, with <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(C_1=1\\)\">\\(C_1=1\\)<\/span>. Submultiplicativity gives <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(s_n\\leq C_ks_k^q\\)\">\\(s_n\\leq C_ks_k^q\\)<\/span>, including <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(r=0\\)\">\\(r=0\\)<\/span> without an identity factor. Hence <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\limsup_ns_n^{1/n}\\leq s_k^{1/k}\\)\">\\(\\limsup_ns_n^{1/n}\\leq s_k^{1/k}\\)<\/span>. Taking the infimum over <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(k\\)\">\\(k\\)<\/span>, and noting that every <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(s_n^{1/n}\\)\">\\(s_n^{1/n}\\)<\/span> is at least that infimum, proves convergence to <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\inf_{k\\geq1}s_k^{1/k}\\)\">\\(\\inf_{k\\geq1}s_k^{1/k}\\)<\/span>. The content of Theorem 5.4 is that this limit is the spectral radius.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Corollary 5.6.<\/strong><\/p>\n<ol>\n<li><span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(r(x^k)=r(x)^k\\)\">\\(r(x^k)=r(x)^k\\)<\/span> and <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(r(cx)=|c|\\,r(x)\\)\">\\(r(cx)=|c|\\,r(x)\\)<\/span>.<\/li>\n<li><span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(r(xy)=r(yx)\\)\">\\(r(xy)=r(yx)\\)<\/span> for all <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(x,y\\)\">\\(x,y\\)<\/span>.<\/li>\n<li>If <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(xy=yx\\)\">\\(xy=yx\\)<\/span>, then <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(r(xy)\\leq r(x)r(y)\\)\">\\(r(xy)\\leq r(x)r(y)\\)<\/span> and <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(r(x+y)\\leq r(x)+r(y)\\)\">\\(r(x+y)\\leq r(x)+r(y)\\)<\/span>.<\/li>\n<li>If <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(A\\)\">\\(A\\)<\/span> is unital and <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(r(1-x)&lt;1\\)\">\\(r(1-x)&lt;1\\)<\/span>, then <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(x\\)\">\\(x\\)<\/span> is invertible, and the series (2.1) converges absolutely.<\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<p><strong>Proof.<\/strong> (1) follows from (5.1). (2) is Proposition 4.2(2). (3) <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\((xy)^n=x^ny^n\\)\">\\((xy)^n=x^ny^n\\)<\/span> gives the first bound. For the second, fix <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\varepsilon&gt;0\\)\">\\(\\varepsilon&gt;0\\)<\/span>. By (5.1) there is <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(C\\geq1\\)\">\\(C\\geq1\\)<\/span> with <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\|x^k\\|\\leq C(r(x)+\\varepsilon)^k\\)\">\\(\\|x^k\\|\\leq C(r(x)+\\varepsilon)^k\\)<\/span> and <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\|y^k\\|\\leq C(r(y)+\\varepsilon)^k\\)\">\\(\\|y^k\\|\\leq C(r(y)+\\varepsilon)^k\\)<\/span> for all <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(k\\geq0\\)\">\\(k\\geq0\\)<\/span>. The binomial theorem holds for commuting elements, and gives <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\|(x+y)^n\\|\\leq C^2(r(x)+r(y)+2\\varepsilon)^n\\)\">\\(\\|(x+y)^n\\|\\leq C^2(r(x)+r(y)+2\\varepsilon)^n\\)<\/span>. (4) Choose <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\rho\\)\">\\(\\rho\\)<\/span> with <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(r(1-x)&lt;\\rho&lt;1\\)\">\\(r(1-x)&lt;\\rho&lt;1\\)<\/span>. By (5.1), <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\|(1-x)^n\\|\\leq\\rho^n\\)\">\\(\\|(1-x)^n\\|\\leq\\rho^n\\)<\/span> for large <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(n\\)\">\\(n\\)<\/span>, so the series converges absolutely, and the proof of Proposition 2.1 applies. <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\square\\)\">\\(\\square\\)<\/span><\/p>\n<p><strong>Examples 5.7.<\/strong><\/p>\n<ul>\n<li>A nonzero nilpotent element <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(N\\)\">\\(N\\)<\/span> (<span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(N^m=0\\)\">\\(N^m=0\\)<\/span>) has <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(r(N)=0&lt;\\|N\\|\\)\">\\(r(N)=0&lt;\\|N\\|\\)<\/span>.<\/li>\n<li><em>The Volterra operator.<\/em> On <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(C([0,1])\\)\">\\(C([0,1])\\)<\/span> with the supremum norm, let <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\((Vf)(t)=\\int_0^tf(s)\\,ds\\)\">\\((Vf)(t)=\\int_0^tf(s)\\,ds\\)<\/span>. Put <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(W_n(t)=\\int_0^t(t-s)^{n-1}f(s)/(n-1)!\\,ds\\)\">\\(W_n(t)=\\int_0^t(t-s)^{n-1}f(s)/(n-1)!\\,ds\\)<\/span>. The fundamental theorem gives <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(W_1=Vf\\)\">\\(W_1=Vf\\)<\/span>. For <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(n\\geq2\\)\">\\(n\\geq2\\)<\/span>, the moving-endpoint formula in <a href=\"../courses/foundations-of-von-neumann-algebras/cauchy-s-theorem-for-cycles-and-its-consequences.html#oa-fnd-ct-07\">Lemma 0.1(4) of the Cauchy lesson<\/a> gives <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(W_n&#x27;=W_{n-1}\\)\">\\(W_n&#x27;=W_{n-1}\\)<\/span> and <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(W_n(0)=0\\)\">\\(W_n(0)=0\\)<\/span>. Thus <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(W_n=VW_{n-1}\\)\">\\(W_n=VW_{n-1}\\)<\/span> by the fundamental theorem, and induction gives <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(V^nf=W_n\\)\">\\(V^nf=W_n\\)<\/span>. So <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\|V^n\\|=1/n!\\)\">\\(\\|V^n\\|=1/n!\\)<\/span>, with equality at <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(f\\equiv1\\)\">\\(f\\equiv1\\)<\/span> and <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(t=1\\)\">\\(t=1\\)<\/span>. Since at least half of the factors of <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(n!\\)\">\\(n!\\)<\/span> are at least <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(n/2\\)\">\\(n/2\\)<\/span>, <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\((n!)^{1/n}\\geq(n/2)^{1/2}\\to\\infty\\)\">\\((n!)^{1/n}\\geq(n/2)^{1/2}\\to\\infty\\)<\/span>. So <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(r(V)=0\\)\">\\(r(V)=0\\)<\/span>. The spectrum is not empty (Theorem 5.2) and lies in <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\{0\\}\\)\">\\(\\{0\\}\\)<\/span>, so <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\sigma(V)=\\{0\\}\\)\">\\(\\sigma(V)=\\{0\\}\\)<\/span>, although <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(V\\neq0\\)\">\\(V\\neq0\\)<\/span>.<\/li>\n<li><em>Commutativity is needed in (3).<\/em> In <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(M_2(\\mathbb C)\\)\">\\(M_2(\\mathbb C)\\)<\/span>, <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(E_{12}\\)\">\\(E_{12}\\)<\/span> and <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(E_{21}\\)\">\\(E_{21}\\)<\/span> square to <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(0\\)\">\\(0\\)<\/span>, so each has spectral radius <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(0\\)\">\\(0\\)<\/span>. But <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(E_{12}+E_{21}\\)\">\\(E_{12}+E_{21}\\)<\/span> has eigenvalues <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\pm1\\)\">\\(\\pm1\\)<\/span>, and <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(E_{12}E_{21}=E_{11}\\)\">\\(E_{12}E_{21}=E_{11}\\)<\/span> has eigenvalues <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(0,1\\)\">\\(0,1\\)<\/span>; both have spectral radius <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(1\\)\">\\(1\\)<\/span>.<\/li>\n<li><em>Self-adjoint elements of a C*-algebra.<\/em> If <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(x=x^*\\)\">\\(x=x^*\\)<\/span>, then <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\|x^2\\|=\\|x^*x\\|=\\|x\\|^2\\)\">\\(\\|x^2\\|=\\|x^*x\\|=\\|x\\|^2\\)<\/span>, so <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\|x^{2^k}\\|=\\|x\\|^{2^k}\\)\">\\(\\|x^{2^k}\\|=\\|x\\|^{2^k}\\)<\/span> for all <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(k\\)\">\\(k\\)<\/span>, and (5.1) along the subsequence <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(2^k\\)\">\\(2^k\\)<\/span> gives <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(r(x)=\\|x\\|\\)\">\\(r(x)=\\|x\\|\\)<\/span>. The same holds for normal elements; this is proved in the <a href=\"../courses/foundations-of-von-neumann-algebras/c-star-algebras-continuous-functional-calculus-automatic-continuity-positive-cones.html#oa-fnd-cf-02\">next lesson, on C*-algebras<\/a>.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p><strong>Remark 5.8<\/strong> (at most one C*-norm). A <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(*\\)\">\\(*\\)<\/span>-algebra carries at most one norm that makes it a C*-algebra. Indeed, let <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(N\\)\">\\(N\\)<\/span> be such a norm and <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(u\\)\">\\(u\\)<\/span> an element. Since <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(u^*u\\)\">\\(u^*u\\)<\/span> is self-adjoint, the last example gives <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(N(u)^2=N(u^*u)=r(u^*u)\\)\">\\(N(u)^2=N(u^*u)=r(u^*u)\\)<\/span>. The spectral radius <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(r(u^*u)\\)\">\\(r(u^*u)\\)<\/span> is determined by the quasi-spectrum of <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(u^*u\\)\">\\(u^*u\\)<\/span>, which is defined algebraically and does not depend on the norm. In particular, for a nonunital C*-algebra <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(A\\)\">\\(A\\)<\/span>, the C*-norm <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(p\\)\">\\(p\\)<\/span> on <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(A_1\\)\">\\(A_1\\)<\/span> of <a title=\"Reference location unresolved in this edition\" data-unresolved-reference=\"#oa-fnd-bn-05\">Proposition 3.4<\/a> is the only norm that makes <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(A_1\\)\">\\(A_1\\)<\/span> a C*-algebra.<\/p>\n<h2>6. The holomorphic functional calculus<\/h2>\n<p>For a polynomial <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(p\\)\">\\(p\\)<\/span>, the element <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(p(x)\\)\">\\(p(x)\\)<\/span> makes sense in every unital algebra. This section defines <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(f(x)\\)\">\\(f(x)\\)<\/span> for every function <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(f\\)\">\\(f\\)<\/span> that is holomorphic on a neighbourhood of <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\sigma_A(x)\\)\">\\(\\sigma_A(x)\\)<\/span>, by Cauchy's integral formula with the resolvent in place of <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\((\\lambda-z)^{-1}\\)\">\\((\\lambda-z)^{-1}\\)<\/span>. It then proves that <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(f\\mapsto f(x)\\)\">\\(f\\mapsto f(x)\\)<\/span> is a homomorphism, and that the spectrum of <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(f(x)\\)\">\\(f(x)\\)<\/span> is <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(f(\\sigma_A(x))\\)\">\\(f(\\sigma_A(x))\\)<\/span>.<\/p>\n<h3>Paths, cycles and integrals<\/h3>\n<p>A <em>path<\/em> is a piecewise continuously differentiable map <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\gamma:[a,b]\\to\\mathbb C\\)\">\\(\\gamma:[a,b]\\to\\mathbb C\\)<\/span>. It is <em>closed<\/em> if <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\gamma(a)=\\gamma(b)\\)\">\\(\\gamma(a)=\\gamma(b)\\)<\/span>. A <em>cycle<\/em> <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\Gamma\\)\">\\(\\Gamma\\)<\/span> is a finite family of closed paths <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\gamma_1,\\dots,\\gamma_m\\)\">\\(\\gamma_1,\\dots,\\gamma_m\\)<\/span>, read as a formal sum. <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\Gamma^*\\)\">\\(\\Gamma^*\\)<\/span> is the union of their images, a compact set, and <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\ell(\\Gamma)\\)\">\\(\\ell(\\Gamma)\\)<\/span> is the total length. For a continuous map <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(F\\)\">\\(F\\)<\/span> from <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\Gamma^*\\)\">\\(\\Gamma^*\\)<\/span> into a Banach space <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(X\\)\">\\(X\\)<\/span>,\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n\\int_\\Gamma F(\\lambda)\\,d\\lambda=\\sum_{k=1}^m\\int_{a_k}^{b_k}F(\\gamma_k(t))\\,\\gamma_k&#x27;(t)\\,dt .\n\\]\">\\[\n\\int_\\Gamma F(\\lambda)\\,d\\lambda=\\sum_{k=1}^m\\int_{a_k}^{b_k}F(\\gamma_k(t))\\,\\gamma_k&#x27;(t)\\,dt .\n\\]<\/span>\nEach term is the Riemann integral of a piecewise continuous <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(X\\)\">\\(X\\)<\/span>-valued function on an interval. It exists for the same reason as for scalar functions: uniform continuity makes the Riemann sums a Cauchy net, and <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(X\\)\">\\(X\\)<\/span> is complete. The integral is linear in <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(F\\)\">\\(F\\)<\/span>, satisfies <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\|\\int_\\Gamma F\\|\\leq\\ell(\\Gamma)\\sup_{\\Gamma^*}\\|F\\|\\)\">\\(\\|\\int_\\Gamma F\\|\\leq\\ell(\\Gamma)\\sup_{\\Gamma^*}\\|F\\|\\)<\/span>, and commutes with bounded linear maps: <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(T\\int_\\Gamma F=\\int_\\Gamma T\\circ F\\)\">\\(T\\int_\\Gamma F=\\int_\\Gamma T\\circ F\\)<\/span>. This applies to functionals, and to multiplication on either side by a fixed element of a Banach algebra. Uniform limits pass through the integral. For a continuous <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(F\\)\">\\(F\\)<\/span> on <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\Gamma_1^*\\times\\Gamma_2^*\\)\">\\(\\Gamma_1^*\\times\\Gamma_2^*\\)<\/span> the two iterated integrals agree: apply a functional, use the scalar statement for continuous functions on rectangles, and then the Hahn–Banach theorem. The <em>index<\/em> of a point <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(z\\notin\\Gamma^*\\)\">\\(z\\notin\\Gamma^*\\)<\/span> is\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n\\operatorname{Ind}_\\Gamma(z)=\\frac1{2\\pi i}\\int_\\Gamma\\frac{d\\lambda}{\\lambda-z}.\n\\]\">\\[\n\\operatorname{Ind}_\\Gamma(z)=\\frac1{2\\pi i}\\int_\\Gamma\\frac{d\\lambda}{\\lambda-z}.\n\\]<\/span>\nA cycle <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\Gamma\\)\">\\(\\Gamma\\)<\/span> <em>surrounds<\/em> a compact set <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(K\\)\">\\(K\\)<\/span> in an open set <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(U\\supseteq K\\)\">\\(U\\supseteq K\\)<\/span> if <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\Gamma^*\\subseteq U\\setminus K\\)\">\\(\\Gamma^*\\subseteq U\\setminus K\\)<\/span>, <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\operatorname{Ind}_\\Gamma=1\\)\">\\(\\operatorname{Ind}_\\Gamma=1\\)<\/span> on <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(K\\)\">\\(K\\)<\/span>, and <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\operatorname{Ind}_\\Gamma=0\\)\">\\(\\operatorname{Ind}_\\Gamma=0\\)<\/span> on <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\mathbb C\\setminus U\\)\">\\(\\mathbb C\\setminus U\\)<\/span>. The empty cycle surrounds the empty set.<\/p>\n<p>We use two facts proved in <a href=\"../courses/foundations-of-von-neumann-algebras/cauchy-s-theorem-for-cycles-and-its-consequences.html\">Cauchy's theorem for cycles and its consequences<\/a>, cited as <em>the lesson on Cauchy's theorem<\/em>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Theorem 6.1<\/strong> (Cauchy's theorem). Let <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(U\\subseteq\\mathbb C\\)\">\\(U\\subseteq\\mathbb C\\)<\/span> be open and let <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\Gamma\\)\">\\(\\Gamma\\)<\/span> be a cycle in <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(U\\)\">\\(U\\)<\/span> with <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\operatorname{Ind}_\\Gamma(\\alpha)=0\\)\">\\(\\operatorname{Ind}_\\Gamma(\\alpha)=0\\)<\/span> for every <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\alpha\\notin U\\)\">\\(\\alpha\\notin U\\)<\/span>. Let <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(g:U\\to X\\)\">\\(g:U\\to X\\)<\/span> be a map into a Banach space such that <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\varphi\\circ g\\)\">\\(\\varphi\\circ g\\)<\/span> is holomorphic for every <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\varphi\\in X^*\\)\">\\(\\varphi\\in X^*\\)<\/span>. Then <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(g\\)\">\\(g\\)<\/span> is continuous, <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\int_\\Gamma g(\\lambda)\\,d\\lambda=0\\)\">\\(\\int_\\Gamma g(\\lambda)\\,d\\lambda=0\\)<\/span>, and\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n\\begin{gathered}\ng(z)\\operatorname{Ind}_\\Gamma(z)\\\\\n=\\frac1{2\\pi i}\\int_\\Gamma\\frac{g(\\lambda)}{\\lambda-z}\\,d\\lambda\\\\\n(z\\in U\\setminus\\Gamma^*).\n\\end{gathered}\n\\]\">\\[\n\\begin{gathered}\ng(z)\\operatorname{Ind}_\\Gamma(z)\\\\\n=\\frac1{2\\pi i}\\int_\\Gamma\\frac{g(\\lambda)}{\\lambda-z}\\,d\\lambda\\\\\n(z\\in U\\setminus\\Gamma^*).\n\\end{gathered}\n\\]<\/span><\/p>\n<p>This is Theorem 6.1(1)–(2) of the lesson on Cauchy's theorem. Its scalar case is Theorem 4.2 there. Applying a functional <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\varphi\\in X^*\\)\">\\(\\varphi\\in X^*\\)<\/span> reduces both formulas to the scalar case, and the Hahn–Banach theorem then gives them in <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(X\\)\">\\(X\\)<\/span>.<\/p>\n<p>We use the formula only at points of index <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(1\\)\">\\(1\\)<\/span> or <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(0\\)\">\\(0\\)<\/span>. At a point <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(z\\)\">\\(z\\)<\/span> of index <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(0\\)\">\\(0\\)<\/span> it is the first conclusion, applied to <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\lambda\\mapsto g(\\lambda)/(\\lambda-z)\\)\">\\(\\lambda\\mapsto g(\\lambda)/(\\lambda-z)\\)<\/span> on <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(U\\setminus\\{z\\}\\)\">\\(U\\setminus\\{z\\}\\)<\/span>. Applied on a disc, the formula gives the Taylor expansion of a holomorphic function at each point, in the usual way.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Theorem 6.2<\/strong> (surrounding cycles). If <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(K\\subseteq U\\subseteq\\mathbb C\\)\">\\(K\\subseteq U\\subseteq\\mathbb C\\)<\/span> with <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(K\\)\">\\(K\\)<\/span> compact and <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(U\\)\">\\(U\\)<\/span> open, some cycle made of finitely many oriented segments surrounds <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(K\\)\">\\(K\\)<\/span> in <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(U\\)\">\\(U\\)<\/span>.<\/p>\n<p>This is Theorem 5.1 of the lesson on Cauchy's theorem.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Lemma 6.3<\/strong> (index). Let <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\Gamma\\)\">\\(\\Gamma\\)<\/span> be a cycle. On <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\mathbb C\\setminus\\Gamma^*\\)\">\\(\\mathbb C\\setminus\\Gamma^*\\)<\/span>, the function <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\operatorname{Ind}_\\Gamma\\)\">\\(\\operatorname{Ind}_\\Gamma\\)<\/span> takes integer values, is constant on each connected component, and is <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(0\\)\">\\(0\\)<\/span> on the unbounded component. For the positively oriented circle <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(|\\lambda-c|=\\rho\\)\">\\(|\\lambda-c|=\\rho\\)<\/span>, the index is <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(1\\)\">\\(1\\)<\/span> on the open disc and <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(0\\)\">\\(0\\)<\/span> outside the closed disc.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Proof.<\/strong> Let <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\gamma:[a,b]\\to\\mathbb C\\)\">\\(\\gamma:[a,b]\\to\\mathbb C\\)<\/span> be a closed path and <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(z\\notin\\gamma([a,b])\\)\">\\(z\\notin\\gamma([a,b])\\)<\/span>. Put <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(h(t)=\\int_a^t\\gamma&#x27;(s)/(\\gamma(s)-z)\\,ds\\)\">\\(h(t)=\\int_a^t\\gamma&#x27;(s)/(\\gamma(s)-z)\\,ds\\)<\/span>. The continuous function <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(F(t)=e^{-h(t)}(\\gamma(t)-z)\\)\">\\(F(t)=e^{-h(t)}(\\gamma(t)-z)\\)<\/span> has derivative <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(e^{-h}\\big(-h&#x27;(\\gamma-z)+\\gamma&#x27;\\big)=0\\)\">\\(e^{-h}\\big(-h&#x27;(\\gamma-z)+\\gamma&#x27;\\big)=0\\)<\/span> wherever <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\gamma\\)\">\\(\\gamma\\)<\/span> is differentiable, so <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(F\\)\">\\(F\\)<\/span> is constant. Since <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\gamma(b)=\\gamma(a)\\neq z\\)\">\\(\\gamma(b)=\\gamma(a)\\neq z\\)<\/span>, <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(F(b)=F(a)\\)\">\\(F(b)=F(a)\\)<\/span> gives <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(e^{-h(b)}=1\\)\">\\(e^{-h(b)}=1\\)<\/span>, so <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(h(b)/(2\\pi i)\\)\">\\(h(b)/(2\\pi i)\\)<\/span> is an integer. The index of a cycle is a sum of such integers. For <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(z,w\\notin\\Gamma^*\\)\">\\(z,w\\notin\\Gamma^*\\)<\/span>, with <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(d(\\cdot)=\\operatorname{dist}(\\cdot,\\Gamma^*)\\)\">\\(d(\\cdot)=\\operatorname{dist}(\\cdot,\\Gamma^*)\\)<\/span>,\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n|\\operatorname{Ind}_\\Gamma(z)-\\operatorname{Ind}_\\Gamma(w)|\\leq\\frac{\\ell(\\Gamma)\\,|z-w|}{2\\pi\\,d(z)\\,d(w)} .\n\\]\">\\[\n|\\operatorname{Ind}_\\Gamma(z)-\\operatorname{Ind}_\\Gamma(w)|\\leq\\frac{\\ell(\\Gamma)\\,|z-w|}{2\\pi\\,d(z)\\,d(w)} .\n\\]<\/span>\nSo <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\operatorname{Ind}_\\Gamma\\)\">\\(\\operatorname{Ind}_\\Gamma\\)<\/span> is continuous and integer-valued, hence constant on components. Also <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(|\\operatorname{Ind}_\\Gamma(z)|\\leq\\ell(\\Gamma)/(2\\pi d(z))&lt;1\\)\">\\(|\\operatorname{Ind}_\\Gamma(z)|\\leq\\ell(\\Gamma)/(2\\pi d(z))&lt;1\\)<\/span> when <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(d(z)\\)\">\\(d(z)\\)<\/span> is large, so the index is <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(0\\)\">\\(0\\)<\/span> far out, hence on the whole unbounded component. For the circle, the index at the centre is <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\frac1{2\\pi i}\\int_0^{2\\pi}\\frac{i\\rho e^{it}}{\\rho e^{it}}\\,dt=1\\)\">\\(\\frac1{2\\pi i}\\int_0^{2\\pi}\\frac{i\\rho e^{it}}{\\rho e^{it}}\\,dt=1\\)<\/span>. <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\square\\)\">\\(\\square\\)<\/span><\/p>\n<p>Two sets built from a cycle <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\Gamma\\)\">\\(\\Gamma\\)<\/span> that surrounds a compact set <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(K\\)\">\\(K\\)<\/span> in an open set <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(U\\)\">\\(U\\)<\/span> are used below:\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n\\begin{gathered}\nO_\\Gamma\\\\\n=\\{z\\notin\\Gamma^*:\\operatorname{Ind}_\\Gamma(z)=1\\},\\\\\nK_\\Gamma\\\\\n=\\Gamma^*\\cup\\{z\\notin\\Gamma^*:\\operatorname{Ind}_\\Gamma(z)\\neq0\\}.\n\\end{gathered}\n\\]\">\\[\n\\begin{gathered}\nO_\\Gamma\\\\\n=\\{z\\notin\\Gamma^*:\\operatorname{Ind}_\\Gamma(z)=1\\},\\\\\nK_\\Gamma\\\\\n=\\Gamma^*\\cup\\{z\\notin\\Gamma^*:\\operatorname{Ind}_\\Gamma(z)\\neq0\\}.\n\\end{gathered}\n\\]<\/span>\nBy Lemma 6.3, <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(O_\\Gamma\\)\">\\(O_\\Gamma\\)<\/span> is open, and it contains <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(K\\)\">\\(K\\)<\/span>. The set <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(K_\\Gamma\\)\">\\(K_\\Gamma\\)<\/span> is closed, since its complement is the open set where the index is <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(0\\)\">\\(0\\)<\/span>, and bounded, since the index is <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(0\\)\">\\(0\\)<\/span> on the unbounded component; so <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(K_\\Gamma\\)\">\\(K_\\Gamma\\)<\/span> is compact. Both sets lie in <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(U\\)\">\\(U\\)<\/span>, because <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\Gamma^*\\subseteq U\\)\">\\(\\Gamma^*\\subseteq U\\)<\/span> and the index is <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(0\\)\">\\(0\\)<\/span> off <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(U\\)\">\\(U\\)<\/span>.<\/p>\n","source":"docs/courses/foundations-of-von-neumann-algebras/src/banach-algebras-spectrum-holomorphic-functional-calculus-and-gelfand-theory.md","start_offset":40088,"end_offset":51958,"role":"registered_statement_and_proof_passage"},"d7e87802c55bee1b3af2b3890de006286146cc63b55d1cae7d73a737101f1e94":{"markdown":"## 4. Trace-class operators and the trace\n\nAn operator is of trace class when its matrix entries along pairs of orthonormal families have uniformly bounded absolute sums. For a compact operator this is the summability of its singular values. The trace is defined on these operators and has the properties of the trace of a matrix.\n\n**Definition 4.1.** For \\(x\\in B(H)\\) let\n\\[\n\\begin{gathered}\n\\|x\\|_1\\\\\n=\\sup\\Big\\{\\sum_{j\\in J}|\\langle x\\xi_j,\\eta_j\\rangle|\\Big\\}\\in[0,\\infty],\n\\end{gathered}\n\\tag{4.1}\n\\]\nthe supremum over all finite sets \\(J\\) and all orthonormal families \\((\\xi_j)_{j\\in J}\\) and \\((\\eta_j)_{j\\in J}\\) in \\(H\\). The operators with \\(\\|x\\|_1<\\infty\\) are the *trace-class* (or *nuclear*) operators; they form the set \\(\\mathcal L^1(H)\\). Since the terms are nonnegative, the supremum does not change if infinite index sets \\(J\\) are allowed.\n\n**Lemma 4.2.**\n\n(a) \\(\\mathcal L^1(H)\\) is a linear subspace of \\(B(H)\\), \\(\\|\\cdot\\|_1\\) is a norm on it, \\(\\|x\\|\\le\\|x\\|_1\\), and \\(\\|x^*\\|_1=\\|x\\|_1\\).\n\n(b) \\(\\|\\theta_{\\xi,\\eta}\\|_1=\\|\\xi\\|\\,\\|\\eta\\|\\).\n\n(c) If \\((x_\\alpha)\\) is a net with \\(\\langle x_\\alpha\\xi,\\eta\\rangle\\to\\langle x\\xi,\\eta\\rangle\\) for all \\(\\xi,\\eta\\), then \\(\\|x\\|_1\\le\\liminf_\\alpha\\|x_\\alpha\\|_1\\).\n\n(d) \\((\\mathcal L^1(H),\\|\\cdot\\|_1)\\) is a Banach space.\n\n**Proof.** (a) The inequalities \\(\\|x+y\\|_1\\le\\|x\\|_1+\\|y\\|_1\\) and \\(\\|cx\\|_1=|c|\\,\\|x\\|_1\\) hold term by term. With one pair of unit vectors, \\(|\\langle x\\xi,\\eta\\rangle|\\le\\|x\\|_1\\); the supremum over \\(\\xi,\\eta\\) is \\(\\|x\\|\\). Since \\(|\\langle x^*\\xi_j,\\eta_j\\rangle|=|\\langle x\\eta_j,\\xi_j\\rangle|\\), passing to \\(x^*\\) exchanges the two families.\n\n(b) By Cauchy–Schwarz and Bessel's inequality,\n\\[\n\\begin{gathered}\n\\sum_j|\\langle\\theta_{\\xi,\\eta}\\xi_j,\\eta_j\\rangle|\\\\\n=\\sum_j|\\langle\\xi_j,\\eta\\rangle|\\,|\\langle\\xi,\\eta_j\\rangle|\\\\\n\\le\\Big(\\sum_j|\\langle\\xi_j,\\eta\\rangle|^2\\Big)^{1/2}\\Big(\\sum_j|\\langle\\eta_j,\\xi\\rangle|^2\\Big)^{1/2}\\\\\n\\le\\|\\eta\\|\\,\\|\\xi\\| .\n\\end{gathered}\n\\]\nIf \\(\\xi,\\eta\\ne0\\), the single pair \\(\\xi_1=\\eta/\\|\\eta\\|\\), \\(\\eta_1=\\xi/\\|\\xi\\|\\) gives equality.\n\n(c) For fixed finite families, \\[\n\\begin{gathered}\n\\sum_j|\\langle x\\xi_j,\\eta_j\\rangle|\\\\\n=\\lim_\\alpha\\sum_j|\\langle x_\\alpha\\xi_j,\\eta_j\\rangle|\\\\\n\\le\\liminf_\\alpha\\|x_\\alpha\\|_1.\n\\end{gathered}\n\\]\n\n(d) Let \\((x_k)\\) be Cauchy for \\(\\|\\cdot\\|_1\\). By (a) it is Cauchy in norm, so \\(x_k\\to x\\) in norm. For fixed \\(k\\), apply (c) to the sequence \\((x_m-x_k)_m\\), which converges in norm to \\(x-x_k\\): \\(\\|x-x_k\\|_1\\le\\sup_{m\\ge k}\\|x_m-x_k\\|_1\\), which tends to \\(0\\). So \\(x=(x-x_k)+x_k\\in\\mathcal L^1(H)\\) and \\(x_k\\to x\\) in \\(\\|\\cdot\\|_1\\). \\(\\square\\)\n\n**Theorem 4.3.**\n\n(a) \\(\\|x\\|_2^2\\le\\|x\\|\\,\\|x\\|_1\\) for every \\(x\\in B(H)\\). Hence \\(\\mathcal L^1(H)\\subseteq\\mathcal L^2(H)\\subseteq K(H)\\).\n\n(b) For \\(x\\in K(H)\\), \\(\\|x\\|_1=\\sum_ns_n(x)\\) in \\([0,\\infty]\\). So \\(x\\in\\mathcal L^1(H)\\) if and only if \\(x\\) is compact and \\(\\sum_ns_n(x)<\\infty\\).\n\n(c) If \\(x\\in\\mathcal L^1(H)\\), every decomposition (2.1) of \\(x\\) converges in \\(\\|\\cdot\\|_1\\).\n\n**Proof.** (a) We may assume \\(\\|x\\|_1<\\infty\\). Let \\(\\xi_1,\\dots,\\xi_m\\) be orthonormal, let \\(L\\) be their span and \\(p\\) the projection onto \\(L\\). The operator \\(px^*xp\\) is positive and maps \\(L\\) into \\(L\\), so \\(L\\) has an orthonormal basis \\(\\zeta_1,\\dots,\\zeta_m\\) with \\(px^*xp\\,\\zeta_k=\\sigma_k^2\\zeta_k\\), \\(\\sigma_k\\ge0\\). Then \\(\\langle x\\zeta_k,x\\zeta_l\\rangle=\\langle px^*xp\\,\\zeta_k,\\zeta_l\\rangle=\\sigma_k^2\\delta_{kl}\\): the vectors \\(x\\zeta_k\\) are orthogonal, with norms \\(\\sigma_k\\). Choose orthonormal \\(\\eta_1,\\dots,\\eta_m\\) with \\(x\\zeta_k=\\sigma_k\\eta_k\\): put \\(\\eta_k=\\sigma_k^{-1}x\\zeta_k\\) when \\(\\sigma_k>0\\), and complete by further orthonormal vectors, which is possible since \\(\\dim H\\ge m\\). Then\n\\[\n\\sum_k\\sigma_k=\\sum_k\\langle x\\zeta_k,\\eta_k\\rangle\\le\\|x\\|_1 .\n\\]\nApply Lemma 3.1 to \\(xp\\) and to the two orthonormal bases of \\(H\\) obtained by completing \\((\\xi_i)\\) and \\((\\zeta_k)\\) with one and the same orthonormal basis of \\(L^\\perp\\), on which \\(xp\\) vanishes. This gives \\(\\sum_i\\|x\\xi_i\\|^2=\\sum_k\\|x\\zeta_k\\|^2=\\sum_k\\sigma_k^2\\). Since \\(\\sigma_k\\le\\|x\\|\\),\n\\[\n\\sum_{i\\le m}\\|x\\xi_i\\|^2=\\sum_k\\sigma_k^2\\le\\|x\\|\\sum_k\\sigma_k\\le\\|x\\|\\,\\|x\\|_1 .\n\\]\nEvery finite subfamily of an orthonormal basis is such a family, so \\(\\|x\\|_2^2\\le\\|x\\|\\,\\|x\\|_1\\). The inclusion \\(\\mathcal L^2(H)\\subseteq K(H)\\) is Theorem 3.3(b).\n\n(b) Let \\(x\\in K(H)\\), \\(x\\ne0\\), with decomposition (2.1), and let \\((\\xi_j)\\), \\((\\eta_j)\\) be finite orthonormal families. Since \\(\\langle x\\xi_j,\\eta_j\\rangle=\\sum_ns_n\\langle\\xi_j,e_n\\rangle\\langle f_n,\\eta_j\\rangle\\),\n\\[\n\\begin{gathered}\n\\sum_j|\\langle x\\xi_j,\\eta_j\\rangle|\\\\\n\\le\\sum_ns_n\\sum_j|\\langle\\xi_j,e_n\\rangle|\\,|\\langle f_n,\\eta_j\\rangle|\\\\\n\\le\\sum_ns_n\\Big(\\sum_j|\\langle e_n,\\xi_j\\rangle|^2\\Big)^{1/2}\\\\\n{}\\cdot\\Big(\\sum_j|\\langle f_n,\\eta_j\\rangle|^2\\Big)^{1/2}\\\\\n\\le\\sum_ns_n\n\\end{gathered}\n\\]\nby Cauchy–Schwarz and Bessel's inequality. So \\(\\|x\\|_1\\le\\sum_ns_n\\). Conversely, the families \\(\\xi_j=e_j\\), \\(\\eta_j=f_j\\), \\(j\\le M\\), give \\(\\sum_{j\\le M}\\langle xe_j,f_j\\rangle=\\sum_{j\\le M}s_j\\), so \\(\\|x\\|_1\\ge\\sum_{j\\le M}s_j\\) for every \\(M\\). Together with (a), which shows that a trace-class operator is compact, this proves (b).\n\n(c) The remainder \\(\\sum_{n>M}s_n\\theta_{f_n,e_n}\\) is compact and has a decomposition of the form (2.1) with the numbers \\(s_{M+1},s_{M+2},\\dots\\). By (b) its trace norm is \\(\\sum_{n>M}s_n\\), which tends to \\(0\\). \\(\\square\\)\n\n**Corollary 4.4.**\n\n(a) If \\(x\\in\\mathcal L^1(H)\\) and \\(a,b\\in B(H)\\), then \\(axb\\in\\mathcal L^1(H)\\) and \\(\\|axb\\|_1\\le\\|a\\|\\,\\|x\\|_1\\,\\|b\\|\\). So \\(\\mathcal L^1(H)\\) is a two-sided ideal of \\(B(H)\\), closed under adjoints.\n\n(b) \\(x\\in\\mathcal L^1(H)\\) if and only if \\(|x|\\in\\mathcal L^1(H)\\), if and only if \\(x^*\\in\\mathcal L^1(H)\\), and \\(\\|x\\|_1=\\||x|\\|_1=\\|x^*\\|_1\\).\n\n(c) \\(F(H)\\subseteq\\mathcal L^1(H)\\), and \\(F(H)\\) is dense in \\(\\mathcal L^1(H)\\) for \\(\\|\\cdot\\|_1\\). For \\(x\\in\\mathcal L^1(H)\\), \\(\\|x\\|\\le\\|x\\|_2\\le\\|x\\|_1\\).\n\n(d) If \\(\\sum_n\\|\\xi_n\\|\\,\\|\\eta_n\\|<\\infty\\), the series \\(\\sum_n\\theta_{\\xi_n,\\eta_n}\\) converges in \\(\\|\\cdot\\|_1\\), and its sum \\(t\\) satisfies \\(\\|t\\|_1\\le\\sum_n\\|\\xi_n\\|\\,\\|\\eta_n\\|\\).\n\n**Proof.** (a) follows from Proposition 2.5(a) and Theorem 4.3(b). (b) By Lemma 4.2(a), \\(x^*\\in\\mathcal L^1(H)\\) exactly when \\(x\\in\\mathcal L^1(H)\\), and \\(\\|x^*\\|_1=\\|x\\|_1\\). If \\(x\\in\\mathcal L^1(H)\\), then \\(x\\) is compact by Theorem 4.3(a). If \\(|x|\\in\\mathcal L^1(H)\\), then \\(x\\) is compact too: \\(\\|x\\zeta\\|^2=\\langle x^*x\\zeta,\\zeta\\rangle=\\||x|\\zeta\\|^2\\) for every \\(\\zeta\\), so \\(\\|x\\|_2=\\||x|\\|_2\\), which is finite by Theorem 4.3(a), and \\(\\mathcal L^2(H)\\subseteq K(H)\\) by Theorem 3.3(b). For compact \\(x\\), Proposition 2.5(c) and Theorem 4.3(b) give \\(\\|x\\|_1=\\||x|\\|_1\\) in \\([0,\\infty]\\). (c) A finite-rank operator has only finitely many nonzero singular values. Density follows from Theorem 4.3(c). For \\(x\\in\\mathcal L^1(H)\\), \\(\\|x\\|_2^2=\\sum s_n^2\\le(\\sum s_n)^2\\). (d) By Lemma 4.2(b) the series converges absolutely in the Banach space \\(\\mathcal L^1(H)\\). \\(\\square\\)\n\n**Example 4.5** (three different norms). Let \\(\\xi\\perp\\eta\\) be unit vectors and \\(x=\\theta_{\\xi,\\eta}+\\theta_{\\eta,\\xi}\\). Then \\(x(\\xi\\pm\\eta)=\\pm(\\xi\\pm\\eta)\\) and \\(x\\) vanishes on \\(\\{\\xi,\\eta\\}^\\perp\\). So \\(x\\) is self-adjoint with eigenvalues \\(\\pm1\\), and \\(s(x)=(1,1,0,0,\\dots)\\). Hence \\(\\|x\\|=1\\), \\(\\|x\\|_2=\\sqrt2\\), \\(\\|x\\|_1=2\\), and \\(\\operatorname{Tr}(x)=0\\) (Theorem 4.6). For the diagonal operators of Example 2.2, Example 2.6 shows that \\(d_\\lambda\\in\\mathcal L^2(H)\\) exactly when \\(\\lambda\\in\\ell^2(I)\\) and \\(d_\\lambda\\in\\mathcal L^1(H)\\) exactly when \\(\\lambda\\in\\ell^1(I)\\), with \\(\\|d_\\lambda\\|_2=\\|\\lambda\\|_2\\) and \\(\\|d_\\lambda\\|_1=\\|\\lambda\\|_1\\). The Volterra operator of Example 3.5 lies in \\(\\mathcal L^2(H)\\) but not in \\(\\mathcal L^1(H)\\).\n\n**Theorem 4.6** (the trace). Let \\(x\\in\\mathcal L^1(H)\\) and let \\((\\varepsilon_i)_{i\\in I}\\) be an orthonormal basis. Then \\(\\sum_i\\langle x\\varepsilon_i,\\varepsilon_i\\rangle\\) converges absolutely, \\(\\sum_i|\\langle x\\varepsilon_i,\\varepsilon_i\\rangle|\\le\\|x\\|_1\\), and the sum, denoted \\(\\operatorname{Tr}(x)\\), does not depend on the basis. More precisely, if \\(x=\\sum_n\\theta_{\\xi_n,\\eta_n}\\) with \\(\\sum_n\\|\\xi_n\\|\\,\\|\\eta_n\\|<\\infty\\), as in (2.1), then\n\\[\n\\operatorname{Tr}(x)=\\sum_n\\langle\\xi_n,\\eta_n\\rangle .\n\\tag{4.2}\n\\]\nThe trace is a linear functional on \\(\\mathcal L^1(H)\\) with \\(|\\operatorname{Tr}(x)|\\le\\|x\\|_1\\), \\(\\operatorname{Tr}(x^*)=\\overline{\\operatorname{Tr}(x)}\\), \\(\\operatorname{Tr}(x)\\ge0\\) for \\(x\\ge0\\), and \\(\\operatorname{Tr}(\\theta_{\\xi,\\eta})=\\langle\\xi,\\eta\\rangle\\). For \\(a\\in B(H)\\),\n\\[\n\\begin{gathered}\n\\operatorname{Tr}(ax)\\\\\n=\\operatorname{Tr}(xa),\\\\\n|\\operatorname{Tr}(ax)|\\\\\n\\le\\|a\\|\\,\\|x\\|_1,\n\\end{gathered}\n\\tag{4.3}\n\\]\nand in particular\n\\[\n\\operatorname{Tr}(a\\,\\theta_{\\xi,\\eta})=\\langle a\\xi,\\eta\\rangle=\\omega_{\\xi,\\eta}(a).\n\\tag{4.4}\n\\]\nFinally \\(\\|x\\|_1=\\operatorname{Tr}(|x|)\\).\n\n**Proof.** Taking \\(\\xi_j=\\eta_j=\\varepsilon_j\\) over finite subsets in (4.1) gives absolute convergence and the bound. Let \\(x=\\sum_n\\theta_{\\xi_n,\\eta_n}\\) with \\(\\sum\\|\\xi_n\\|\\,\\|\\eta_n\\|<\\infty\\); the series converges in \\(\\|\\cdot\\|_1\\) by Corollary 4.4(d), hence in norm. Then \\(\\langle x\\varepsilon_i,\\varepsilon_i\\rangle=\\sum_n\\langle\\varepsilon_i,\\eta_n\\rangle\\langle\\xi_n,\\varepsilon_i\\rangle\\). The double family is absolutely summable:\n\\[\n\\begin{gathered}\n\\sum_n\\sum_i|\\langle\\varepsilon_i,\\eta_n\\rangle|\\,|\\langle\\xi_n,\\varepsilon_i\\rangle|\\\\\n\\le\\sum_n\\|\\eta_n\\|\\,\\|\\xi_n\\|<\\infty,\n\\end{gathered}\n\\]\nby Cauchy–Schwarz and Parseval in \\(i\\). So we may sum over \\(i\\) first: \\[\n\\begin{gathered}\n\\sum_i\\langle x\\varepsilon_i,\\varepsilon_i\\rangle\\\\\n=\\sum_n\\sum_i\\langle\\xi_n,\\varepsilon_i\\rangle\\langle\\varepsilon_i,\\eta_n\\rangle\\\\\n=\\sum_n\\langle\\xi_n,\\eta_n\\rangle,\n\\end{gathered}\n\\] by Parseval. The right side does not involve the basis. Every trace-class operator has such a representation, namely (2.1) with \\(\\xi_n=s_nf_n\\), \\(\\eta_n=e_n\\).\n\nLinearity is clear for a fixed basis. \\(\\operatorname{Tr}(x^*)=\\sum_i\\langle x^*\\varepsilon_i,\\varepsilon_i\\rangle=\\sum_i\\overline{\\langle x\\varepsilon_i,\\varepsilon_i\\rangle}\\), positivity is termwise, and \\(\\operatorname{Tr}(\\theta_{\\xi,\\eta})=\\langle\\xi,\\eta\\rangle\\) is (4.2). For (4.3), write \\(x=\\sum_ns_n\\theta_{f_n,e_n}\\) as in (2.1). By Lemma 1.4(b), \\(ax=\\sum_ns_n\\theta_{af_n,e_n}\\) and \\(xa=\\sum_ns_n\\theta_{f_n,a^*e_n}\\), both with \\(\\sum_ns_n\\|a\\|<\\infty\\). By (4.2), \\[\n\\begin{gathered}\n\\operatorname{Tr}(ax)\\\\\n=\\sum_ns_n\\langle af_n,e_n\\rangle\\\\\n=\\sum_ns_n\\langle f_n,a^*e_n\\rangle\\\\\n=\\operatorname{Tr}(xa).\n\\end{gathered}\n\\] The bound follows from \\(|\\operatorname{Tr}(ax)|\\le\\|ax\\|_1\\) and Corollary 4.4(a). Formula (4.4) is (4.2) for \\(a\\theta_{\\xi,\\eta}=\\theta_{a\\xi,\\eta}\\). Finally \\(|x|=\\sum s_n\\theta_{e_n,e_n}\\) by Theorem 2.3(b), so \\(\\operatorname{Tr}(|x|)=\\sum s_n=\\|x\\|_1\\). \\(\\square\\)\n\n**Proposition 4.7** (testing on one basis). Let \\(h\\in B(H)_+\\). The sum \\(\\sum_i\\langle h\\varepsilon_i,\\varepsilon_i\\rangle\\in[0,\\infty]\\) is the same for all orthonormal bases. It is finite if and only if \\(h\\in\\mathcal L^1(H)\\), and then it equals \\(\\operatorname{Tr}(h)=\\|h\\|_1\\). Consequently, an operator \\(x\\) is of trace class if and only if \\(\\sum_i\\langle|x|\\varepsilon_i,\\varepsilon_i\\rangle<\\infty\\) for one orthonormal basis; and then \\(\\operatorname{Tr}(x)=\\sum_i\\langle x\\varepsilon_i,\\varepsilon_i\\rangle\\) for every orthonormal basis, with absolute convergence.\n\n**Proof.** \\(\\sum_i\\langle h\\varepsilon_i,\\varepsilon_i\\rangle=\\sum_i\\|h^{1/2}\\varepsilon_i\\|^2=\\|h^{1/2}\\|_2^2\\), which does not depend on the basis (Lemma 3.1). If it is finite, then \\(h^{1/2}\\in\\mathcal L^2(H)\\subseteq K(H)\\), so \\(h=(h^{1/2})^2\\) is compact. Write \\(h=\\sum_ns_n\\theta_{e_n,e_n}\\) as in Theorem 2.3(e) and complete \\((e_n)\\) to an orthonormal basis: \\(\\sum_ns_n=\\sum_n\\langle he_n,e_n\\rangle\\le\\|h^{1/2}\\|_2^2<\\infty\\). By Theorem 4.3(b), \\(h\\in\\mathcal L^1(H)\\), and by Theorem 4.6 the sum over any basis is \\(\\operatorname{Tr}(h)=\\operatorname{Tr}(|h|)=\\|h\\|_1\\). If \\(h\\in\\mathcal L^1(H)\\), the sum is \\(\\operatorname{Tr}(h)<\\infty\\). The last statement follows from Corollary 4.4(b) and Theorem 4.6. \\(\\square\\)\n\n**Proposition 4.8** (positive parts). Every \\(x\\in\\mathcal L^1(H)\\) can be written \\(x=h_1-h_2+i(h_3-h_4)\\) with positive trace-class operators \\(h_1,\\dots,h_4\\). A self-adjoint \\(x\\in\\mathcal L^1(H)\\) can be written \\(x=h_1-h_2\\) with \\(h_1,h_2\\ge0\\) of trace class and \\(\\|x\\|_1=\\operatorname{Tr}(h_1)+\\operatorname{Tr}(h_2)\\).\n\n**Proof.** The operators \\(\\operatorname{Re}x=\\frac12(x+x^*)\\) and \\(\\operatorname{Im}x=\\frac1{2i}(x-x^*)\\) are self-adjoint, lie in \\(\\mathcal L^1(H)\\) by Corollary 4.4(b), and \\(x=\\operatorname{Re}x+i\\operatorname{Im}x\\). For self-adjoint \\(x\\in\\mathcal L^1(H)\\), \\(x\\ne0\\), Theorem 2.3(e) gives \\(x=\\sum_n\\lambda_n\\theta_{e_n,e_n}\\) with real \\(\\lambda_n\\) and \\(\\sum|\\lambda_n|=\\|x\\|_1\\). Put \\(h_1=\\sum_{\\lambda_n>0}\\lambda_n\\theta_{e_n,e_n}\\) and \\(h_2=\\sum_{\\lambda_n<0}|\\lambda_n|\\theta_{e_n,e_n}\\). \\(\\square\\)\n\n**Proposition 4.9** (products of Hilbert–Schmidt operators).\n\n(a) An operator \\(x\\) is Hilbert–Schmidt if and only if \\(x^*x\\) is of trace class, and then \\(\\operatorname{Tr}(x^*x)=\\|x\\|_2^2\\).\n\n(b) If \\(x,y\\in\\mathcal L^2(H)\\), then \\(xy\\in\\mathcal L^1(H)\\), \\(\\|xy\\|_1\\le\\|x\\|_2\\,\\|y\\|_2\\), and \\(\\operatorname{Tr}(y^*x)=\\langle x,y\\rangle_2\\).\n\n(c) Every \\(t\\in\\mathcal L^1(H)\\) is a product \\(t=xy\\) of two Hilbert–Schmidt operators, with \\(\\|x\\|_2^2=\\|y\\|_2^2=\\|t\\|_1\\).\n\n**Proof.** (a) \\(\\sum_i\\langle x^*x\\varepsilon_i,\\varepsilon_i\\rangle=\\sum_i\\|x\\varepsilon_i\\|^2\\); apply Proposition 4.7 to \\(h=x^*x\\). (b) For finite orthonormal families, extend each to an orthonormal basis and use Cauchy–Schwarz:\n\\[\n\\begin{gathered}\n\\sum_j|\\langle xy\\xi_j,\\eta_j\\rangle|\\\\\n=\\sum_j|\\langle y\\xi_j,x^*\\eta_j\\rangle|\\\\\n\\le\\Big(\\sum_j\\|y\\xi_j\\|^2\\Big)^{1/2}\\Big(\\sum_j\\|x^*\\eta_j\\|^2\\Big)^{1/2}\\\\\n\\le\\|y\\|_2\\,\\|x^*\\|_2\\\\\n=\\|x\\|_2\\,\\|y\\|_2 .\n\\end{gathered}\n\\]\nThen \\[\n\\begin{gathered}\n\\operatorname{Tr}(y^*x)\\\\\n=\\sum_i\\langle y^*x\\varepsilon_i,\\varepsilon_i\\rangle\\\\\n=\\sum_i\\langle x\\varepsilon_i,y\\varepsilon_i\\rangle\\\\\n=\\langle x,y\\rangle_2.\n\\end{gathered}\n\\] (c) With (2.1) for \\(t\\ne0\\), put \\(x=\\sum_ns_n^{1/2}\\theta_{f_n,e_n}\\) and \\(y=\\sum_ns_n^{1/2}\\theta_{e_n,e_n}\\). Both are Hilbert–Schmidt with \\(\\|x\\|_2^2=\\|y\\|_2^2=\\sum s_n\\) (Theorem 3.3(b)), and \\(xy=\\sum_ns_n\\theta_{f_n,e_n}=t\\) because \\(\\theta_{f_n,e_n}\\theta_{e_m,e_m}=\\delta_{nm}\\theta_{f_n,e_n}\\). \\(\\square\\)","html":"<h2>4. Trace-class operators and the trace<\/h2>\n<p>An operator is of trace class when its matrix entries along pairs of orthonormal families have uniformly bounded absolute sums. For a compact operator this is the summability of its singular values. The trace is defined on these operators and has the properties of the trace of a matrix.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Definition 4.1.<\/strong> For <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(x\\in B(H)\\)\">\\(x\\in B(H)\\)<\/span> let\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n\\begin{gathered}\n\\|x\\|_1\\\\\n=\\sup\\Big\\{\\sum_{j\\in J}|\\langle x\\xi_j,\\eta_j\\rangle|\\Big\\}\\in[0,\\infty],\n\\end{gathered}\n\\tag{4.1}\n\\]\">\\[\n\\begin{gathered}\n\\|x\\|_1\\\\\n=\\sup\\Big\\{\\sum_{j\\in J}|\\langle x\\xi_j,\\eta_j\\rangle|\\Big\\}\\in[0,\\infty],\n\\end{gathered}\n\\tag{4.1}\n\\]<\/span>\nthe supremum over all finite sets <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(J\\)\">\\(J\\)<\/span> and all orthonormal families <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\((\\xi_j)_{j\\in J}\\)\">\\((\\xi_j)_{j\\in J}\\)<\/span> and <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\((\\eta_j)_{j\\in J}\\)\">\\((\\eta_j)_{j\\in J}\\)<\/span> in <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(H\\)\">\\(H\\)<\/span>. The operators with <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\|x\\|_1&lt;\\infty\\)\">\\(\\|x\\|_1&lt;\\infty\\)<\/span> are the <em>trace-class<\/em> (or <em>nuclear<\/em>) operators; they form the set <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\mathcal L^1(H)\\)\">\\(\\mathcal L^1(H)\\)<\/span>. Since the terms are nonnegative, the supremum does not change if infinite index sets <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(J\\)\">\\(J\\)<\/span> are allowed.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Lemma 4.2.<\/strong><\/p>\n<p>(a) <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\mathcal L^1(H)\\)\">\\(\\mathcal L^1(H)\\)<\/span> is a linear subspace of <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(B(H)\\)\">\\(B(H)\\)<\/span>, <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\|\\cdot\\|_1\\)\">\\(\\|\\cdot\\|_1\\)<\/span> is a norm on it, <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\|x\\|\\le\\|x\\|_1\\)\">\\(\\|x\\|\\le\\|x\\|_1\\)<\/span>, and <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\|x^*\\|_1=\\|x\\|_1\\)\">\\(\\|x^*\\|_1=\\|x\\|_1\\)<\/span>.<\/p>\n<p>(b) <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\|\\theta_{\\xi,\\eta}\\|_1=\\|\\xi\\|\\,\\|\\eta\\|\\)\">\\(\\|\\theta_{\\xi,\\eta}\\|_1=\\|\\xi\\|\\,\\|\\eta\\|\\)<\/span>.<\/p>\n<p>(c) If <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\((x_\\alpha)\\)\">\\((x_\\alpha)\\)<\/span> is a net with <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\langle x_\\alpha\\xi,\\eta\\rangle\\to\\langle x\\xi,\\eta\\rangle\\)\">\\(\\langle x_\\alpha\\xi,\\eta\\rangle\\to\\langle x\\xi,\\eta\\rangle\\)<\/span> for all <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\xi,\\eta\\)\">\\(\\xi,\\eta\\)<\/span>, then <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\|x\\|_1\\le\\liminf_\\alpha\\|x_\\alpha\\|_1\\)\">\\(\\|x\\|_1\\le\\liminf_\\alpha\\|x_\\alpha\\|_1\\)<\/span>.<\/p>\n<p>(d) <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\((\\mathcal L^1(H),\\|\\cdot\\|_1)\\)\">\\((\\mathcal L^1(H),\\|\\cdot\\|_1)\\)<\/span> is a Banach space.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Proof.<\/strong> (a) The inequalities <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\|x+y\\|_1\\le\\|x\\|_1+\\|y\\|_1\\)\">\\(\\|x+y\\|_1\\le\\|x\\|_1+\\|y\\|_1\\)<\/span> and <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\|cx\\|_1=|c|\\,\\|x\\|_1\\)\">\\(\\|cx\\|_1=|c|\\,\\|x\\|_1\\)<\/span> hold term by term. With one pair of unit vectors, <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(|\\langle x\\xi,\\eta\\rangle|\\le\\|x\\|_1\\)\">\\(|\\langle x\\xi,\\eta\\rangle|\\le\\|x\\|_1\\)<\/span>; the supremum over <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\xi,\\eta\\)\">\\(\\xi,\\eta\\)<\/span> is <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\|x\\|\\)\">\\(\\|x\\|\\)<\/span>. Since <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(|\\langle x^*\\xi_j,\\eta_j\\rangle|=|\\langle x\\eta_j,\\xi_j\\rangle|\\)\">\\(|\\langle x^*\\xi_j,\\eta_j\\rangle|=|\\langle x\\eta_j,\\xi_j\\rangle|\\)<\/span>, passing to <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(x^*\\)\">\\(x^*\\)<\/span> exchanges the two families.<\/p>\n<p>(b) By Cauchy–Schwarz and Bessel's inequality,\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n\\begin{gathered}\n\\sum_j|\\langle\\theta_{\\xi,\\eta}\\xi_j,\\eta_j\\rangle|\\\\\n=\\sum_j|\\langle\\xi_j,\\eta\\rangle|\\,|\\langle\\xi,\\eta_j\\rangle|\\\\\n\\le\\Big(\\sum_j|\\langle\\xi_j,\\eta\\rangle|^2\\Big)^{1/2}\\Big(\\sum_j|\\langle\\eta_j,\\xi\\rangle|^2\\Big)^{1/2}\\\\\n\\le\\|\\eta\\|\\,\\|\\xi\\| .\n\\end{gathered}\n\\]\">\\[\n\\begin{gathered}\n\\sum_j|\\langle\\theta_{\\xi,\\eta}\\xi_j,\\eta_j\\rangle|\\\\\n=\\sum_j|\\langle\\xi_j,\\eta\\rangle|\\,|\\langle\\xi,\\eta_j\\rangle|\\\\\n\\le\\Big(\\sum_j|\\langle\\xi_j,\\eta\\rangle|^2\\Big)^{1/2}\\Big(\\sum_j|\\langle\\eta_j,\\xi\\rangle|^2\\Big)^{1/2}\\\\\n\\le\\|\\eta\\|\\,\\|\\xi\\| .\n\\end{gathered}\n\\]<\/span>\nIf <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\xi,\\eta\\ne0\\)\">\\(\\xi,\\eta\\ne0\\)<\/span>, the single pair <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\xi_1=\\eta/\\|\\eta\\|\\)\">\\(\\xi_1=\\eta/\\|\\eta\\|\\)<\/span>, <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\eta_1=\\xi/\\|\\xi\\|\\)\">\\(\\eta_1=\\xi/\\|\\xi\\|\\)<\/span> gives equality.<\/p>\n<p>(c) For fixed finite families, <span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n\\begin{gathered}\n\\sum_j|\\langle x\\xi_j,\\eta_j\\rangle|\\\\\n=\\lim_\\alpha\\sum_j|\\langle x_\\alpha\\xi_j,\\eta_j\\rangle|\\\\\n\\le\\liminf_\\alpha\\|x_\\alpha\\|_1.\n\\end{gathered}\n\\]\">\\[\n\\begin{gathered}\n\\sum_j|\\langle x\\xi_j,\\eta_j\\rangle|\\\\\n=\\lim_\\alpha\\sum_j|\\langle x_\\alpha\\xi_j,\\eta_j\\rangle|\\\\\n\\le\\liminf_\\alpha\\|x_\\alpha\\|_1.\n\\end{gathered}\n\\]<\/span><\/p>\n<p>(d) Let <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\((x_k)\\)\">\\((x_k)\\)<\/span> be Cauchy for <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\|\\cdot\\|_1\\)\">\\(\\|\\cdot\\|_1\\)<\/span>. By (a) it is Cauchy in norm, so <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(x_k\\to x\\)\">\\(x_k\\to x\\)<\/span> in norm. For fixed <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(k\\)\">\\(k\\)<\/span>, apply (c) to the sequence <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\((x_m-x_k)_m\\)\">\\((x_m-x_k)_m\\)<\/span>, which converges in norm to <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(x-x_k\\)\">\\(x-x_k\\)<\/span>: <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\|x-x_k\\|_1\\le\\sup_{m\\ge k}\\|x_m-x_k\\|_1\\)\">\\(\\|x-x_k\\|_1\\le\\sup_{m\\ge k}\\|x_m-x_k\\|_1\\)<\/span>, which tends to <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(0\\)\">\\(0\\)<\/span>. So <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(x=(x-x_k)+x_k\\in\\mathcal L^1(H)\\)\">\\(x=(x-x_k)+x_k\\in\\mathcal L^1(H)\\)<\/span> and <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(x_k\\to x\\)\">\\(x_k\\to x\\)<\/span> in <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\|\\cdot\\|_1\\)\">\\(\\|\\cdot\\|_1\\)<\/span>. <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\square\\)\">\\(\\square\\)<\/span><\/p>\n<p><strong>Theorem 4.3.<\/strong><\/p>\n<p>(a) <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\|x\\|_2^2\\le\\|x\\|\\,\\|x\\|_1\\)\">\\(\\|x\\|_2^2\\le\\|x\\|\\,\\|x\\|_1\\)<\/span> for every <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(x\\in B(H)\\)\">\\(x\\in B(H)\\)<\/span>. Hence <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\mathcal L^1(H)\\subseteq\\mathcal L^2(H)\\subseteq K(H)\\)\">\\(\\mathcal L^1(H)\\subseteq\\mathcal L^2(H)\\subseteq K(H)\\)<\/span>.<\/p>\n<p>(b) For <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(x\\in K(H)\\)\">\\(x\\in K(H)\\)<\/span>, <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\|x\\|_1=\\sum_ns_n(x)\\)\">\\(\\|x\\|_1=\\sum_ns_n(x)\\)<\/span> in <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\([0,\\infty]\\)\">\\([0,\\infty]\\)<\/span>. So <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(x\\in\\mathcal L^1(H)\\)\">\\(x\\in\\mathcal L^1(H)\\)<\/span> if and only if <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(x\\)\">\\(x\\)<\/span> is compact and <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\sum_ns_n(x)&lt;\\infty\\)\">\\(\\sum_ns_n(x)&lt;\\infty\\)<\/span>.<\/p>\n<p>(c) If <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(x\\in\\mathcal L^1(H)\\)\">\\(x\\in\\mathcal L^1(H)\\)<\/span>, every decomposition (2.1) of <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(x\\)\">\\(x\\)<\/span> converges in <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\|\\cdot\\|_1\\)\">\\(\\|\\cdot\\|_1\\)<\/span>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Proof.<\/strong> (a) We may assume <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\|x\\|_1&lt;\\infty\\)\">\\(\\|x\\|_1&lt;\\infty\\)<\/span>. Let <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\xi_1,\\dots,\\xi_m\\)\">\\(\\xi_1,\\dots,\\xi_m\\)<\/span> be orthonormal, let <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(L\\)\">\\(L\\)<\/span> be their span and <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(p\\)\">\\(p\\)<\/span> the projection onto <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(L\\)\">\\(L\\)<\/span>. The operator <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(px^*xp\\)\">\\(px^*xp\\)<\/span> is positive and maps <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(L\\)\">\\(L\\)<\/span> into <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(L\\)\">\\(L\\)<\/span>, so <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(L\\)\">\\(L\\)<\/span> has an orthonormal basis <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\zeta_1,\\dots,\\zeta_m\\)\">\\(\\zeta_1,\\dots,\\zeta_m\\)<\/span> with <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(px^*xp\\,\\zeta_k=\\sigma_k^2\\zeta_k\\)\">\\(px^*xp\\,\\zeta_k=\\sigma_k^2\\zeta_k\\)<\/span>, <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\sigma_k\\ge0\\)\">\\(\\sigma_k\\ge0\\)<\/span>. Then <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\langle x\\zeta_k,x\\zeta_l\\rangle=\\langle px^*xp\\,\\zeta_k,\\zeta_l\\rangle=\\sigma_k^2\\delta_{kl}\\)\">\\(\\langle x\\zeta_k,x\\zeta_l\\rangle=\\langle px^*xp\\,\\zeta_k,\\zeta_l\\rangle=\\sigma_k^2\\delta_{kl}\\)<\/span>: the vectors <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(x\\zeta_k\\)\">\\(x\\zeta_k\\)<\/span> are orthogonal, with norms <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\sigma_k\\)\">\\(\\sigma_k\\)<\/span>. Choose orthonormal <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\eta_1,\\dots,\\eta_m\\)\">\\(\\eta_1,\\dots,\\eta_m\\)<\/span> with <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(x\\zeta_k=\\sigma_k\\eta_k\\)\">\\(x\\zeta_k=\\sigma_k\\eta_k\\)<\/span>: put <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\eta_k=\\sigma_k^{-1}x\\zeta_k\\)\">\\(\\eta_k=\\sigma_k^{-1}x\\zeta_k\\)<\/span> when <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\sigma_k&gt;0\\)\">\\(\\sigma_k&gt;0\\)<\/span>, and complete by further orthonormal vectors, which is possible since <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\dim H\\ge m\\)\">\\(\\dim H\\ge m\\)<\/span>. Then\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n\\sum_k\\sigma_k=\\sum_k\\langle x\\zeta_k,\\eta_k\\rangle\\le\\|x\\|_1 .\n\\]\">\\[\n\\sum_k\\sigma_k=\\sum_k\\langle x\\zeta_k,\\eta_k\\rangle\\le\\|x\\|_1 .\n\\]<\/span>\nApply Lemma 3.1 to <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(xp\\)\">\\(xp\\)<\/span> and to the two orthonormal bases of <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(H\\)\">\\(H\\)<\/span> obtained by completing <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\((\\xi_i)\\)\">\\((\\xi_i)\\)<\/span> and <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\((\\zeta_k)\\)\">\\((\\zeta_k)\\)<\/span> with one and the same orthonormal basis of <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(L^\\perp\\)\">\\(L^\\perp\\)<\/span>, on which <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(xp\\)\">\\(xp\\)<\/span> vanishes. This gives <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\sum_i\\|x\\xi_i\\|^2=\\sum_k\\|x\\zeta_k\\|^2=\\sum_k\\sigma_k^2\\)\">\\(\\sum_i\\|x\\xi_i\\|^2=\\sum_k\\|x\\zeta_k\\|^2=\\sum_k\\sigma_k^2\\)<\/span>. Since <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\sigma_k\\le\\|x\\|\\)\">\\(\\sigma_k\\le\\|x\\|\\)<\/span>,\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n\\sum_{i\\le m}\\|x\\xi_i\\|^2=\\sum_k\\sigma_k^2\\le\\|x\\|\\sum_k\\sigma_k\\le\\|x\\|\\,\\|x\\|_1 .\n\\]\">\\[\n\\sum_{i\\le m}\\|x\\xi_i\\|^2=\\sum_k\\sigma_k^2\\le\\|x\\|\\sum_k\\sigma_k\\le\\|x\\|\\,\\|x\\|_1 .\n\\]<\/span>\nEvery finite subfamily of an orthonormal basis is such a family, so <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\|x\\|_2^2\\le\\|x\\|\\,\\|x\\|_1\\)\">\\(\\|x\\|_2^2\\le\\|x\\|\\,\\|x\\|_1\\)<\/span>. The inclusion <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\mathcal L^2(H)\\subseteq K(H)\\)\">\\(\\mathcal L^2(H)\\subseteq K(H)\\)<\/span> is Theorem 3.3(b).<\/p>\n<p>(b) Let <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(x\\in K(H)\\)\">\\(x\\in K(H)\\)<\/span>, <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(x\\ne0\\)\">\\(x\\ne0\\)<\/span>, with decomposition (2.1), and let <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\((\\xi_j)\\)\">\\((\\xi_j)\\)<\/span>, <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\((\\eta_j)\\)\">\\((\\eta_j)\\)<\/span> be finite orthonormal families. Since <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\langle x\\xi_j,\\eta_j\\rangle=\\sum_ns_n\\langle\\xi_j,e_n\\rangle\\langle f_n,\\eta_j\\rangle\\)\">\\(\\langle x\\xi_j,\\eta_j\\rangle=\\sum_ns_n\\langle\\xi_j,e_n\\rangle\\langle f_n,\\eta_j\\rangle\\)<\/span>,\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n\\begin{gathered}\n\\sum_j|\\langle x\\xi_j,\\eta_j\\rangle|\\\\\n\\le\\sum_ns_n\\sum_j|\\langle\\xi_j,e_n\\rangle|\\,|\\langle f_n,\\eta_j\\rangle|\\\\\n\\le\\sum_ns_n\\Big(\\sum_j|\\langle e_n,\\xi_j\\rangle|^2\\Big)^{1/2}\\\\\n{}\\cdot\\Big(\\sum_j|\\langle f_n,\\eta_j\\rangle|^2\\Big)^{1/2}\\\\\n\\le\\sum_ns_n\n\\end{gathered}\n\\]\">\\[\n\\begin{gathered}\n\\sum_j|\\langle x\\xi_j,\\eta_j\\rangle|\\\\\n\\le\\sum_ns_n\\sum_j|\\langle\\xi_j,e_n\\rangle|\\,|\\langle f_n,\\eta_j\\rangle|\\\\\n\\le\\sum_ns_n\\Big(\\sum_j|\\langle e_n,\\xi_j\\rangle|^2\\Big)^{1/2}\\\\\n{}\\cdot\\Big(\\sum_j|\\langle f_n,\\eta_j\\rangle|^2\\Big)^{1/2}\\\\\n\\le\\sum_ns_n\n\\end{gathered}\n\\]<\/span>\nby Cauchy–Schwarz and Bessel's inequality. So <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\|x\\|_1\\le\\sum_ns_n\\)\">\\(\\|x\\|_1\\le\\sum_ns_n\\)<\/span>. Conversely, the families <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\xi_j=e_j\\)\">\\(\\xi_j=e_j\\)<\/span>, <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\eta_j=f_j\\)\">\\(\\eta_j=f_j\\)<\/span>, <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(j\\le M\\)\">\\(j\\le M\\)<\/span>, give <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\sum_{j\\le M}\\langle xe_j,f_j\\rangle=\\sum_{j\\le M}s_j\\)\">\\(\\sum_{j\\le M}\\langle xe_j,f_j\\rangle=\\sum_{j\\le M}s_j\\)<\/span>, so <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\|x\\|_1\\ge\\sum_{j\\le M}s_j\\)\">\\(\\|x\\|_1\\ge\\sum_{j\\le M}s_j\\)<\/span> for every <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(M\\)\">\\(M\\)<\/span>. Together with (a), which shows that a trace-class operator is compact, this proves (b).<\/p>\n<p>(c) The remainder <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\sum_{n&gt;M}s_n\\theta_{f_n,e_n}\\)\">\\(\\sum_{n&gt;M}s_n\\theta_{f_n,e_n}\\)<\/span> is compact and has a decomposition of the form (2.1) with the numbers <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(s_{M+1},s_{M+2},\\dots\\)\">\\(s_{M+1},s_{M+2},\\dots\\)<\/span>. By (b) its trace norm is <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\sum_{n&gt;M}s_n\\)\">\\(\\sum_{n&gt;M}s_n\\)<\/span>, which tends to <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(0\\)\">\\(0\\)<\/span>. <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\square\\)\">\\(\\square\\)<\/span><\/p>\n<p><strong>Corollary 4.4.<\/strong><\/p>\n<p>(a) If <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(x\\in\\mathcal L^1(H)\\)\">\\(x\\in\\mathcal L^1(H)\\)<\/span> and <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(a,b\\in B(H)\\)\">\\(a,b\\in B(H)\\)<\/span>, then <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(axb\\in\\mathcal L^1(H)\\)\">\\(axb\\in\\mathcal L^1(H)\\)<\/span> and <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\|axb\\|_1\\le\\|a\\|\\,\\|x\\|_1\\,\\|b\\|\\)\">\\(\\|axb\\|_1\\le\\|a\\|\\,\\|x\\|_1\\,\\|b\\|\\)<\/span>. So <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\mathcal L^1(H)\\)\">\\(\\mathcal L^1(H)\\)<\/span> is a two-sided ideal of <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(B(H)\\)\">\\(B(H)\\)<\/span>, closed under adjoints.<\/p>\n<p>(b) <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(x\\in\\mathcal L^1(H)\\)\">\\(x\\in\\mathcal L^1(H)\\)<\/span> if and only if <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(|x|\\in\\mathcal L^1(H)\\)\">\\(|x|\\in\\mathcal L^1(H)\\)<\/span>, if and only if <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(x^*\\in\\mathcal L^1(H)\\)\">\\(x^*\\in\\mathcal L^1(H)\\)<\/span>, and <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\|x\\|_1=\\||x|\\|_1=\\|x^*\\|_1\\)\">\\(\\|x\\|_1=\\||x|\\|_1=\\|x^*\\|_1\\)<\/span>.<\/p>\n<p>(c) <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(F(H)\\subseteq\\mathcal L^1(H)\\)\">\\(F(H)\\subseteq\\mathcal L^1(H)\\)<\/span>, and <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(F(H)\\)\">\\(F(H)\\)<\/span> is dense in <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\mathcal L^1(H)\\)\">\\(\\mathcal L^1(H)\\)<\/span> for <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\|\\cdot\\|_1\\)\">\\(\\|\\cdot\\|_1\\)<\/span>. For <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(x\\in\\mathcal L^1(H)\\)\">\\(x\\in\\mathcal L^1(H)\\)<\/span>, <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\|x\\|\\le\\|x\\|_2\\le\\|x\\|_1\\)\">\\(\\|x\\|\\le\\|x\\|_2\\le\\|x\\|_1\\)<\/span>.<\/p>\n<p>(d) If <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\sum_n\\|\\xi_n\\|\\,\\|\\eta_n\\|&lt;\\infty\\)\">\\(\\sum_n\\|\\xi_n\\|\\,\\|\\eta_n\\|&lt;\\infty\\)<\/span>, the series <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\sum_n\\theta_{\\xi_n,\\eta_n}\\)\">\\(\\sum_n\\theta_{\\xi_n,\\eta_n}\\)<\/span> converges in <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\|\\cdot\\|_1\\)\">\\(\\|\\cdot\\|_1\\)<\/span>, and its sum <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(t\\)\">\\(t\\)<\/span> satisfies <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\|t\\|_1\\le\\sum_n\\|\\xi_n\\|\\,\\|\\eta_n\\|\\)\">\\(\\|t\\|_1\\le\\sum_n\\|\\xi_n\\|\\,\\|\\eta_n\\|\\)<\/span>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Proof.<\/strong> (a) follows from Proposition 2.5(a) and Theorem 4.3(b). (b) By Lemma 4.2(a), <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(x^*\\in\\mathcal L^1(H)\\)\">\\(x^*\\in\\mathcal L^1(H)\\)<\/span> exactly when <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(x\\in\\mathcal L^1(H)\\)\">\\(x\\in\\mathcal L^1(H)\\)<\/span>, and <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\|x^*\\|_1=\\|x\\|_1\\)\">\\(\\|x^*\\|_1=\\|x\\|_1\\)<\/span>. If <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(x\\in\\mathcal L^1(H)\\)\">\\(x\\in\\mathcal L^1(H)\\)<\/span>, then <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(x\\)\">\\(x\\)<\/span> is compact by Theorem 4.3(a). If <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(|x|\\in\\mathcal L^1(H)\\)\">\\(|x|\\in\\mathcal L^1(H)\\)<\/span>, then <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(x\\)\">\\(x\\)<\/span> is compact too: <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\|x\\zeta\\|^2=\\langle x^*x\\zeta,\\zeta\\rangle=\\||x|\\zeta\\|^2\\)\">\\(\\|x\\zeta\\|^2=\\langle x^*x\\zeta,\\zeta\\rangle=\\||x|\\zeta\\|^2\\)<\/span> for every <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\zeta\\)\">\\(\\zeta\\)<\/span>, so <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\|x\\|_2=\\||x|\\|_2\\)\">\\(\\|x\\|_2=\\||x|\\|_2\\)<\/span>, which is finite by Theorem 4.3(a), and <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\mathcal L^2(H)\\subseteq K(H)\\)\">\\(\\mathcal L^2(H)\\subseteq K(H)\\)<\/span> by Theorem 3.3(b). For compact <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(x\\)\">\\(x\\)<\/span>, Proposition 2.5(c) and Theorem 4.3(b) give <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\|x\\|_1=\\||x|\\|_1\\)\">\\(\\|x\\|_1=\\||x|\\|_1\\)<\/span> in <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\([0,\\infty]\\)\">\\([0,\\infty]\\)<\/span>. (c) A finite-rank operator has only finitely many nonzero singular values. Density follows from Theorem 4.3(c). For <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(x\\in\\mathcal L^1(H)\\)\">\\(x\\in\\mathcal L^1(H)\\)<\/span>, <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\|x\\|_2^2=\\sum s_n^2\\le(\\sum s_n)^2\\)\">\\(\\|x\\|_2^2=\\sum s_n^2\\le(\\sum s_n)^2\\)<\/span>. (d) By Lemma 4.2(b) the series converges absolutely in the Banach space <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\mathcal L^1(H)\\)\">\\(\\mathcal L^1(H)\\)<\/span>. <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\square\\)\">\\(\\square\\)<\/span><\/p>\n<p><strong>Example 4.5<\/strong> (three different norms). Let <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\xi\\perp\\eta\\)\">\\(\\xi\\perp\\eta\\)<\/span> be unit vectors and <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(x=\\theta_{\\xi,\\eta}+\\theta_{\\eta,\\xi}\\)\">\\(x=\\theta_{\\xi,\\eta}+\\theta_{\\eta,\\xi}\\)<\/span>. Then <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(x(\\xi\\pm\\eta)=\\pm(\\xi\\pm\\eta)\\)\">\\(x(\\xi\\pm\\eta)=\\pm(\\xi\\pm\\eta)\\)<\/span> and <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(x\\)\">\\(x\\)<\/span> vanishes on <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\{\\xi,\\eta\\}^\\perp\\)\">\\(\\{\\xi,\\eta\\}^\\perp\\)<\/span>. So <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(x\\)\">\\(x\\)<\/span> is self-adjoint with eigenvalues <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\pm1\\)\">\\(\\pm1\\)<\/span>, and <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(s(x)=(1,1,0,0,\\dots)\\)\">\\(s(x)=(1,1,0,0,\\dots)\\)<\/span>. Hence <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\|x\\|=1\\)\">\\(\\|x\\|=1\\)<\/span>, <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\|x\\|_2=\\sqrt2\\)\">\\(\\|x\\|_2=\\sqrt2\\)<\/span>, <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\|x\\|_1=2\\)\">\\(\\|x\\|_1=2\\)<\/span>, and <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\operatorname{Tr}(x)=0\\)\">\\(\\operatorname{Tr}(x)=0\\)<\/span> (Theorem 4.6). For the diagonal operators of Example 2.2, Example 2.6 shows that <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(d_\\lambda\\in\\mathcal L^2(H)\\)\">\\(d_\\lambda\\in\\mathcal L^2(H)\\)<\/span> exactly when <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\lambda\\in\\ell^2(I)\\)\">\\(\\lambda\\in\\ell^2(I)\\)<\/span> and <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(d_\\lambda\\in\\mathcal L^1(H)\\)\">\\(d_\\lambda\\in\\mathcal L^1(H)\\)<\/span> exactly when <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\lambda\\in\\ell^1(I)\\)\">\\(\\lambda\\in\\ell^1(I)\\)<\/span>, with <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\|d_\\lambda\\|_2=\\|\\lambda\\|_2\\)\">\\(\\|d_\\lambda\\|_2=\\|\\lambda\\|_2\\)<\/span> and <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\|d_\\lambda\\|_1=\\|\\lambda\\|_1\\)\">\\(\\|d_\\lambda\\|_1=\\|\\lambda\\|_1\\)<\/span>. The Volterra operator of Example 3.5 lies in <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\mathcal L^2(H)\\)\">\\(\\mathcal L^2(H)\\)<\/span> but not in <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\mathcal L^1(H)\\)\">\\(\\mathcal L^1(H)\\)<\/span>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Theorem 4.6<\/strong> (the trace). Let <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(x\\in\\mathcal L^1(H)\\)\">\\(x\\in\\mathcal L^1(H)\\)<\/span> and let <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\((\\varepsilon_i)_{i\\in I}\\)\">\\((\\varepsilon_i)_{i\\in I}\\)<\/span> be an orthonormal basis. Then <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\sum_i\\langle x\\varepsilon_i,\\varepsilon_i\\rangle\\)\">\\(\\sum_i\\langle x\\varepsilon_i,\\varepsilon_i\\rangle\\)<\/span> converges absolutely, <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\sum_i|\\langle x\\varepsilon_i,\\varepsilon_i\\rangle|\\le\\|x\\|_1\\)\">\\(\\sum_i|\\langle x\\varepsilon_i,\\varepsilon_i\\rangle|\\le\\|x\\|_1\\)<\/span>, and the sum, denoted <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\operatorname{Tr}(x)\\)\">\\(\\operatorname{Tr}(x)\\)<\/span>, does not depend on the basis. More precisely, if <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(x=\\sum_n\\theta_{\\xi_n,\\eta_n}\\)\">\\(x=\\sum_n\\theta_{\\xi_n,\\eta_n}\\)<\/span> with <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\sum_n\\|\\xi_n\\|\\,\\|\\eta_n\\|&lt;\\infty\\)\">\\(\\sum_n\\|\\xi_n\\|\\,\\|\\eta_n\\|&lt;\\infty\\)<\/span>, as in (2.1), then\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n\\operatorname{Tr}(x)=\\sum_n\\langle\\xi_n,\\eta_n\\rangle .\n\\tag{4.2}\n\\]\">\\[\n\\operatorname{Tr}(x)=\\sum_n\\langle\\xi_n,\\eta_n\\rangle .\n\\tag{4.2}\n\\]<\/span>\nThe trace is a linear functional on <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\mathcal L^1(H)\\)\">\\(\\mathcal L^1(H)\\)<\/span> with <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(|\\operatorname{Tr}(x)|\\le\\|x\\|_1\\)\">\\(|\\operatorname{Tr}(x)|\\le\\|x\\|_1\\)<\/span>, <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\operatorname{Tr}(x^*)=\\overline{\\operatorname{Tr}(x)}\\)\">\\(\\operatorname{Tr}(x^*)=\\overline{\\operatorname{Tr}(x)}\\)<\/span>, <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\operatorname{Tr}(x)\\ge0\\)\">\\(\\operatorname{Tr}(x)\\ge0\\)<\/span> for <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(x\\ge0\\)\">\\(x\\ge0\\)<\/span>, and <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\operatorname{Tr}(\\theta_{\\xi,\\eta})=\\langle\\xi,\\eta\\rangle\\)\">\\(\\operatorname{Tr}(\\theta_{\\xi,\\eta})=\\langle\\xi,\\eta\\rangle\\)<\/span>. For <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(a\\in B(H)\\)\">\\(a\\in B(H)\\)<\/span>,\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n\\begin{gathered}\n\\operatorname{Tr}(ax)\\\\\n=\\operatorname{Tr}(xa),\\\\\n|\\operatorname{Tr}(ax)|\\\\\n\\le\\|a\\|\\,\\|x\\|_1,\n\\end{gathered}\n\\tag{4.3}\n\\]\">\\[\n\\begin{gathered}\n\\operatorname{Tr}(ax)\\\\\n=\\operatorname{Tr}(xa),\\\\\n|\\operatorname{Tr}(ax)|\\\\\n\\le\\|a\\|\\,\\|x\\|_1,\n\\end{gathered}\n\\tag{4.3}\n\\]<\/span>\nand in particular\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n\\operatorname{Tr}(a\\,\\theta_{\\xi,\\eta})=\\langle a\\xi,\\eta\\rangle=\\omega_{\\xi,\\eta}(a).\n\\tag{4.4}\n\\]\">\\[\n\\operatorname{Tr}(a\\,\\theta_{\\xi,\\eta})=\\langle a\\xi,\\eta\\rangle=\\omega_{\\xi,\\eta}(a).\n\\tag{4.4}\n\\]<\/span>\nFinally <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\|x\\|_1=\\operatorname{Tr}(|x|)\\)\">\\(\\|x\\|_1=\\operatorname{Tr}(|x|)\\)<\/span>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Proof.<\/strong> Taking <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\xi_j=\\eta_j=\\varepsilon_j\\)\">\\(\\xi_j=\\eta_j=\\varepsilon_j\\)<\/span> over finite subsets in (4.1) gives absolute convergence and the bound. Let <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(x=\\sum_n\\theta_{\\xi_n,\\eta_n}\\)\">\\(x=\\sum_n\\theta_{\\xi_n,\\eta_n}\\)<\/span> with <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\sum\\|\\xi_n\\|\\,\\|\\eta_n\\|&lt;\\infty\\)\">\\(\\sum\\|\\xi_n\\|\\,\\|\\eta_n\\|&lt;\\infty\\)<\/span>; the series converges in <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\|\\cdot\\|_1\\)\">\\(\\|\\cdot\\|_1\\)<\/span> by Corollary 4.4(d), hence in norm. Then <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\langle x\\varepsilon_i,\\varepsilon_i\\rangle=\\sum_n\\langle\\varepsilon_i,\\eta_n\\rangle\\langle\\xi_n,\\varepsilon_i\\rangle\\)\">\\(\\langle x\\varepsilon_i,\\varepsilon_i\\rangle=\\sum_n\\langle\\varepsilon_i,\\eta_n\\rangle\\langle\\xi_n,\\varepsilon_i\\rangle\\)<\/span>. The double family is absolutely summable:\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n\\begin{gathered}\n\\sum_n\\sum_i|\\langle\\varepsilon_i,\\eta_n\\rangle|\\,|\\langle\\xi_n,\\varepsilon_i\\rangle|\\\\\n\\le\\sum_n\\|\\eta_n\\|\\,\\|\\xi_n\\|&lt;\\infty,\n\\end{gathered}\n\\]\">\\[\n\\begin{gathered}\n\\sum_n\\sum_i|\\langle\\varepsilon_i,\\eta_n\\rangle|\\,|\\langle\\xi_n,\\varepsilon_i\\rangle|\\\\\n\\le\\sum_n\\|\\eta_n\\|\\,\\|\\xi_n\\|&lt;\\infty,\n\\end{gathered}\n\\]<\/span>\nby Cauchy–Schwarz and Parseval in <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(i\\)\">\\(i\\)<\/span>. So we may sum over <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(i\\)\">\\(i\\)<\/span> first: <span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n\\begin{gathered}\n\\sum_i\\langle x\\varepsilon_i,\\varepsilon_i\\rangle\\\\\n=\\sum_n\\sum_i\\langle\\xi_n,\\varepsilon_i\\rangle\\langle\\varepsilon_i,\\eta_n\\rangle\\\\\n=\\sum_n\\langle\\xi_n,\\eta_n\\rangle,\n\\end{gathered}\n\\]\">\\[\n\\begin{gathered}\n\\sum_i\\langle x\\varepsilon_i,\\varepsilon_i\\rangle\\\\\n=\\sum_n\\sum_i\\langle\\xi_n,\\varepsilon_i\\rangle\\langle\\varepsilon_i,\\eta_n\\rangle\\\\\n=\\sum_n\\langle\\xi_n,\\eta_n\\rangle,\n\\end{gathered}\n\\]<\/span> by Parseval. The right side does not involve the basis. Every trace-class operator has such a representation, namely (2.1) with <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\xi_n=s_nf_n\\)\">\\(\\xi_n=s_nf_n\\)<\/span>, <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\eta_n=e_n\\)\">\\(\\eta_n=e_n\\)<\/span>.<\/p>\n<p>Linearity is clear for a fixed basis. <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\operatorname{Tr}(x^*)=\\sum_i\\langle x^*\\varepsilon_i,\\varepsilon_i\\rangle=\\sum_i\\overline{\\langle x\\varepsilon_i,\\varepsilon_i\\rangle}\\)\">\\(\\operatorname{Tr}(x^*)=\\sum_i\\langle x^*\\varepsilon_i,\\varepsilon_i\\rangle=\\sum_i\\overline{\\langle x\\varepsilon_i,\\varepsilon_i\\rangle}\\)<\/span>, positivity is termwise, and <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\operatorname{Tr}(\\theta_{\\xi,\\eta})=\\langle\\xi,\\eta\\rangle\\)\">\\(\\operatorname{Tr}(\\theta_{\\xi,\\eta})=\\langle\\xi,\\eta\\rangle\\)<\/span> is (4.2). For (4.3), write <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(x=\\sum_ns_n\\theta_{f_n,e_n}\\)\">\\(x=\\sum_ns_n\\theta_{f_n,e_n}\\)<\/span> as in (2.1). By Lemma 1.4(b), <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(ax=\\sum_ns_n\\theta_{af_n,e_n}\\)\">\\(ax=\\sum_ns_n\\theta_{af_n,e_n}\\)<\/span> and <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(xa=\\sum_ns_n\\theta_{f_n,a^*e_n}\\)\">\\(xa=\\sum_ns_n\\theta_{f_n,a^*e_n}\\)<\/span>, both with <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\sum_ns_n\\|a\\|&lt;\\infty\\)\">\\(\\sum_ns_n\\|a\\|&lt;\\infty\\)<\/span>. By (4.2), <span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n\\begin{gathered}\n\\operatorname{Tr}(ax)\\\\\n=\\sum_ns_n\\langle af_n,e_n\\rangle\\\\\n=\\sum_ns_n\\langle f_n,a^*e_n\\rangle\\\\\n=\\operatorname{Tr}(xa).\n\\end{gathered}\n\\]\">\\[\n\\begin{gathered}\n\\operatorname{Tr}(ax)\\\\\n=\\sum_ns_n\\langle af_n,e_n\\rangle\\\\\n=\\sum_ns_n\\langle f_n,a^*e_n\\rangle\\\\\n=\\operatorname{Tr}(xa).\n\\end{gathered}\n\\]<\/span> The bound follows from <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(|\\operatorname{Tr}(ax)|\\le\\|ax\\|_1\\)\">\\(|\\operatorname{Tr}(ax)|\\le\\|ax\\|_1\\)<\/span> and Corollary 4.4(a). Formula (4.4) is (4.2) for <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(a\\theta_{\\xi,\\eta}=\\theta_{a\\xi,\\eta}\\)\">\\(a\\theta_{\\xi,\\eta}=\\theta_{a\\xi,\\eta}\\)<\/span>. Finally <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(|x|=\\sum s_n\\theta_{e_n,e_n}\\)\">\\(|x|=\\sum s_n\\theta_{e_n,e_n}\\)<\/span> by Theorem 2.3(b), so <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\operatorname{Tr}(|x|)=\\sum s_n=\\|x\\|_1\\)\">\\(\\operatorname{Tr}(|x|)=\\sum s_n=\\|x\\|_1\\)<\/span>. <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\square\\)\">\\(\\square\\)<\/span><\/p>\n<p><strong>Proposition 4.7<\/strong> (testing on one basis). Let <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(h\\in B(H)_+\\)\">\\(h\\in B(H)_+\\)<\/span>. The sum <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\sum_i\\langle h\\varepsilon_i,\\varepsilon_i\\rangle\\in[0,\\infty]\\)\">\\(\\sum_i\\langle h\\varepsilon_i,\\varepsilon_i\\rangle\\in[0,\\infty]\\)<\/span> is the same for all orthonormal bases. It is finite if and only if <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(h\\in\\mathcal L^1(H)\\)\">\\(h\\in\\mathcal L^1(H)\\)<\/span>, and then it equals <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\operatorname{Tr}(h)=\\|h\\|_1\\)\">\\(\\operatorname{Tr}(h)=\\|h\\|_1\\)<\/span>. Consequently, an operator <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(x\\)\">\\(x\\)<\/span> is of trace class if and only if <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\sum_i\\langle|x|\\varepsilon_i,\\varepsilon_i\\rangle&lt;\\infty\\)\">\\(\\sum_i\\langle|x|\\varepsilon_i,\\varepsilon_i\\rangle&lt;\\infty\\)<\/span> for one orthonormal basis; and then <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\operatorname{Tr}(x)=\\sum_i\\langle x\\varepsilon_i,\\varepsilon_i\\rangle\\)\">\\(\\operatorname{Tr}(x)=\\sum_i\\langle x\\varepsilon_i,\\varepsilon_i\\rangle\\)<\/span> for every orthonormal basis, with absolute convergence.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Proof.<\/strong> <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\sum_i\\langle h\\varepsilon_i,\\varepsilon_i\\rangle=\\sum_i\\|h^{1/2}\\varepsilon_i\\|^2=\\|h^{1/2}\\|_2^2\\)\">\\(\\sum_i\\langle h\\varepsilon_i,\\varepsilon_i\\rangle=\\sum_i\\|h^{1/2}\\varepsilon_i\\|^2=\\|h^{1/2}\\|_2^2\\)<\/span>, which does not depend on the basis (Lemma 3.1). If it is finite, then <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(h^{1/2}\\in\\mathcal L^2(H)\\subseteq K(H)\\)\">\\(h^{1/2}\\in\\mathcal L^2(H)\\subseteq K(H)\\)<\/span>, so <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(h=(h^{1/2})^2\\)\">\\(h=(h^{1/2})^2\\)<\/span> is compact. Write <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(h=\\sum_ns_n\\theta_{e_n,e_n}\\)\">\\(h=\\sum_ns_n\\theta_{e_n,e_n}\\)<\/span> as in Theorem 2.3(e) and complete <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\((e_n)\\)\">\\((e_n)\\)<\/span> to an orthonormal basis: <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\sum_ns_n=\\sum_n\\langle he_n,e_n\\rangle\\le\\|h^{1/2}\\|_2^2&lt;\\infty\\)\">\\(\\sum_ns_n=\\sum_n\\langle he_n,e_n\\rangle\\le\\|h^{1/2}\\|_2^2&lt;\\infty\\)<\/span>. By Theorem 4.3(b), <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(h\\in\\mathcal L^1(H)\\)\">\\(h\\in\\mathcal L^1(H)\\)<\/span>, and by Theorem 4.6 the sum over any basis is <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\operatorname{Tr}(h)=\\operatorname{Tr}(|h|)=\\|h\\|_1\\)\">\\(\\operatorname{Tr}(h)=\\operatorname{Tr}(|h|)=\\|h\\|_1\\)<\/span>. If <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(h\\in\\mathcal L^1(H)\\)\">\\(h\\in\\mathcal L^1(H)\\)<\/span>, the sum is <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\operatorname{Tr}(h)&lt;\\infty\\)\">\\(\\operatorname{Tr}(h)&lt;\\infty\\)<\/span>. The last statement follows from Corollary 4.4(b) and Theorem 4.6. <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\square\\)\">\\(\\square\\)<\/span><\/p>\n<p><strong>Proposition 4.8<\/strong> (positive parts). Every <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(x\\in\\mathcal L^1(H)\\)\">\\(x\\in\\mathcal L^1(H)\\)<\/span> can be written <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(x=h_1-h_2+i(h_3-h_4)\\)\">\\(x=h_1-h_2+i(h_3-h_4)\\)<\/span> with positive trace-class operators <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(h_1,\\dots,h_4\\)\">\\(h_1,\\dots,h_4\\)<\/span>. A self-adjoint <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(x\\in\\mathcal L^1(H)\\)\">\\(x\\in\\mathcal L^1(H)\\)<\/span> can be written <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(x=h_1-h_2\\)\">\\(x=h_1-h_2\\)<\/span> with <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(h_1,h_2\\ge0\\)\">\\(h_1,h_2\\ge0\\)<\/span> of trace class and <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\|x\\|_1=\\operatorname{Tr}(h_1)+\\operatorname{Tr}(h_2)\\)\">\\(\\|x\\|_1=\\operatorname{Tr}(h_1)+\\operatorname{Tr}(h_2)\\)<\/span>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Proof.<\/strong> The operators <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\operatorname{Re}x=\\frac12(x+x^*)\\)\">\\(\\operatorname{Re}x=\\frac12(x+x^*)\\)<\/span> and <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\operatorname{Im}x=\\frac1{2i}(x-x^*)\\)\">\\(\\operatorname{Im}x=\\frac1{2i}(x-x^*)\\)<\/span> are self-adjoint, lie in <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\mathcal L^1(H)\\)\">\\(\\mathcal L^1(H)\\)<\/span> by Corollary 4.4(b), and <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(x=\\operatorname{Re}x+i\\operatorname{Im}x\\)\">\\(x=\\operatorname{Re}x+i\\operatorname{Im}x\\)<\/span>. For self-adjoint <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(x\\in\\mathcal L^1(H)\\)\">\\(x\\in\\mathcal L^1(H)\\)<\/span>, <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(x\\ne0\\)\">\\(x\\ne0\\)<\/span>, Theorem 2.3(e) gives <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(x=\\sum_n\\lambda_n\\theta_{e_n,e_n}\\)\">\\(x=\\sum_n\\lambda_n\\theta_{e_n,e_n}\\)<\/span> with real <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\lambda_n\\)\">\\(\\lambda_n\\)<\/span> and <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\sum|\\lambda_n|=\\|x\\|_1\\)\">\\(\\sum|\\lambda_n|=\\|x\\|_1\\)<\/span>. Put <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(h_1=\\sum_{\\lambda_n&gt;0}\\lambda_n\\theta_{e_n,e_n}\\)\">\\(h_1=\\sum_{\\lambda_n&gt;0}\\lambda_n\\theta_{e_n,e_n}\\)<\/span> and <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(h_2=\\sum_{\\lambda_n&lt;0}|\\lambda_n|\\theta_{e_n,e_n}\\)\">\\(h_2=\\sum_{\\lambda_n&lt;0}|\\lambda_n|\\theta_{e_n,e_n}\\)<\/span>. <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\square\\)\">\\(\\square\\)<\/span><\/p>\n<p><strong>Proposition 4.9<\/strong> (products of Hilbert–Schmidt operators).<\/p>\n<p>(a) An operator <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(x\\)\">\\(x\\)<\/span> is Hilbert–Schmidt if and only if <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(x^*x\\)\">\\(x^*x\\)<\/span> is of trace class, and then <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\operatorname{Tr}(x^*x)=\\|x\\|_2^2\\)\">\\(\\operatorname{Tr}(x^*x)=\\|x\\|_2^2\\)<\/span>.<\/p>\n<p>(b) If <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(x,y\\in\\mathcal L^2(H)\\)\">\\(x,y\\in\\mathcal L^2(H)\\)<\/span>, then <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(xy\\in\\mathcal L^1(H)\\)\">\\(xy\\in\\mathcal L^1(H)\\)<\/span>, <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\|xy\\|_1\\le\\|x\\|_2\\,\\|y\\|_2\\)\">\\(\\|xy\\|_1\\le\\|x\\|_2\\,\\|y\\|_2\\)<\/span>, and <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\operatorname{Tr}(y^*x)=\\langle x,y\\rangle_2\\)\">\\(\\operatorname{Tr}(y^*x)=\\langle x,y\\rangle_2\\)<\/span>.<\/p>\n<p>(c) Every <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(t\\in\\mathcal L^1(H)\\)\">\\(t\\in\\mathcal L^1(H)\\)<\/span> is a product <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(t=xy\\)\">\\(t=xy\\)<\/span> of two Hilbert–Schmidt operators, with <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\|x\\|_2^2=\\|y\\|_2^2=\\|t\\|_1\\)\">\\(\\|x\\|_2^2=\\|y\\|_2^2=\\|t\\|_1\\)<\/span>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Proof.<\/strong> (a) <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\sum_i\\langle x^*x\\varepsilon_i,\\varepsilon_i\\rangle=\\sum_i\\|x\\varepsilon_i\\|^2\\)\">\\(\\sum_i\\langle x^*x\\varepsilon_i,\\varepsilon_i\\rangle=\\sum_i\\|x\\varepsilon_i\\|^2\\)<\/span>; apply Proposition 4.7 to <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(h=x^*x\\)\">\\(h=x^*x\\)<\/span>. (b) For finite orthonormal families, extend each to an orthonormal basis and use Cauchy–Schwarz:\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n\\begin{gathered}\n\\sum_j|\\langle xy\\xi_j,\\eta_j\\rangle|\\\\\n=\\sum_j|\\langle y\\xi_j,x^*\\eta_j\\rangle|\\\\\n\\le\\Big(\\sum_j\\|y\\xi_j\\|^2\\Big)^{1/2}\\Big(\\sum_j\\|x^*\\eta_j\\|^2\\Big)^{1/2}\\\\\n\\le\\|y\\|_2\\,\\|x^*\\|_2\\\\\n=\\|x\\|_2\\,\\|y\\|_2 .\n\\end{gathered}\n\\]\">\\[\n\\begin{gathered}\n\\sum_j|\\langle xy\\xi_j,\\eta_j\\rangle|\\\\\n=\\sum_j|\\langle y\\xi_j,x^*\\eta_j\\rangle|\\\\\n\\le\\Big(\\sum_j\\|y\\xi_j\\|^2\\Big)^{1/2}\\Big(\\sum_j\\|x^*\\eta_j\\|^2\\Big)^{1/2}\\\\\n\\le\\|y\\|_2\\,\\|x^*\\|_2\\\\\n=\\|x\\|_2\\,\\|y\\|_2 .\n\\end{gathered}\n\\]<\/span>\nThen <span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n\\begin{gathered}\n\\operatorname{Tr}(y^*x)\\\\\n=\\sum_i\\langle y^*x\\varepsilon_i,\\varepsilon_i\\rangle\\\\\n=\\sum_i\\langle x\\varepsilon_i,y\\varepsilon_i\\rangle\\\\\n=\\langle x,y\\rangle_2.\n\\end{gathered}\n\\]\">\\[\n\\begin{gathered}\n\\operatorname{Tr}(y^*x)\\\\\n=\\sum_i\\langle y^*x\\varepsilon_i,\\varepsilon_i\\rangle\\\\\n=\\sum_i\\langle x\\varepsilon_i,y\\varepsilon_i\\rangle\\\\\n=\\langle x,y\\rangle_2.\n\\end{gathered}\n\\]<\/span> (c) With (2.1) for <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(t\\ne0\\)\">\\(t\\ne0\\)<\/span>, put <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(x=\\sum_ns_n^{1/2}\\theta_{f_n,e_n}\\)\">\\(x=\\sum_ns_n^{1/2}\\theta_{f_n,e_n}\\)<\/span> and <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(y=\\sum_ns_n^{1/2}\\theta_{e_n,e_n}\\)\">\\(y=\\sum_ns_n^{1/2}\\theta_{e_n,e_n}\\)<\/span>. Both are Hilbert–Schmidt with <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\|x\\|_2^2=\\|y\\|_2^2=\\sum s_n\\)\">\\(\\|x\\|_2^2=\\|y\\|_2^2=\\sum s_n\\)<\/span> (Theorem 3.3(b)), and <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(xy=\\sum_ns_n\\theta_{f_n,e_n}=t\\)\">\\(xy=\\sum_ns_n\\theta_{f_n,e_n}=t\\)<\/span> because <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\theta_{f_n,e_n}\\theta_{e_m,e_m}=\\delta_{nm}\\theta_{f_n,e_n}\\)\">\\(\\theta_{f_n,e_n}\\theta_{e_m,e_m}=\\delta_{nm}\\theta_{f_n,e_n}\\)<\/span>. <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\square\\)\">\\(\\square\\)<\/span><\/p>\n","source":"docs/courses/foundations-of-von-neumann-algebras/src/compact-and-trace-class-operators-the-predual-of-b-h-and-the-operator-topologies.md","start_offset":54014,"end_offset":68667,"role":"registered_statement_and_proof_passage"}});
